(window.COURSES=window.COURSES||{})["ds"]=JSON.parse("{\"id\":\"ds\",\"name\":\"Data Structures\",\"short\":\"DS\",\"code\":\"CS201\",\"by\":\"Prof. Lamiaa Hassaan, Dr. Ahmed Ibrahim\",\"lectures\":[{\"n\":1,\"title\":\"Introduction, Arrays and Structs\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Data, data types and data structures\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Data</b>: a collection of facts from which a conclusion may be drawn. <i>Data: temperature 35°C; conclusion: it is hot.</i> Types of data: <b>textual</b> (your name), <b>numeric</b> (your ID), <b>audio</b> (your voice), <b>video</b> (your voice and picture).\",\"Programs <b>read</b> data, <b>store</b> it temporarily, <b>process</b> it and finally <b>output</b> results.\",\"<b>Data types</b> (also called <b>atomic</b> data types) have <b>no component parts</b>, e.g. <code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>float</code>. A data type is <b>(i) a domain of allowed values</b> and <b>(ii) a set of operations</b> on these values. The compiler signals an error if a wrong operation is performed, e.g. <code>char x,y,z; z=x*y;</code> is not allowed.\",\"Examples: <b>boolean</b>: domain 0,1, operations and, or, =. <b>char</b>: domain ASCII, operations =, &lt;&gt;, &lt;. <b>integer</b>: domain -maxint to +maxint, operations +, -, =, ==, &lt;&gt;, &lt;.\",\"<b>Data structure</b>: a particular way of <b>storing and organizing data</b> in a computer so that it can be used <b>efficiently and effectively</b>. It is the <b>logical or mathematical model</b> of a particular organization of data, a group of data elements grouped together under one name (e.g. an array). Data structures make code cleaner and easier to understand.\",\"<b>The need for data structures</b>: Goal: to organize data. Criteria: to facilitate efficient <b>storage</b>, <b>retrieval</b> and <b>manipulation</b> of data. Design issue: select and design appropriate data types (the main motivation to learn data structures).\"]},{\"h\":\"Classification of data structures (lecture + Lab 1)\",\"table\":[[\"Class\",\"Meaning\",\"Examples\"],[\"Primitive\",\"Basic built-in types\",\"int, float, char (and pointers)\"],[\"Non-primitive, linear, static\",\"Elements in a sequence, size fixed at design time\",\"Arrays\"],[\"Non-primitive, linear, dynamic\",\"Elements in a sequence, size can change\",\"Stack, Queue, Linked list\"],[\"Non-primitive, non-linear\",\"Elements are not in a single sequence (hierarchy / network)\",\"Trees, Graphs\"]]},{\"h\":\"Operations and Abstract Data Types (ADT)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Navigating</b> (traversing): accessing each data element <b>exactly once</b> so that certain items may be processed.\",\"<b>Searching</b>: finding the location of the data element (<b>key</b>) in the structure. <b>Insertion</b>: adding a new element. <b>Deletion</b>: removing an element.\",\"<b>Sorting</b>: arranging elements in a logical order (ascending/descending). <b>Merging</b>: combining elements from two or more data structures into one.\",\"<b>ADT</b>: an <b>abstraction of a data structure</b> which provides <b>only the interface</b> to which a data structure must adhere. The interface gives no details about how it is implemented or in which programming language (it lets us think abstractly about the data).\",\"ADTs are <b>purely theoretical entities</b>: used to simplify the description of abstract algorithms and to classify and evaluate data structures. An ADT is a collection of data and a set of operations on this data.\",\"ADT → implementation examples: <b>List</b> → dynamic array, linked list. <b>Queue</b> → linked-list based queue, array based queue. <b>Map</b> → tree map, hash map / hash table. Analogy: <b>Vehicle</b> (ADT) → golf car, bicycle, smart car (implementations).\"]},{\"h\":\"Arrays (lecture + Lab 1)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"size\":6,\"init\":[5,12,9,20,7],\"ops\":[\"search 20\"]},\"pts\":[\"An <b>array</b> is a data structure used to process multiple elements with the <b>same data type</b> when the number of elements is known; a group of elements of the same type stored in <b>consecutive memory locations</b>.\",\"An element is located with a mathematical formula: <b>location(i) = i - 1</b>, i.e. the i-th element of the list is in position i-1 of the array (index is <b>zero-based</b>: 0 to size-1).\",\"Syntax: <code>dataType arrayName[arraySize];</code> The size must be an <b>integer constant greater than zero</b> and must be pre-specified. The subscript (index) must be an integer.\",\"Ways to put data in: <code>int arr[5]={10,20,30,40,50};</code> (all), <code>char arr[5]={'a','b','c'};</code> (first 3 only), <code>arr[0]=10;</code> (specific index), <code>cin&gt;&gt;arr[2];</code> (at run time).\",\"C++ passes <b>whole arrays by reference</b> to functions, but <b>individual elements are passed by value</b> like simple variables.\",\"Size in memory = number of elements × size of type, e.g. <code>int arr[15]</code> with 4-byte int = <b>60 bytes</b>.\",\"Advantage: easy to store elements of the same type, direct access by index. <b>Disadvantage: fixed size</b> (declared at design time, cannot grow).\"]},{\"h\":\"Array insert and delete (professor's code)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"size\":7,\"init\":[12,25,31,47,58],\"ops\":[\"insert 2 40\",\"delete 4\"]},\"code\":\"// Delete the k'th element if it exists.\\n{\\n   if (Find(k, x)) {\\n      for (int i = k; i < length; i++)\\n         element[i-1] = element[i];   // shift left\\n      length--;\\n   }\\n}\\n\\n// Insert x after the k'th element.\\n{\\n   if (k < 0 || k > length) throw OutOfBounds();\\n   if (length == MaxSize) throw NoMem();\\n   for (int i = length-1; i >= k; i--)\\n      element[i+1] = element[i];      // shift right\\n   element[k] = x;\\n   length++;\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Array operations: sum, average, sort\",\"code\":\"// Sum  (output is 12206)\\nint billy[] = {16, 2, 77, 40, 12071};\\nint n, result = 0;\\nint main() {\\n   for (n = 0; n < 5; n++) result += billy[n];\\n   cout << result;\\n   return 0;\\n}\\n\\n// Average (prints average = 10)\\nconst int size = 3;\\nint nums[size] = {5, 10, 15};\\ndouble average = 0;\\nfor (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) average += nums[i];\\ncout << \\\"average = \\\" << average / size;\\n\\n// Sort (descending, because of billy[n] < billy[m])\\nint billy[5] = {16, 2, 77, 40, 12};\\nfor (n = 0; n < 4; n++)\\n   for (m = n+1; m < 5; m++)\\n      if (billy[n] < billy[m])\\n      { temp = billy[n]; billy[n] = billy[m]; billy[m] = temp; }\\nfor (m = 0; m < 5; m++) cout << billy[m] << \\\"\\\\n\\\";   // 77 40 16 12 2\"},{\"h\":\"2D arrays and matrices\",\"pts\":[\"Tabular data (e.g. a <b>matrix</b>) is represented as a 2-dimensional array: <code>int a[3][4];</code> has 3 rows (0..2) and 4 columns (0..3), elements a[0][0] .. a[2][3].\",\"Matrices are normally indexed from <b>1</b>, and support add, multiply and transpose, which are <b>not</b> supported directly by C++'s 2D array.\",\"<b>Locating x[i][j]</b> (row-major): if x has r rows and c columns, each row has c elements, there are i rows before row i, so <b>i·c</b> elements before x[i][0]; x[i][j] maps to position <b>i·c + j</b> of the 1D array.\",\"<b>Transpose</b>: an m×n matrix becomes n×m with M<sup>T</sup>(j,i) = M(i,j).\",\"<b>Addition</b>: only for matrices of the <b>same dimensions</b>: C(i,j) = A(i,j) + B(i,j).\",\"<b>Multiplication</b>: only defined when <b>columns of A = rows of B</b>. A (m×n) × B (n×q) = C (m×q) with C(i,j) = Σ<sub>k=1..n</sub> A(i,k)·B(k,j).\"]},{\"h\":\"Matrix multiplication (professor's code, core)\",\"code\":\"int a[10][10], b[10][10], mult[10][10], r1, c1, r2, c2, i, j, k;\\ndo {\\n   cout << \\\"column of first matrix must equal rows of second.\\\";\\n   cin >> r1 >> c1;          // rows, columns of first\\n   cin >> r2 >> c2;          // rows, columns of second\\n} while (c1 != r2);\\n// ... read a and b ...\\nfor (i = 0; i < r1; ++i)\\n   for (j = 0; j < c2; ++j) {\\n      mult[i][j] = 0;\\n      for (k = 0; k < c1; ++k)\\n         mult[i][j] += a[i][k] * b[k][j];\\n   }\\n\\n// Matrix summation (global result c)\\nvoid Sum(int a[][cols], int b[][cols])\\n{ for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++)\\n     for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++)\\n        c[i][j] = a[i][j] + b[i][j]; }\"},{\"h\":\"Structs\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>struct</b> is a data structure containing a group of data elements grouped together under one name. These elements, called <b>members</b>, can have <b>different types and different lengths</b>. Structs are also known as <b>records</b> and members as <b>fields</b>.\",\"Unlike arrays (same type), structs are <b>user-defined</b> types that store items of <b>non-similar</b> data types.\",\"Two ways to create objects: (1) after the closing brace: <code>struct product { int weight; float price; } apple, banana, melon;</code> (2) separately: <code>product apple; product banana, melon;</code>. Members are accessed with the dot: <code>apple.weight</code>, <code>apple.price</code>.\",\"<b>Array of structs</b>: <code>struct StudentRec { string name; int mark; } Student[2];</code> then <code>Student[i].name</code>, <code>Student[i].mark</code>.\",\"<b>Passing a struct to a function</b>: <code>void printInfo(Employee emp) { cout&lt;&lt;emp.id; cout&lt;&lt;emp.salary; }</code> called as <code>Employee e1 = {1, 1000}; printInfo(e1);</code> (passed by value, a copy).\",\"This is a favourite final-exam question: \\\"Using an <b>array of struct</b> write a program to take a table from the user and calculate totals/averages\\\" (see the past papers).\"]},{\"h\":\"Array of struct example (exam pattern)\",\"code\":\"#include <iostream>\\n#include <string>\\nusing namespace std;\\nstruct Employee {\\n    string name;\\n    float salary, bonus, deduction, total;\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    const int N = 4;\\n    Employee e[N];\\n    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)\\n    {\\n        cout << \\\"Enter name, salary, bonus, deduction: \\\";\\n        cin >> e[i].name >> e[i].salary >> e[i].bonus >> e[i].deduction;\\n        e[i].total = e[i].salary + e[i].bonus - e[i].deduction;\\n    }\\n    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)\\n        cout << e[i].name << \\\" total salary = \\\" << e[i].total << endl;\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n// Note: the lectures use Visual Studio style (#include \\\"stdafx.h\\\", void main()).\\n// With g++ use int main() and drop stdafx.h.\"}],\"cards\":[[\"Data (definition)\",\"A collection of facts from which a conclusion may be drawn.\"],[\"Data type = ?\",\"(i) a domain of allowed values and (ii) a set of operations on these values.\"],[\"Atomic data types\",\"Data types with no component parts, e.g. int, char, float.\"],[\"Data structure (definition)\",\"A particular way of storing and organizing data in a computer so that it can be used efficiently and effectively.\"],[\"Criteria of data structures\",\"Facilitate efficient storage, retrieval and manipulation of data.\"],[\"Linear data structures\",\"Array (static); stack, queue, linked list (dynamic).\"],[\"Non-linear data structures\",\"Trees and graphs.\"],[\"Navigating (traversing)\",\"Accessing each data element exactly once so certain items may be processed.\"],[\"Merging\",\"Combining data elements from two or more data structures into one.\"],[\"ADT\",\"An abstraction of a data structure that provides only the interface, with no implementation details or language.\"],[\"location(i) in an array\",\"i - 1: the i-th element is at index i-1 (zero-based).\"],[\"Position of x[i][j] in 1D (c columns)\",\"i·c + j (row-major).\"],[\"Condition to multiply A×B\",\"Number of columns of A = number of rows of B; m×n times n×q gives m×q.\"],[\"Condition to add A+B\",\"Same dimensions.\"],[\"Size of int arr[15] (4-byte int)\",\"60 bytes.\"],[\"Struct\",\"A group of data elements (members) of possibly different types grouped under one name. Also called a record; members are fields.\"],[\"How are arrays passed to functions?\",\"Whole arrays by reference; individual elements by value.\"],[\"Main disadvantage of arrays\",\"Fixed size.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Define data structure and explain why we need data structures.\",\"A data structure is a particular way of storing and organizing data in a computer so that it can be used efficiently and effectively; the logical or mathematical model of a particular organization of data. The goal is to organize data. The criteria are to facilitate efficient storage, retrieval and manipulation of data. The design issue is to select and design appropriate data types.\"],[\"List and explain the data structure operations.\",\"Navigating: access each element exactly once. Searching: find the location of a key. Insertion: add a new element. Deletion: remove an element. Sorting: arrange elements in ascending/descending order. Merging: combine elements of two or more structures into one.\"],[\"What is an ADT? Give examples of ADTs and their implementations.\",\"An abstract data type is an abstraction of a data structure that provides only the interface the structure must adhere to, without saying how or in which language it is implemented. It is a collection of data plus a set of operations. Examples: List → dynamic array or linked list; Queue → array-based or linked-list-based queue; Map → tree map or hash map.\"],[\"Classify data structures with examples.\",\"Primitive: int, float, char (and pointers). Non-primitive: linear (static: arrays; dynamic: stack, queue, linked list) and non-linear (trees, graphs).\"],[\"Explain how element x[i][j] of a 2D array with r rows and c columns is located in memory.\",\"Row-major order: each row holds c elements and there are i complete rows before row i, so i·c elements come before x[i][0]. Hence x[i][j] is at position i·c + j of the equivalent 1D array. Example: int a[3][4], a[2][1] → 2·4+1 = 9.\"],[\"Compare arrays and structs.\",\"An array groups elements of the same data type accessed by an integer index; a struct groups members of different types and lengths accessed by name with the dot operator. Both group data under one name; an array of structs combines them (e.g. an array of employee records).\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"Assuming int is 4 bytes, what is the size of <code>int arr[15];</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"15\",\"15 is the number of elements, not the number of bytes.\"],[\"19\",\"19 comes from adding the element count and the element size (15 + 4). Array size in bytes is count × size of one element.\"],[\"11\",\"This subtracts 4 from 15, which has no meaning.\"],[\"60\",\"Correct. An array occupies number of elements × size of each element: 15 × 4 bytes = 60 bytes (L1, Array-based representation).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"In general, the index of the first element in an array is:\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"Matrices are indexed from 1 in math, but C++ arrays are zero-based.\"],[\"0\",\"Correct. C++ arrays are zero-based: indices run from 0 to size-1.\"],[\"2\",\"No C++ array starts at index 2. The ith element is at position i − 1, so the first element is at index 0 (L1, location(i) = i − 1).\"],[\"-1\",\"-1 is used as the empty value of TOP/front, not an array index.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following data structures is a linear type?\",\"o\":[[\"Graph\",\"A graph is non-linear: its elements form a network, not a single sequence. The lecture's linear examples are array, stack, queue and linked list.\"],[\"Binary tree\",\"A binary tree is still a tree: its nodes form a hierarchy (parent and children), so it is non-linear, not a single sequence.\"],[\"Tree\",\"A tree arranges elements in a hierarchy, not one after another, so the lecture classifies it as non-linear together with graphs.\"],[\"Linked list\",\"Correct. A linked list stores elements in a sequence, so it is linear.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following data structures is a non-linear type?\",\"o\":[[\"Array\",\"An array is linear (and static): its elements sit in one sequence with a size fixed at design time.\"],[\"Stack\",\"A stack is a linear dynamic structure: its elements form one sequence, accessed only at the top.\"],[\"Tree\",\"Correct. In a tree the elements are not in a single sequence but in a hierarchy, so it is non-linear, like a graph; arrays, stacks and queues are linear (L1, Types of data structures).\"],[\"Queue\",\"A queue is a linear dynamic structure: elements form one sequence, added at the rear and removed at the front.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following data structures is a non-linear type?\",\"o\":[[\"Linked list\",\"A linked list is linear: each node points to the next one, so the elements form a single sequence.\"],[\"Stack\",\"A stack is linear: its elements form one sequence, with insertion and deletion at the top only.\"],[\"Tree\",\"Correct. A tree is hierarchical, so it is non-linear.\"],[\"Circular queue\",\"A circular queue is still a linear structure (only the indexing wraps around).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"What are the advantages of arrays?\",\"o\":[[\"Easier to store elements of the same data type\",\"Correct. Arrays hold many elements of one type under one name with index access.\"],[\"Elements in an array cannot be sorted\",\"False: arrays can be sorted (the lecture has a sort example), and this would not be an advantage anyway.\"],[\"Index of first element of an array is 1\",\"False: C++ arrays are zero-based, so the first element is at index 0 (location(i) = i − 1). Matrices in math start at 1, arrays do not.\"],[\"Objects of mixed data types can be stored\",\"False: that describes a struct, not an array.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"When a search starts at the beginning of a list and checks every element in that list, it is:\",\"o\":[[\"Linear search\",\"Correct. Linear search checks the elements one by one from the start (Lab 1/Lab 2).\"],[\"Binary search\",\"Binary search repeatedly halves a sorted list; it does not check every element.\"],[\"Hash search\",\"Hash search computes the position directly with a hash function.\"],[\"Binary tree search\",\"BST search moves left or right from the root, not through every element.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"The array is a group of data elements grouped together under one name with different types.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Elements of an array all have the same type; different types describes a struct.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. That is the definition of a struct. An array holds elements of the same data type.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"The disadvantage of an array is ______.\",\"o\":[[\"Fixed size\",\"Correct (official model answer). The size must be declared at design time and cannot change.\"],[\"Slow access by index\",\"Index access is an array's strength: any element is reached directly by its index (computed by a formula), with no walking through other elements.\"],[\"It needs extra memory for pointers\",\"Extra memory for pointers is the cost of a linked list, where every node stores a next pointer. An array stores only the data.\"],[\"It can only hold characters\",\"An array can hold elements of any one data type (int, float, char, structs…); the only rule is that all elements share the same type.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"Which operation means \\\"accessing each data element exactly once so that certain items may be processed\\\"?\",\"o\":[[\"Searching\",\"Searching means finding the location of one data element (key) in the structure, not visiting every element once.\"],[\"Navigating (traversing)\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's definition of navigating.\"],[\"Merging\",\"Merging combines elements from two or more structures.\"],[\"Sorting\",\"Sorting means arranging the elements in a logical order (ascending or descending), not visiting each one once.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"For <code>int a[3][4]</code> stored row by row, element a[2][1] is at which position of the equivalent 1D array?\",\"o\":[[\"7\",\"This uses i·r + j = 2·3 + 1; the formula uses the number of columns c.\"],[\"9\",\"Correct. Row-major storage puts i·c elements before row i, so x[i][j] is at i·c + j. With c = 4 columns: 2·4 + 1 = 9 (L1, Locating element x[i][j]).\"],[\"6\",\"6 swaps i and j: j·c + i = 1·4 + 2. The row index multiplies the column count: 2·4 + 1 = 9.\"],[\"11\",\"11 is the position of the last element a[2][3] (2·4 + 3). a[2][1] is two places earlier: 2·4 + 1 = 9.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Matrix A is 2×3 and B is 3×4. What is A×B?\",\"o\":[[\"Not defined\",\"It is defined: columns of A (3) equal rows of B (3).\"],[\"3×3\",\"3×3 uses the inner dimension (3), which only has to match. The product takes the rows of A and the columns of B: 2×4.\"],[\"2×4\",\"Correct. A×B is defined because columns of A (3) = rows of B (3), and an m×n times n×q product is m×q, so 2×3 · 3×4 = 2×4 (L1, Matrix multiplication).\"],[\"4×2\",\"4×2 reverses the order: it is the size of the transpose of A×B. A×B takes rows of A (2) and columns of B (4), so it is 2×4.\"]],\"a\":2}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"A data type is defined in the lecture as:\",\"o\":[[\"A group of elements of the same type stored together\",\"A group of same-type elements stored together under one name is an array, which is a data structure, not a data type.\"],[\"A domain of allowed values and a set of operations on these values\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's two-part definition.\"],[\"The logical model of a particular organization of data\",\"The logical or mathematical model of a particular organization of data is the lecture's definition of a data structure.\"],[\"An interface without implementation details\",\"Providing only an interface, with no implementation details, is the definition of an abstract data type (ADT).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Why does <code>char x, y, z; z = x * y;</code> produce a compiler error in the lecture's example?\",\"o\":[[\"Because x and y are not initialized\",\"Uninitialized values give a warning at most, not the point of the example.\"],[\"Because char variables cannot be declared together\",\"Declaring several variables on one line is legal.\"],[\"Because multiplication is not in the set of operations allowed for the char type\",\"Correct. A data type includes its allowed operations; the compiler signals an error for a wrong operation.\"],[\"Because z must be declared as int\",\"The lecture's point is the operation, not the result type.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"What does the lecture's sort code print for <code>billy[5] = {16, 2, 77, 40, 12}</code> (it swaps when <code>billy[n] &lt; billy[m]</code>)?\",\"o\":[[\"2 12 16 40 77\",\"That is ascending order; the condition &lt; swaps the larger value to the front.\"],[\"16 2 77 40 12\",\"That is the original array. The nested loops swap billy[n] and billy[m] whenever billy[n] is smaller, so the array ends up sorted in descending order.\"],[\"77 16 40 12 2\",\"Not fully sorted; the nested loops finish the sort.\"],[\"77 40 16 12 2\",\"Correct. Swapping whenever billy[n] &lt; billy[m] produces descending order.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Array element = {5, 2, 4, 8, 1}, length = 5. After the lecture's Insert with k = 2 and x = 9, the array is:\",\"o\":[[\"5 2 9 4 8 1\",\"Correct. Elements from index 2 shift right and 9 is placed at element[2] (after the 2nd element). Checked with g++.\"],[\"5 9 2 4 8 1\",\"5 9 2 4 8 1 is inserting after the 1st element (k = 1). With k = 2, elements from index 2 shift right and 9 goes into element[2].\"],[\"9 5 2 4 8 1\",\"9 at the front is insertion with k = 0. With k = 2 the loop shifts only element[2..4] right and sets element[2] = 9.\"],[\"5 2 4 9 8 1\",\"That puts 9 after the 3rd element (k = 3). With k = 2, the code writes element[2] = 9, so 9 follows the 2 and comes before the 4.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Array {5, 2, 4, 8, 1}. The lecture's Delete with k = 2 (delete the 2nd element) gives:\",\"o\":[[\"5 2 8 1\",\"Removing the 4 is deleting the 3rd element (k = 3). With k = 2 the loop copies element[2] into element[1], overwriting the 2.\"],[\"5 4 8 1\",\"Correct. element[i-1] = element[i] for i = 2..4 shifts 4, 8, 1 left over the 2; length becomes 4.\"],[\"2 4 8 1\",\"Removing the 5 is deleting the 1st element (k = 1). With k = 2 the loop starts at i = 2 and overwrites element[1], the 2.\"],[\"5 2 4 8\",\"Removing the 1 is deleting the last element (k = 5), which only decrements length. k = 2 removes the 2 by shifting 4, 8, 1 left.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Array x has 4 rows and 5 columns, stored row by row. x[3][2] maps to which position of the 1D array?\",\"o\":[[\"14\",\"14 = 3·4 + 2 multiplies by the number of rows (4). Each row holds c = 5 elements, so the formula is i·c + j.\"],[\"11\",\"11 = 3 + 2·4 swaps the roles of i and j and uses the rows. Row-major position is i·c + j = 3·5 + 2 = 17.\"],[\"17\",\"Correct. In row-major order there are i·c elements before row i, so x[i][j] is at i·c + j = 3·5 + 2 = 17 (L1, Locating element x[i][j]).\"],[\"20\",\"20 = 4 × 5 is the total number of elements; positions only run from 0 to 19, so 20 is not a valid position.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which pair correctly matches an ADT with one of its implementations?\",\"o\":[[\"Map → Hash table\",\"Correct. The lecture lists tree map and hash map / hash table as Map implementations.\"],[\"Queue → Binary search tree\",\"Queues are implemented with arrays or linked lists.\"],[\"List → Hash table\",\"Lists are implemented as dynamic arrays or linked lists.\"],[\"Vehicle → Queue\",\"In the analogy, vehicle implementations are golf car, bicycle, smart car.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which statement about passing arrays to functions in C++ is correct?\",\"o\":[[\"Whole arrays and individual elements are both passed by value\",\"Only individual elements are passed by value. A whole array is passed by reference, so a function can change the caller's array.\"],[\"Whole arrays are passed by reference, individual elements by value\",\"Correct. C++ passes a whole array by reference (the function works on the caller's array), while a single element like a[2] is passed by value like a simple variable (L1 notes; Lab 1).\"],[\"Whole arrays are passed by value, individual elements by reference\",\"This is the reverse. Whole arrays are passed by reference and individual elements by value.\"],[\"Arrays cannot be passed to functions\",\"Arrays can be passed to functions: Lab 2 passes an array to LinearSearch, and the matrix Sum function takes int a[][cols].\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Combining data elements from two or more data structures into one is called:\",\"o\":[[\"Sorting\",\"Sorting arranges the elements of one structure in a logical order (ascending or descending); it does not combine structures.\"],[\"Navigating\",\"Navigating means accessing each element of a structure exactly once so it can be processed, not combining structures.\"],[\"Insertion\",\"Insertion adds one new data element to a structure; combining whole structures into one is merging.\"],[\"Merging\",\"Correct. The lecture defines merging as combining data elements from two or more data structures into one (L1, Data structure operations).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which declaration creates three objects of a struct in one statement, the \\\"first way\\\" from the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"struct product { int weight; float price; } apple, banana, melon;\",\"Correct. The object names follow the closing brace.\"],[\"product { int weight; float price; } apple;\",\"The struct keyword is missing: a definition must start with struct, e.g. struct product { … } apple;.\"],[\"struct product apple, banana, melon { int weight; };\",\"The member list in braces must come right after the struct name, and the object names come after the closing brace: struct product { … } apple, banana, melon;.\"],[\"product[3] = {apple, banana, melon};\",\"This is array initializer syntax with no type or members, so it defines no struct. Objects are declared after the closing brace or as product apple;.\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":2,\"title\":\"Stack using Arrays\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"What is a stack?\",\"pts\":[\"A stack is a <b>non-primitive linear</b> data structure: an <b>ordered list</b> in which insertion of new items and deletion of existing items are done from <b>only one end</b>, called the <b>top</b> (TOP).\",\"Because all insertions and deletions happen at the top, the <b>last added element is the first removed</b>: <b>LIFO</b> (Last-In-First-Out), also written <b>FILO</b> (First-In-Last-Out).\",\"Technique: the stack <b>base remains fixed</b>; adding an element <b>increments</b> top, removing the top element <b>decrements</b> top.\",\"Real-life examples: <b>books and clothes</b> piled on each other; <b>mobile call logs</b>; <b>browsers</b> (back button returns to the previous site); <b>text editors</b> (undo/redo).\",\"Applications (Lab 3): <b>backtracking</b> (maze path), <b>function calls</b> (keeping information about active functions), <b>string reversal</b> (push each character, then pop).\"]},{\"h\":\"Operations and terminology\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"stack\",\"size\":4,\"ops\":[\"push 10\",\"push 20\",\"push 30\",\"pop\",\"push 40\",\"push 50\",\"push 60\",\"pop\"]},\"pts\":[\"<b>PUSH</b>: adding a new element at the top. After every push top is <b>incremented by one</b>. If the array is full it is the <b>STACK-FULL</b> condition, and pushing then gives <b>STACK OVERFLOW</b>.\",\"<b>POP</b>: deleting the element at the top. After every pop top is <b>decremented by one</b>. Popping when there is no element gives <b>STACK UNDERFLOW</b>.\",\"<b>MAXSIZE</b>: not a standard term; the maximum size of the stack.\",\"<b>TOP</b>: refers to the top of the stack; used to check overflow/underflow. <b>Initially TOP = -1</b>, so on push top is <b>first incremented</b> and then the item is stored at <code>stack[top]</code>.\",\"<b>Underflow</b>: stack contains no element (top at the bottom, top == -1). <b>Overflow</b>: stack is full (top == MAXSIZE-1, top at the highest location).\"]},{\"h\":\"Static vs dynamic implementation\",\"table\":[[\"Static (array)\",\"Dynamic (linked list)\"],[\"Uses an array to create the stack\",\"Uses pointers (linked list representation)\"],[\"Very simple technique\",\"More code but flexible\"],[\"Size declared at design time and cannot be varied\",\"Grows and shrinks at run time, no fixed size\"],[\"Not efficient in memory utilization\",\"Uses only the memory it needs (plus a pointer per node)\"]]},{\"h\":\"isEmpty, isFull, push, pop (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"int MAXSIZE = 4;\\nint stack_arr[4];\\nint top = -1;\\n\\nbool isEmpty(int top)\\n{\\n   if (top == -1) return true;\\n   else return false;\\n}\\nbool isFull(int top)\\n{\\n   if (top == MAXSIZE - 1) return true;\\n   else return false;\\n}\\nvoid push(int item)\\n{\\n   if (isFull(top))\\n      cout << \\\"Stack overflow\\\";\\n   else\\n   { top = top + 1; stack_arr[top] = item; }\\n}\\nint pop()\\n{\\n   if (isEmpty(top)) { cout << \\\"Stack underflow\\\"; return -1; }\\n   else\\n   { int item = stack_arr[top]; top = top - 1; return item; }\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"display, count, search, get (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"void display()      // items in LIFO order\\n{\\n   if (isEmpty(top)) cout << \\\"Nothing to display, stack is empty\\\\n\\\";\\n   else\\n      for (int j = top; j >= 0; j--)\\n         cout << \\\"Item number \\\" << j << \\\" is \\\" << Stack[j] << \\\"\\\\n\\\";\\n}\\nint count()         // from top to bottom\\n{\\n   int c = 0, index = top;\\n   while (index >= 0) { c++; index--; }\\n   return c;        // (simply top + 1)\\n}\\nvoid search(int item)   // from top to bottom\\n{\\n   int temp = top, flag = 0;\\n   while (temp >= 0)\\n   {\\n      if (Stack[temp] == item)\\n      { flag = 1; cout << \\\"Item found at location\\\" << temp << \\\"\\\\n\\\"; break; }\\n      else temp--;       // slide writes temp=temp--; which is undefined/buggy, use temp--\\n   }\\n   if (flag == 0) cout << \\\"Not Found\\\\n\\\";\\n}\\nvoid get()          // show top element without popping (peek)\\n{\\n   if (top != -1) cout << Stack[top] << \\\" is the element at the top of the stack\\\";\\n   else cout << \\\"Stack is Empty\\\";\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Lab 3: stack using a dynamic array\",\"pts\":[\"In the lab the size is read at run time: <code>cin&gt;&gt;size; stack_arr = new int[size];</code> and <code>isFull()</code> checks <code>top == size-1</code>.\",\"The menu uses (i) push, (o) pop, (t) traverse (display from top down to index 0), (e) exit.\",\"Assignments: reverse a string using a stack; sum and average of a full stack; bonus <code>Stack_Copy()</code> from s1 to empty s2 (pop into a temp stack, then pop from temp into s2 to keep the order).\",\"Exam pattern for the statements of push: <code>top++; stack[top] = item;</code> (increment first, then store) because top starts at -1.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Stack\",\"A non-primitive linear structure where insertion and deletion happen at one end (top). LIFO.\"],[\"LIFO\",\"Last-In-First-Out (same as FILO): the last element pushed is the first popped.\"],[\"PUSH\",\"Add an element at the top; top is incremented by one.\"],[\"POP\",\"Delete the element at the top; top is decremented by one.\"],[\"Initial value of TOP\",\"-1\"],[\"Stack empty condition (array)\",\"top == -1\"],[\"Stack full condition (array)\",\"top == MAXSIZE - 1\"],[\"Stack overflow\",\"Pushing when the stack is full.\"],[\"Stack underflow\",\"Popping when the stack is empty.\"],[\"Correct push statements\",\"top++; stack[top] = item;\"],[\"Correct pop statements\",\"item = stack[top]; top--;\"],[\"Static implementation\",\"Uses an array; size fixed at design time; not memory efficient.\"],[\"Dynamic implementation\",\"Linked list representation using pointers.\"],[\"Real-life stack examples\",\"Piled books/clothes, call logs, browser back, undo/redo.\"],[\"Stack applications (lab)\",\"Backtracking, function calls, string reversal.\"],[\"count() of an array stack\",\"top + 1 (counting from top down to 0).\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Define a stack and explain why it is called LIFO.\",\"A stack is a non-primitive linear data structure, an ordered list in which addition and deletion are done from only one end called the top. Since all insertions and deletions happen at the top, the last added element is the first to be removed, so it is a Last-In-First-Out list.\"],[\"Explain stack overflow and stack underflow.\",\"Overflow happens when the stack is full (top == MAXSIZE-1) and a push is attempted: no new element can be accommodated. Underflow happens when the stack has no element (top == -1) and a pop is attempted.\"],[\"Write C++ POP and isEmpty functions for an array stack.\",\"bool isEmpty(int top){ if (top == -1) return true; else return false; }<br>int pop(){ if (isEmpty(top)) { cout&lt;&lt;\\\"Stack underflow\\\"; return -1; } else { int item = stack_arr[top]; top = top-1; return item; } }\"],[\"Write C++ PUSH and isFull functions for an array stack.\",\"bool isFull(int top){ if (top == MAXSIZE-1) return true; else return false; }<br>void push(int item){ if (isFull(top)) cout&lt;&lt;\\\"Stack overflow\\\"; else { top = top+1; stack_arr[top] = item; } }\"],[\"Compare static and dynamic implementation of a stack.\",\"Static uses an array: very simple but not flexible because its size is declared during program design and cannot vary, and it is not efficient in memory utilization. Dynamic uses a linked list with pointers: it works for an unlimited (variable) number of values and needs no fixed size.\"],[\"Give real-life examples and applications of stacks.\",\"Examples: books/clothes piled on each other, mobile call logs, browser history (back button), undo/redo in text editors. Applications: backtracking (maze), function calls, string reversal.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"Pushing an element into a stack already having five elements when the stack size is 5 is called:\",\"o\":[[\"Crash\",\"\\\"Crash\\\" is not a stack condition. Pushing when top == MAXSIZE − 1 is the STACK-FULL condition, which the lecture calls stack overflow.\"],[\"Overflow\",\"Correct. Pushing onto a full stack (top == MAXSIZE-1) is stack overflow.\"],[\"Underflow\",\"Underflow is the opposite case: performing pop on an empty stack (top == −1). Here the stack is full, so the push overflows.\"],[\"User flow\",\"\\\"User flow\\\" is not a stack term. A push onto a full stack (5 of 5 elements) is called stack overflow.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a correct statement for a stack data structure?\",\"o\":[[\"Stack follows FIFO\",\"Correct (this is the wrong statement). A stack follows LIFO; FIFO is a queue.\"],[\"Arrays can be used to implement the stack\",\"This statement is true: the static implementation of a stack uses an array, with TOP holding the index of the top element.\"],[\"Elements are stored in a sequential manner\",\"This statement is true: a stack is a non-primitive linear data structure, an ordered list whose elements are stored one after another.\"],[\"Top of the stack has the last inserted element\",\"This statement is true: all insertions happen at the top, so the last inserted element sits at the top and is the first removed (LIFO).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"To insert an item into a stack, identify the correct set of statements:\",\"o\":[[\"top++; stack[top] = item;\",\"Correct. Top starts at -1, so it is incremented first, then the item is stored there.\"],[\"item = stack[top]; top--;\",\"Reading stack[top] and then decrementing top is the pop operation; it removes an item instead of inserting one.\"],[\"stack[item] = top; item++;\",\"This uses the item as an array index and stores top in it, so the value is never placed at the top. Push is top = top + 1; stack[top] = item.\"],[\"stack[top] = item; top++;\",\"With top starting at -1 this writes to stack[-1] first.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"The process of removing an element from a stack is called:\",\"o\":[[\"Create\",\"Create sets up a new empty stack (top = −1); it does not remove anything. Removing the top element is pop.\"],[\"Push\",\"Push is the opposite: it adds a new element at the top and increments top. Removing is pop.\"],[\"Evaluation\",\"Evaluation is not one of the stack's operations. The lecture's basic operations are push (insert) and pop (delete).\"],[\"Pop\",\"Correct. Pop is the process of deleting the element at the top of the stack; after each pop, top is decremented by one (L2, Operations on stack).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"The process of inserting an element in a stack is called:\",\"o\":[[\"Create\",\"Create only sets up an empty stack with top = −1; adding an element to the top is push.\"],[\"Push\",\"Correct. Push is the process of adding a new element to the top of the stack: top is first incremented, then the item is stored at stack[top] (L2, Operations on stack).\"],[\"Evaluation\",\"Evaluation is not one of the stack's operations. The lecture's basic operations are push (insert) and pop (delete).\"],[\"Pop\",\"Pop is the opposite operation: it deletes the top element and decrements top. Inserting is push.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Insertions and deletions in the stack follow FIFO.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"FIFO (first in, first out) is the queue rule. In a stack both insertion and deletion happen at the top, so it is LIFO.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Insertions and deletions in a stack both happen at the top, so the last element added is the first removed: LIFO, not FIFO (L2, What is a stack?).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Function push() in a stack is used to delete an item.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"push() adds an item at the top (top = top + 1; stack[top] = item). Deleting the top item is pop().\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. push() inserts a new item at the top of the stack; deleting the top item is done by pop() (L2, Push and Pop functions).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Insertions and deletions in a stack follow FILO.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. First-In-Last-Out is the same rule as LIFO.\"],[\"False\",\"FILO and LIFO describe the same order.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Function ______ in a stack is used to insert an item.\",\"o\":[[\"pop()\",\"pop() deletes the top item and decrements top; it does not insert.\"],[\"push()\",\"Correct (official model answer). push() inserts a new item at the top: it checks isFull, then does top = top + 1; stack_arr[top] = item (L2, Push function).\"],[\"get()\",\"get shows the top without changing the stack.\"],[\"count()\",\"count() only returns how many items are stored (top + 1); it does not change the stack.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"Function ______ in a stack is used to delete an item.\",\"o\":[[\"push()\",\"push() inserts an item at the top; it does not delete anything.\"],[\"search()\",\"search() walks from top down to 0 looking for an item and reports its location; it does not remove it.\"],[\"pop()\",\"Correct (official model answer B). pop() deletes the top item: it checks isEmpty, then reads stack_arr[top] and does top = top − 1 (L2, Pop function).\"],[\"display()\",\"display() only prints the items from top to 0 in LIFO order; the stack is unchanged.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"A stack, in other words, is called a LIFO list.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct (model answer A): last in, first out.\"],[\"False\",\"The statement is true: since all insertions and deletions are at the top, the last added element is the first removed, so a stack is a LIFO list.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"A stack is called a FIFO list.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"FIFO is the rule of a queue (insert at rear, delete at front). A stack inserts and deletes at the top, so it is LIFO/FILO.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The model answer corrects it to FILO (= LIFO).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"A stack is a data structure in which insertions and deletions are restricted to one end.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct (model answer B). Both happen at the top.\"],[\"False\",\"Both operations happen at the same end, the top.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"With MAXSIZE = 4 and top initially -1, after push(1), push(2), pop(), push(3), push(4), push(5) what is top?\",\"o\":[[\"2\",\"Count again: after these operations four elements are stored.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. Elements 1,3,4,5 occupy indices 0..3, so top = 3 (the stack is now full).\"],[\"4\",\"top is the index of the top element and can never exceed MAXSIZE − 1 = 3; four stored elements give top = 3, not 4.\"],[\"-1\",\"−1 means an empty stack. After these operations four elements (1, 3, 4, 5) are stored, so top = 3.\"]],\"a\":1}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"MAXSIZE = 4, top = -1. Operations: push(10), push(20), push(30), pop(), push(40), push(50), push(60). What happens?\",\"o\":[[\"All pushes succeed and top = 5\",\"The stack holds at most MAXSIZE = 4 elements, so top cannot exceed 3; push(60) finds the stack full and prints stack overflow.\"],[\"push(50) causes overflow\",\"After the pop there are 3 elements (10, 20, 40), so 50 fits as the 4th.\"],[\"push(60) causes stack overflow; the stack holds 10 20 40 50\",\"Correct. After 50 the stack is full (top = 3), so 60 overflows.\"],[\"pop() causes underflow\",\"Underflow happens only when popping an empty stack. When pop() runs, the stack holds 10, 20, 30, so it removes 30 normally.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"For the stack 10, 20, 40, 50 (50 on top), display() prints:\",\"o\":[[\"10 20 40 50\",\"display loops from top down to 0, which is reverse order.\"],[\"50 40 20 10\",\"Correct. for (j = top; j &gt;= 0; j--) prints in LIFO order.\"],[\"50 10 20 40\",\"display() prints indices top, top−1, …, 0 in a straight loop, so the order can only be 50 40 20 10; it never jumps from 50 to the bottom.\"],[\"Only 50\",\"Printing only the top element is what get() does. display() loops j = top down to 0 and prints every item.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Pushing the characters of \\\"DATA\\\" one by one and then popping them all gives:\",\"o\":[[\"DATA\",\"Getting DATA back unchanged would require FIFO order (a queue). A stack pops the last pushed character first, so it reverses the string.\"],[\"ATAD\",\"Correct. LIFO reverses the string (checked with g++).\"],[\"ADAT\",\"ADAT is not the reverse of DATA. Pops come in exact reverse push order: A, T, A, D.\"],[\"TADA\",\"TADA is not the reverse of DATA. The last pushed character (A) pops first, giving A, T, A, D.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the array stack, what does count() return when top = 6?\",\"o\":[[\"6\",\"6 is the index of the top element. Indices 0 to 6 hold 7 items, so count() returns top + 1 = 7.\"],[\"5\",\"5 undercounts by two. With top = 6, items sit at indices 0..6, which is 7 items.\"],[\"-1\",\"count() returns 0 (not −1) for an empty stack, and the stack is not empty here: top = 6 means 7 items.\"],[\"7\",\"Correct. count() starts at index = top and counts down to 0, so it returns top + 1 = 6 + 1 = 7 items (L2, count function).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Why is TOP initialized to -1?\",\"o\":[[\"So that on push top is first incremented to 0 and the item is stored at index 0\",\"Correct. The lecture says TOP starts at −1 so that on every push TOP is first incremented and the item is stored at that location; the first item lands at index 0 (L2, Stack terminologies).\"],[\"Because C++ arrays start at -1\",\"C++ arrays start at index 0, not −1. −1 is just the \\\"empty\\\" value of TOP, so the first push moves it to 0.\"],[\"To mark the stack as full\",\"−1 marks an empty stack (isEmpty: top == −1). The stack is full when top == MAXSIZE − 1.\"],[\"So that pop returns -1 the first time\",\"pop returns -1 on underflow, but that is not why top starts at -1.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which real-life example from the lecture is NOT a stack?\",\"o\":[[\"Undo/redo in a text editor\",\"Undo/redo in text editors is one of the lecture's stack examples: the last action done is the first undone.\"],[\"Browser back button history\",\"Browser back history is one of the lecture's stack examples: back opens the most recently visited site first.\"],[\"A line of people waiting to buy tickets\",\"Correct. In a ticket line the first person to arrive is served first, which is FIFO, a queue. The lecture's stack examples are piles of books, call logs, browser history and undo/redo (L2, What is a stack?).\"],[\"A pile of books\",\"A pile of books (or clothes) is the lecture's first stack example: you can only add or take the top one.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which statement describes the static (array) implementation of a stack?\",\"o\":[[\"Very simple, but the size is fixed at design time and memory use is not efficient\",\"Correct. The static implementation uses an array: very simple, but not flexible, because the size is declared at program design and cannot change, and memory use is not efficient (L2, Stack implementation).\"],[\"Uses pointers and can grow without limit\",\"Using pointers is the dynamic (linked list) implementation. The static version is an array with a fixed MAXSIZE.\"],[\"Allows insertion at both ends\",\"A stack inserts and deletes at only one end, the top. Insertion at both ends is not a stack property.\"],[\"Never overflows\",\"An array stack overflows when top == MAXSIZE-1.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"The stack holds 4, 9, 2 (2 on top). After pop(), pop(), push(6), what does get() display?\",\"o\":[[\"2\",\"2 was on top, so the first pop() removed it; it is no longer in the stack.\"],[\"9\",\"9 was removed by the second pop(), leaving only 4 before 6 was pushed.\"],[\"4\",\"4 is still in the stack, but at the bottom: push(6) placed 6 above it, and get() shows the top.\"],[\"6\",\"Correct. After two pops only 4 remains; pushing 6 makes it the top.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the lecture's search(), the slide writes <code>temp = temp--;</code>. What should it be?\",\"o\":[[\"temp++;\",\"The search moves from top down to 0, so temp must decrease.\"],[\"temp--; (or temp = temp - 1;)\",\"Correct. temp = temp--; does not reliably decrease temp, so it can loop forever.\"],[\"top--;\",\"top-- would move the top of the stack and in effect lose items. search() must only move its own copy, temp, down to 0.\"],[\"temp = top;\",\"temp = top resets the index to the top on every pass, so the loop would check the same element forever. It needs temp--.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which lab application uses a stack to keep information about active functions?\",\"o\":[[\"Backtracking\",\"Backtracking uses a stack to return along a maze path.\"],[\"String reversal\",\"In string reversal the stack reverses a string by pushing its characters and popping them; it does not track active functions.\"],[\"Function call\",\"Correct. Lab 3 lists the function call as a stack application: the stack keeps information about the active functions, the last called returning first (Lab 3, Stack applications).\"],[\"Hashing\",\"Hashing (L10) maps keys to table slots with a hash function; it is not one of the lab's stack applications.\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":3,\"title\":\"Linear and Circular Queues using Arrays\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"What is a queue?\",\"pts\":[\"A queue is a <b>non-primitive linear</b> data structure, <b>open at both ends</b>, whose operations are performed in <b>FIFO</b> (First In First Out) order.\",\"All <b>insertions</b> are made at one end, the <b>tail or rear</b>, and all <b>deletions</b> are made at the other end, the <b>head or front</b>.\",\"Like a line waiting to buy tickets: the first person in line is served first.\",\"<b>Front (head)</b>: position of the entry ready to be served (the first to be removed). <b>Rear (tail)</b>: position of the last entry, the one most recently added.\",\"<b>enqueue()</b> inserts at the rear; <b>dequeue()</b> removes from the front. Initially head = tail = -1.\"]},{\"h\":\"Linear queue: enqueue and dequeue (professor's code)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"queue\",\"size\":5,\"ops\":[\"enqueue 10\",\"enqueue 20\",\"enqueue 30\",\"dequeue\",\"dequeue\"]},\"code\":\"bool isFull(int tail)\\n{\\n   if (tail == maxsize - 1) return true;\\n   else return false;\\n}\\nvoid enqueue(int item)\\n{\\n   if (isFull(tail))\\n   { cout << \\\"\\\\nQueue is full\\\"; return; }\\n   else\\n   {\\n      if (head == -1) head = 0;   // first element\\n      tail = tail + 1;\\n      queue[tail] = item;\\n   }\\n}\\nbool isEmpty(int head)\\n{\\n   if (head == -1 || head > tail) return true;\\n   else return false;\\n}\\nint dequeue()\\n{\\n   int element;\\n   if (isEmpty(head))\\n   { cout << \\\"Queue Underflow \\\"; return -1; }\\n   else\\n   {\\n      element = queue[head];\\n      head++;\\n   }\\n   return element;\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Problem of the linear queue\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"queue\",\"size\":5,\"ops\":[\"enqueue 10\",\"enqueue 20\",\"enqueue 30\",\"enqueue 40\",\"enqueue 50\",\"dequeue\",\"dequeue\",\"enqueue 60\"]},\"pts\":[\"In the slide trace (array 0..7) the rear moves right on every insert and the front moves right on every delete.\",\"When the rear reaches the last index (<b>rear == MAX-1</b>) the queue reports full, <b>even if elements were dequeued</b> and the cells at the start are empty (\\\"NO SPACE HERE\\\" at the end while there is \\\"SPACE HERE\\\" at the front).\",\"So after some insertions and deletions there is <b>non-usable empty space</b>; dequeuing moves the front forward and reduces the usable size of the queue. This memory wastage is the <b>need for the circular queue</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Circular queue\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"cqueue\",\"size\":5,\"ops\":[\"enqueue 10\",\"enqueue 20\",\"enqueue 30\",\"enqueue 40\",\"enqueue 50\",\"dequeue\",\"dequeue\",\"enqueue 60\",\"enqueue 70\",\"enqueue 80\"]},\"pts\":[\"A circular queue is the extended version of a regular queue where the <b>last element is connected to the first element</b>, forming a circle-like structure. It solves the major limitation of the linear queue (unusable empty space).\",\"Two pointers: <b>head</b> tracks the first element, <b>tail</b> tracks the last. Initially both are <b>-1</b> (queue empty).\",\"<b>Enqueue</b>: check full with two cases: (1) <b>head == 0 &amp;&amp; tail == SIZE-1</b>; (2) <b>tail is just one less than head</b> (the slide writes <code>tail == (head-1) % (MAXSIZE-1)</code>; the standard single test is <code>(tail+1) % SIZE == head</code>). For the first element set head = 0. Circularly increase tail: <code>tail = (tail+1) % SIZE</code>. Store the element at tail.\",\"<b>Dequeue</b>: check empty (head == -1). Return the value at head. Circularly increase head: <code>head = (head+1) % SIZE</code>. For the <b>last element</b> (head == tail) reset head and tail to -1.\",\"Slide example: after 2 dequeues both queues have front at 72 and rear at 24; inserting 100 is <b>not possible in the linear queue</b> (rear at the end) but <b>possible in the circular queue</b> (rear wraps to index 0).\"]},{\"h\":\"Linear vs circular queue\",\"table\":[[\"Linear queue\",\"Circular queue\"],[\"Elements arranged in a straight line\",\"Last position is connected back to the first\"],[\"Full when rear == MAX-1\",\"Full when (front == 0 && rear == MAX-1) or rear is one behind front\"],[\"Empty cells before front cannot be reused (memory wastage)\",\"Freed cells are reused (rear wraps around)\"],[\"rear = rear + 1\",\"rear = (rear + 1) % MAX\"],[\"Empty when front == -1 or front > rear\",\"Empty when front == -1 (reset to -1 after removing the last element)\"]]},{\"h\":\"Circular queue enQueue / deQueue (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"bool isFull()\\n{\\n   if ((front == 0 && rear == MAX_SIZE - 1) || (rear == (front - 1) % (MAX_SIZE - 1)))\\n      return true;\\n   return false;\\n}\\nvoid enQueue(int value)\\n{\\n   if (isFull()) { cout << \\\"Queue is full.\\\" << endl; }\\n   else\\n   {\\n      if (front == -1) { front = 0; }\\n      rear = (rear + 1) % MAX_SIZE;\\n      arr[rear] = value;\\n      cout << \\\"Enqueued element: \\\" << value << endl;\\n   }\\n}\\nbool isEmpty()\\n{\\n   if (front == -1) return true;\\n   return false;\\n}\\nint deQueue()\\n{\\n   int element;\\n   if (isEmpty()) { cout << \\\"Queue is empty.\\\" << endl; return -1; }\\n   else\\n   {\\n      element = arr[front];\\n      if (front == rear) { front = -1; rear = -1; }   // last element removed\\n      else { front = (front + 1) % MAX_SIZE; }\\n      cout << \\\"Dequeued element: \\\" << element << endl;\\n      return element;\\n   }\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Worked trace (Exam 2022/23, size 5)\",\"formula\":[\"enqueue(10): front = 0, rear = 0, arr[0] = 10\",\"enqueue(20): rear = 1 → print \\\"Head = 0 Tail = 1\\\"\",\"enqueue(30): rear = 2\",\"cout << dequeue(): returns 10, front = 1 → prints 10\",\"x = dequeue(): x = 20, front = 2\",\"print \\\"Head = 2 Tail = 2\\\" (one element, 30, is left)\",\"(If the slide's deQueue/enQueue with their own cout messages are used, \\\"Enqueued element: …\\\" and \\\"Dequeued element: …\\\" lines also appear; checked with g++.)\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Queue\",\"Non-primitive linear structure open at both ends; FIFO.\"],[\"FIFO\",\"First In First Out: the first element inserted is the first removed.\"],[\"Where does enqueue insert?\",\"At the rear (tail).\"],[\"Where does dequeue remove?\",\"From the front (head).\"],[\"Linear queue full condition\",\"rear == MAX - 1\"],[\"Linear queue empty condition\",\"head == -1 || head > tail\"],[\"Initial head and tail\",\"-1 and -1\"],[\"Problem of the linear queue\",\"Cells freed by dequeue cannot be reused; rear stays at the end so the queue looks full (memory wastage).\"],[\"Circular queue\",\"Queue whose last position connects to the first, reusing freed cells.\"],[\"Circular queue: advance rear\",\"rear = (rear + 1) % MAX_SIZE\"],[\"Circular queue empty\",\"front == rear == -1\"],[\"Circular queue full (two cases)\",\"front == 0 && rear == MAX-1, or rear is one less than front.\"],[\"Circular dequeue of the last element\",\"Reset front and rear to -1.\"],[\"Need for the circular queue\",\"To avoid memory wastage.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Define a queue and its front and rear.\",\"A queue is a non-primitive linear data structure open at both ends, operating in FIFO order. All insertions are made at one end (tail/rear) and all deletions at the other (head/front). The front is the position of the entry ready to be served; the rear is the position of the most recently added entry.\"],[\"What is the problem of the linear queue and how does the circular queue solve it?\",\"In a linear queue, dequeuing moves the front forward, and when the rear reaches MAX-1 no element can be enqueued even if cells at the beginning are empty, so space is wasted. A circular queue connects the last position to the first: rear = (rear+1) % MAX wraps to index 0 and reuses the freed cells.\"],[\"Write C++ enqueue and isFull functions for a linear queue.\",\"bool isFull(int tail){ if (tail == maxsize-1) return true; else return false; }<br>void enqueue(int item){ if (isFull(tail)) { cout&lt;&lt;\\\"Queue is full\\\"; return; } else { if (head == -1) head = 0; tail = tail+1; queue[tail] = item; } }\"],[\"Write C++ dequeue and isEmpty functions for a linear queue.\",\"bool isEmpty(int head){ if (head == -1 || head &gt; tail) return true; else return false; }<br>int dequeue(){ int element; if (isEmpty(head)) { cout&lt;&lt;\\\"Queue Underflow\\\"; return -1; } else { element = queue[head]; head++; } return element; }\"],[\"Explain the enqueue and dequeue steps of a circular queue.\",\"Enqueue: check full (head == 0 and tail == SIZE-1, or tail just one less than head); for the first element set head = 0; circularly increase tail by 1 (if it reaches the end set it to 0); store the element at tail. Dequeue: check empty (head == -1); take the value at head; if it was the last element (head == tail) reset head and tail to -1, else circularly increase head by 1.\"],[\"Compare linear and circular queues.\",\"Linear: straight line, full when rear == MAX-1, freed cells are wasted, rear = rear+1. Circular: last cell connected to first, full when front == 0 and rear == MAX-1 or rear one behind front, freed cells reused, rear = (rear+1) % MAX, reset to -1 when the last element is removed.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"In a linear queue implemented using an array of size MAX, the queue gets full when:\",\"o\":[[\"Front = (rear + 1) mod MAX\",\"front == (rear + 1) mod MAX is the full test of a circular queue, where rear wraps around. A linear queue never wraps; it is full when rear reaches MAX − 1.\"],[\"Front = rear + 1\",\"In the lecture code front > rear means empty, not full.\"],[\"Rear = MAX - 1\",\"Correct. The lecture's isFull returns true when tail == maxsize − 1: the rear is at the last cell and a linear queue cannot move past it (L3, C++ enqueue function).\"],[\"Rear = front\",\"rear == front means the head and tail are on the same cell, i.e. exactly one element is stored, not a full queue.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following determines the need for the circular queue?\",\"o\":[[\"Avoid memory wastage\",\"Correct. The linear queue cannot reuse cells freed by dequeue.\"],[\"Access the queue using priority\",\"Serving elements by priority is what a priority queue does; the circular queue still serves in FIFO order.\"],[\"Follows the FIFO principle\",\"Both linear and circular queues follow FIFO, so this is not the reason.\"],[\"None\",\"There is a reason: the circular queue exists to reuse the cells a linear queue wastes after dequeues (option a).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"If a circular queue is implemented using an array of size MAX_SIZE, which condition specifies that the circular queue is empty?\",\"o\":[[\"front = rear = 0\",\"front = rear = 0 means one element at index 0.\"],[\"front = rear + 1\",\"front == rear + 1 is not the circular queue's empty test; the lecture's isEmpty checks front == −1, after both are reset to −1.\"],[\"front = rear = -1\",\"Correct. The lecture initializes and resets both to -1; isEmpty checks front == -1.\"],[\"None\",\"One of the options is right: the lecture starts with front = rear = −1 and resets both to −1 when the last element leaves (option c).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"The condition if (front == 0 && rear == MAX_SIZE - 1) is enough to check the fullness of a circular queue.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"That condition only covers Case 1 (front 0, rear at the end). It misses Case 2: rear wrapped around and sits just one behind front.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The queue is also full when rear is just one less than front (rear wrapped around).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"The value of REAR is increased by 1 when an element is inserted in the queue.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. enqueue does tail = tail + 1 (circularly in a circular queue).\"],[\"False\",\"The statement is true: enqueue does tail = tail + 1 (or (rear + 1) % MAX_SIZE in a circular queue) before storing the item.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following data structures allows insertion and deletion from both ends?\",\"o\":[[\"Stack\",\"A stack inserts and deletes at one end only, the top; it is not open at both ends.\"],[\"Queue\",\"Correct (expected answer). The lecture says a queue is \\\"open at both ends\\\": insert at the rear, delete at the front. Strictly, insertion AND deletion at both ends is a deque, which is not offered.\"],[\"Linked list\",\"A linked list can insert/delete anywhere, but the question targets the queue's \\\"both ends\\\" definition.\"],[\"Array\",\"A plain array has no defined insertion or deletion ends; stacks and queues are built on top of it with TOP or front/rear indices.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"The circular queue is the same as the regular queue.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"They differ in how indices advance and reuse space.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. A circular queue connects the last position to the first and reuses freed cells.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Enqueue() inserts an element at the rear of the queue.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. All insertions are made at one end, the tail (rear): enqueue does tail = tail + 1 and stores the item at queue[tail] (L3, What is a queue?).\"],[\"False\",\"The statement is true: a queue inserts only at the rear (tail) and deletes only at the front (head).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Dequeue(): removes the element at the front of the queue.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. All deletions are made at the head (front): dequeue reads queue[head] and then moves head forward (L3, C++ dequeue function).\"],[\"False\",\"The statement is true: dequeue always removes the element at the front (head) of the queue, the oldest one (FIFO).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Removing an element occurs at the ______ of the queue.\",\"o\":[[\"Rear\",\"The rear (tail) is where enqueue adds new elements; elements are removed from the other end, the front.\"],[\"Front\",\"Correct (official model answer). A queue deletes at the front (head): the first entry ready to be served, which is the one that came in first (L3, What is a queue?).\"],[\"Middle\",\"A queue never removes from the middle; it only deletes at the front, keeping FIFO order.\"],[\"Top\",\"Top is a stack term (stacks add and remove at the top). Queues remove at the front.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"enqueue() inserts an element at the ______ of the queue.\",\"o\":[[\"Front\",\"The front (head) is where elements leave the queue through dequeue, not where they are inserted.\"],[\"Top\",\"Top is the single end of a stack. A queue has two ends: rear for insertion and front for deletion.\"],[\"Rear\",\"Correct (official model answer B). enqueue adds the new element at the rear (tail): tail = tail + 1; queue[tail] = item (L3, C++ enqueue function).\"],[\"Head\",\"Head is another name for the front, where elements are removed. Insertion happens at the tail (rear).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"A queue is a data structure in which insertions and deletions are restricted to one end.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Restricting both insertion and deletion to one end describes a stack. A queue is open at both ends: insert at the rear, delete at the front.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Model answer: insertions at the rear and deletions at the front.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"A circular queue of size 5: enqueue 10, 20, 30, then dequeue twice. What are head and tail?\",\"o\":[[\"head = 0, tail = 2\",\"head = 0 ignores the dequeues. Each dequeue moves head one step forward, so two dequeues move it from 0 to 2.\"],[\"head = 2, tail = 2\",\"Correct. The enqueues fill indices 0, 1, 2 (head = 0, tail = 2). Each dequeue does head = (head + 1) % 5, so head = 2; only 30 remains, at head = tail = 2 (L3, Circular queue deQueue).\"],[\"head = -1, tail = -1\",\"Both are reset to −1 only when the last element is removed. One element (30) is still in the queue.\"],[\"head = 1, tail = 2\",\"head = 1 is the state after only one dequeue. The second dequeue removes 20 and moves head to 2.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"A linear queue of size 5: enqueue 1, 2, 3, 4, 5; dequeue twice; then enqueue(6). What happens?\",\"o\":[[\"6 is stored at index 0\",\"Storing 6 at index 0 is what a circular queue does. A linear queue's tail only increases, so it cannot go back to index 0.\"],[\"6 is stored at index 5\",\"Index 5 does not exist in a queue of size 5 (indices 0..4); isFull stops the insert because tail == maxsize − 1.\"],[\"\\\"Queue is full\\\", because tail == maxsize-1 even though indices 0 and 1 are free\",\"Correct. This is exactly the problem of the linear queue.\"],[\"\\\"Queue Underflow\\\"\",\"Queue Underflow is printed by dequeue on an empty queue. This is an enqueue, and isFull blocks it.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Same operations on a CIRCULAR queue of size 5 (enqueue 1..5, dequeue twice, enqueue 6). Where is 6 stored?\",\"o\":[[\"Index 0\",\"Correct. rear = (4 + 1) % 5 = 0, a cell freed by dequeue.\"],[\"Index 5\",\"Index 5 does not exist (indices 0..4). The circular queue computes rear = (4 + 1) % 5 = 0 instead of 5.\"],[\"It is rejected as full\",\"Two cells were freed, so the circular queue is not full.\"],[\"Index 2\",\"Index 2 is where front is now (the element 3). The new rear is the next cell after 4, wrapping around to (4 + 1) % 5 = 0.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the linear queue, which condition means the queue is empty?\",\"o\":[[\"head == tail\",\"head == tail means head and tail are on the same cell, which holds exactly one element.\"],[\"head == -1 || head &gt; tail\",\"Correct. The lecture's isEmpty returns true when head == −1 (nothing was ever inserted) or head &gt; tail (every inserted element has been dequeued) (L3, C++ dequeue function).\"],[\"tail == maxsize - 1\",\"tail == maxsize − 1 is the linear queue's full test (isFull), not the empty test.\"],[\"tail == -1 || tail &gt; head\",\"This swaps head and tail. Emptiness depends on head: head == −1 or head having passed tail (head &gt; tail).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the circular queue deQueue, what happens when front == rear before removal?\",\"o\":[[\"front = (front + 1) % MAX_SIZE\",\"Advancing front by (front + 1) % MAX_SIZE is the else branch, used when more than one element remains.\"],[\"Nothing; the element is not removed\",\"The element is removed and returned. Because it was the only one (front == rear), the queue becomes empty.\"],[\"rear = front - 1\",\"The lecture never sets rear = front − 1. When the last element is removed, both front and rear are reset to −1.\"],[\"front and rear are both reset to -1\",\"Correct. The last element was removed, so the queue becomes empty.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Circular queue with MAX_SIZE = 4: front = 2, rear = 3. After enQueue(x), rear is:\",\"o\":[[\"4\",\"Index 4 is out of range for MAX_SIZE = 4 (indices 0..3). The modulo wraps it: (3 + 1) % 4 = 0.\"],[\"0\",\"Correct. enQueue moves rear circularly: rear = (rear + 1) % MAX_SIZE = (3 + 1) % 4 = 0, reusing the cell at the start (L3, Circular queue enQueue).\"],[\"3\",\"rear must move to a new cell before storing x, or x would overwrite the element at index 3.\"],[\"1\",\"1 would be two steps from 3 (3 → 0 → 1). enQueue advances rear by exactly one: (3 + 1) % 4 = 0.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the slide's linear queue trace, the rear is at the last index but there is space at the front. This is called:\",\"o\":[[\"Stack overflow\",\"It is a queue, and the queue is not really full.\"],[\"Underflow\",\"Underflow means removing from an empty queue. Here the queue still has elements; the free cells are just unreachable.\"],[\"Non-usable empty space (memory wastage) of the linear queue\",\"Correct. After dequeues the front moves forward, but the linear queue's tail is still at the end, so the freed cells at the front are non-usable empty space; the circular queue solves this (L3, Problem of linear queue).\"],[\"Collision\",\"Collision is a hashing term (two keys hashing to the same slot, L10); it has nothing to do with queues.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Linear queue (maxsize 8): enqueue 4, 7, 9; dequeue; enqueue 3. What are head and tail?\",\"o\":[[\"head = 1, tail = 3\",\"Correct. head moved from 0 to 1 after one dequeue; tail went 0, 1, 2, 3.\"],[\"head = 0, tail = 3\",\"head = 0 ignores the dequeue. dequeue reads queue[head] and does head++, so head becomes 1.\"],[\"head = 1, tail = 2\",\"tail = 2 is the state before enqueue(3). That enqueue does tail = tail + 1, so tail becomes 3.\"],[\"head = 0, tail = 4\",\"There were only 4 enqueues, so tail went 0, 1, 2, 3; and the dequeue moved head to 1, not 0.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which is the standard single condition for a FULL circular queue?\",\"o\":[[\"rear == MAX_SIZE - 1\",\"rear == MAX_SIZE − 1 alone is the linear queue's full test. In a circular queue rear can be at the end while cells at the front are free.\"],[\"front == -1\",\"front == −1 is the circular queue's empty test (isEmpty), the opposite of full.\"],[\"front == rear\",\"front == rear means a single element is stored, not a full queue.\"],[\"(rear + 1) % MAX_SIZE == front\",\"Correct. It covers both lecture cases (front 0 / rear MAX-1, and rear just behind front).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the slide example, after two dequeues front is at 72 and rear at 24 (rear at the last cell). Inserting 100 is:\",\"o\":[[\"Possible in both queues\",\"It is not possible in the linear queue: its rear is at the last cell, so isFull is true even though the front cells are free.\"],[\"Not possible in the linear queue but possible in the circular queue\",\"Correct. The linear queue's rear is at the last cell, so isFull blocks 100; the circular queue wraps rear to index 0, one of the cells freed by the dequeues (L3, Operations of circular queue).\"],[\"Possible in the linear queue only\",\"This is the reverse. The linear queue cannot reuse the freed front cells; only the circular queue can.\"],[\"Impossible in both queues\",\"The circular queue can insert 100: rear wraps to index 0, a cell freed by the two dequeues.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Queue holds (front) 8, 3, 5 (rear). What does the sequence dequeue(), enqueue(8), dequeue() return the second time?\",\"o\":[[\"8\",\"8 was removed by the first dequeue; the re-inserted 8 is at the rear.\"],[\"5\",\"5 is still behind 3 in the queue, so it cannot leave before 3.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. FIFO: first 8 leaves, then 3 is at the front.\"],[\"Nothing, the queue is empty\",\"After the first dequeue, 3 and 5 remain and 8 is added at the rear, so the second dequeue has elements to remove.\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":4,\"title\":\"Pointers and Dynamic Allocation\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"What is a pointer?\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>pointer is a data type</b>; you can create variables of this type like any other type. A pointer variable <b>contains a memory address</b> and <b>points to a specific data type</b>.\",\"Lab definition: a <b>pointer variable</b> is a variable whose content is an <b>address</b> (a memory address).\",\"Example: <code>int x; int *pointer1 = &amp;x;</code> pointer1 holds the address of x (e.g. 0x2000), not its value.\"]},{\"h\":\"Pointer operations\",\"table\":[[\"Expression\",\"Meaning\"],[\"Type* aPtr;\",\"Declares a pointer to Type\"],[\"aPtr = &object;\",\"Assigns the address of object to aPtr (& = address-of)\"],[\"*aPtr\",\"Accesses the object through the pointer (dereference)\"],[\"(*aPtr).member\",\"Member of the structure aPtr points to\"],[\"aPtr->member\",\"Same as (*aPtr).member (arrow operator)\"],[\"new Type / new Type[n]\",\"Allocates memory on the heap at run time and returns its address\"],[\"delete p / delete[] p\",\"Frees memory allocated with new / new[]\"]]},{\"h\":\"Pointers and function arguments: fakeSwap vs trueSwap\",\"code\":\"// Solution 1: pass by value (does NOT swap)\\nvoid fakeSwap(int a, int b)\\n{\\n   int tmp;\\n   tmp = a;\\n   a = b;\\n   b = tmp;        // only the copies a and b are swapped\\n}\\n// Solution 2: pass addresses (swaps)\\nvoid trueSwap(int* a, int* b)\\n{\\n   int tmp;\\n   tmp = *a;       // tmp = 1\\n   *a = *b;        // x = 2\\n   *b = tmp;       // y = 1\\n}\\nint main()\\n{\\n   int x = 1, y = 2;\\n   fakeSwap(x, y);\\n   cout << \\\"x= \\\" << x << \\\" y= \\\" << y << endl;   // x= 1 y= 2\\n   trueSwap(&x, &y);\\n   cout << \\\"x= \\\" << x << \\\" y= \\\" << y << endl;   // x= 2 y= 1\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Why fakeSwap fails (memory trace from the slides)\",\"formula\":[\"main: x = 1 at 0x2000, y = 2 at 0x2010\",\"fakeSwap(x, y) creates NEW variables a = 1 (0x2038), b = 2 (0x2040), tmp (0x2060)\",\"tmp = a → 1; a = b → a = 2; b = tmp → b = 1\",\"function returns, a and b disappear; x and y are unchanged: x = 1, y = 2\",\"trueSwap(&x, &y): a = address of x, b = address of y\",\"tmp = *a → 1; *a = *b → x = 2; *b = tmp → y = 1  ⇒ x = 2, y = 1\"]},{\"h\":\"Lab: pointer program\",\"code\":\"#include <iostream>\\nusing namespace std;\\nint main()\\n{\\n   int x = 5, *ptr;\\n   ptr = &x;              // stores the address of x in ptr\\n   cout << \\\"The Value of X =\\\" << x << endl;                 // 5\\n   cout << \\\"The Address of X =\\\" << &x << endl;              // e.g. 001DFAD8\\n   cout << \\\"The Value of ptr =\\\" << ptr << endl;             // same address as &x\\n   cout << \\\"The Value pointed by pointer ptr \\\" << *ptr << endl;  // 5\\n   cout << \\\"The Address of ptr =\\\" << &ptr << endl;          // e.g. 001DFACC\\n   *ptr = 24;\\n   cout << \\\"The Value of X after changes =\\\" << x << endl;   // 24\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Dynamic arrays and memory segments (Lab)\",\"pts\":[\"A normal array's size must be a <b>constant expression</b> fixed at design time. <code>new</code> allocates memory <b>at run time</b> using any variable value as the size: <code>int *arr; cin&gt;&gt;size; arr = new int[size]; ... delete[] arr;</code>\",\"<code>new</code> allocates the memory needed for the object/array and the pointer stores the address of this block.\",\"Every program has three memory segments: <b>code segment</b> (compiled machine code), <b>stack segment</b> (local/temporary variables) and <b>heap segment</b> (dynamic memory allocation with new).\",\"Linked lists, linked stacks and linked queues use <b>dynamic</b> memory allocation: each node is created with <code>new</code> at run time.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Pointer\",\"A data type whose variables contain a memory address and point to a specific data type.\"],[\"&x\",\"The address of x.\"],[\"*p\",\"The value stored at the address held in p (dereference).\"],[\"p->member\",\"Equivalent to (*p).member.\"],[\"fakeSwap(x, y) result\",\"x and y unchanged (pass by value swaps only the copies).\"],[\"trueSwap(&x, &y) result\",\"x and y swapped (pointers let the function change the originals).\"],[\"new\",\"Allocates memory at run time (heap) and returns its address.\"],[\"delete[] arr\",\"Frees a dynamic array allocated with new[].\"],[\"Three memory segments\",\"Code segment, stack segment (local variables), heap segment (dynamic allocation).\"],[\"Dynamic vs normal array size\",\"Normal: constant at design time. Dynamic: any value at run time.\"],[\"int x=5,*ptr; ptr=&x; *ptr=24; x = ?\",\"24\"],[\"Where are local variables stored?\",\"In the stack segment.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"What is a pointer? Explain & and * with an example.\",\"A pointer is a data type whose variables contain a memory address of a variable of a specific type. &amp;x gives the address of x; *p accesses the value at the address in p. Example: int x = 5, *ptr; ptr = &amp;x; *ptr = 24; now x is 24 because ptr points to x.\"],[\"Explain why fakeSwap does not swap x and y while trueSwap does.\",\"fakeSwap(int a, int b) receives copies of x and y in new memory locations; swapping a and b does not touch x and y. trueSwap(int* a, int* b) receives the addresses of x and y, so *a and *b refer to x and y themselves; tmp = *a; *a = *b; *b = tmp; swaps the originals (x = 2, y = 1).\"],[\"Compare a normal array and a dynamic array.\",\"A normal array's size must be a constant expression decided at design time, and it lives in the stack segment (if local). A dynamic array is allocated with new at run time using any variable size (heap segment), accessed through a pointer, and must be freed with delete[].\"],[\"Name the three memory segments of a program.\",\"Code segment: compiled machine code. Stack segment: local/temporary variables. Heap segment: dynamically allocated memory (new/delete).\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"Linked list is considered as an example of ______ type of memory allocation.\",\"o\":[[\"Static\",\"Static allocation fixes the size at compile time (arrays).\"],[\"Dynamic\",\"Correct. Each node is created with new at run time.\"],[\"Compile time\",\"Compile-time allocation is static allocation: the size is fixed before the program runs, as with an array. Linked list nodes are created with new at run time.\"],[\"Heap\",\"Nodes do live on the heap, but the allocation type asked for is dynamic.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"After <code>int x = 5, *ptr; ptr = &x; *ptr = 24;</code> what is x?\",\"o\":[[\"5\",\"5 was x's value before the last statement. ptr holds &x, so *ptr = 24 writes 24 into x itself.\"],[\"24\",\"Correct. ptr points to x, so *ptr = 24 changes x.\"],[\"The address of x\",\"The address of x is what ptr holds (ptr = &x). x itself holds a value, and *ptr = 24 changed it to 24.\"],[\"29\",\"29 would mean 24 was added to 5. *ptr = 24 is an assignment through the pointer: it replaces x's value with 24.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Lecture 4 example\"},{\"q\":\"With <code>int x = 1, y = 2; fakeSwap(x, y);</code> the program prints:\",\"o\":[[\"x= 2 y= 1\",\"x= 2 y= 1 is the output of trueSwap(&x, &y), which swaps through pointers. fakeSwap gets copies, so x and y stay 1 and 2.\"],[\"x= 1 y= 2\",\"Correct. Pass by value swaps only the copies a and b.\"],[\"x= 1 y= 1\",\"fakeSwap never assigns to x or y; it only changes its own copies a and b. Both keep their values: x = 1, y = 2.\"],[\"Compile error\",\"The code compiles: fakeSwap(int a, int b) is called with two ints. It just has no effect on x and y.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Lecture 4 example\"},{\"q\":\"If p points to a struct, <code>p->price</code> is equivalent to:\",\"o\":[[\"*p.price\",\"Without brackets . binds first: *(p.price), which is wrong.\"],[\"(*p).price\",\"Correct. The arrow operator dereferences then selects the member.\"],[\"&p.price\",\"&p.price tries to take the address of p.price; it neither dereferences p nor reads the member's value. p->price is (*p).price.\"],[\"p.price\",\"p is a pointer, not a struct object, so the dot cannot be applied to it directly. You must dereference first: (*p).price.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which memory segment is used by new?\",\"o\":[[\"Code segment\",\"The code segment holds the compiled (machine) code of the program, not data allocated with new.\"],[\"Stack segment\",\"The stack segment holds local and temporary variables. Memory requested with new comes from the heap.\"],[\"Heap segment\",\"Correct. Every program has three segments: code (machine code), stack (local/temporary variables) and heap, which is used for dynamic memory allocation with new (Lab 3, Dynamic array).\"],[\"Data register\",\"A data register is a CPU register, not one of the program's three memory segments (code, stack, heap).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which statement correctly frees <code>arr = new int[size];</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"delete arr;\",\"For arrays allocated with new[] use delete[].\"],[\"free arr;\",\"free arr; is not valid C++ syntax (free is a C function). Memory from new[] must be released with delete[].\"],[\"delete[] arr;\",\"Correct. A block allocated with new int[size] is released with delete[] arr;, exactly as the Lab 3 dynamic array program does at the end (Lab 3, Dynamic array).\"],[\"arr = NULL;\",\"This loses the address without freeing (memory leak).\"]],\"a\":2}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"<code>int a = 3, b = 7; int *p = &a, *q = &b; *p = *q + 1; q = p; *q = 2 * (*q);</code> What are a and b?\",\"o\":[[\"a = 8, b = 14\",\"b = 14 assumes q still points to b (2 · 7). But q = p makes q point to a, so the doubling changes a: 2 · 8 = 16.\"],[\"a = 16, b = 7\",\"Correct. *p = 7 + 1 sets a = 8; q now points to a; *q = 2 · 8 = 16. b is unchanged (checked with g++).\"],[\"a = 3, b = 16\",\"a cannot stay 3: *p = *q + 1 writes 7 + 1 = 8 into a through p. And b is never written, so it stays 7.\"],[\"a = 8, b = 7\",\"a = 8 is the value after the first assignment only. After q = p, *q = 2 · (*q) doubles a to 16.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which expression gives the address of variable x?\",\"o\":[[\"*x\",\"*x dereferences a pointer (reads the value it points to); it does not give the address of x.\"],[\"x*\",\"x* is not a valid expression. The * goes in a declaration before the name (int *p) or before a pointer to dereference.\"],[\"&x\",\"Correct. The address-of operator & gives a variable's memory address, e.g. ptr = &x stores x's address in ptr (L4, Pointer operations).\"],[\"x->\",\"-> accesses a struct member through a pointer (aPtr->member); it does not give an address.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In fakeSwap(int a, int b), why are x and y unchanged in main?\",\"o\":[[\"Because a and b are copies stored at different addresses\",\"Correct. Pass by value gives the function its own variables.\"],[\"Because tmp is not initialized\",\"tmp is assigned (tmp = a) before it is used, so it is not uninitialized. The swap does happen, but only on the copies a and b.\"],[\"Because the function has no return statement\",\"A return statement would not help: a void function returning nothing changes nothing, and a and b are separate copies of x and y.\"],[\"Because x and y are constants\",\"x and y are ordinary int variables (int x = 1, y = 2), not constants. They are unchanged because only copies were passed.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which call correctly uses trueSwap(int* a, int* b)?\",\"o\":[[\"trueSwap(x, y);\",\"trueSwap takes int* parameters, so it needs the addresses &x and &y; passing x and y passes their values.\"],[\"trueSwap(*x, *y);\",\"x and y are ints, not pointers, so *x and *y cannot be dereferenced. The function needs &x and &y.\"],[\"trueSwap(&x, y);\",\"Both parameters are int*, so both arguments must be addresses; y is passed by value here.\"],[\"trueSwap(&x, &y);\",\"Correct. trueSwap(int* a, int* b) takes addresses, so it is called with &x and &y; then *a and *b reach x and y themselves and the swap is real (L4, Solution 2).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the lab program, <code>cout &lt;&lt; ptr;</code> prints:\",\"o\":[[\"The value of x (5)\",\"The value 5 is printed by cout &lt;&lt; *ptr (the value pointed to) or cout &lt;&lt; x. ptr itself holds an address.\"],[\"The address of x\",\"Correct. ptr = &x stores the address of x in ptr, so printing ptr shows that address (001DFAD8 in the lab), not the value (Lab 3, Program #1).\"],[\"The address of ptr\",\"The address of ptr itself is printed by cout &lt;&lt; &ptr (001DFACC in the lab); ptr holds the address of x.\"],[\"24\",\"24 is assigned later through *ptr = 24, and it is a value of x, not what ptr holds.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between <code>int arr[10];</code> and <code>arr = new int[size];</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"The first is faster to index\",\"Both are indexed the same way, arr[i]; the lab's difference is when the size is decided, not indexing speed.\"],[\"Only the second can store ints\",\"Both declarations store ints. The difference is that new allows a size chosen at run time.\"],[\"The first's size must be a constant at design time; the second's size can be any value at run time\",\"Correct. A regular array's size must be a constant expression fixed when the program is designed, while new assigns memory at run time using any variable value as the size (Lab 3, Dynamic array).\"],[\"The second cannot be freed\",\"Memory from new[] can and should be freed, with delete[] arr; as the lab program does.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which memory segment holds local/temporary variables?\",\"o\":[[\"Stack segment\",\"Correct. The lab names three segments: code (machine code), stack (local/temporary variables) and heap (dynamic allocation) (Lab 3, Dynamic array).\"],[\"Heap segment\",\"The heap segment is used for dynamic memory allocation with new, not for local variables.\"],[\"Code segment\",\"The code segment holds the compiled object/machine code, not variables.\"],[\"Register segment\",\"There is no register segment; the lab's three segments are code, stack and heap, and locals go on the stack.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"<code>struct P { int w; }; P item; P *ptr = &item;</code> Which statement sets w to 5 through ptr?\",\"o\":[[\"ptr.w = 5;\",\"ptr is a pointer, not an object, so ptr.w is invalid. You must dereference: ptr->w or (*ptr).w.\"],[\"*ptr.w = 5;\",\"The dot binds tighter than *, so this parses as *(ptr.w), which is invalid. Brackets are needed: (*ptr).w = 5.\"],[\"&ptr->w = 5;\",\"&ptr->w is the address of w, and an address cannot be assigned to. Setting the member is ptr->w = 5.\"],[\"ptr->w = 5;\",\"Correct. If ptr points to a structure, ptr->w is equivalent to (*ptr).w, so ptr->w = 5 sets item.w to 5 (L4, Pointer operations).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A pointer variable is:\",\"o\":[[\"A variable whose content is a memory address\",\"Correct. The lab defines a pointer variable as a variable whose content is an address (a memory address); it points to a specific data type (Lab 3, Pointers; L4).\"],[\"A variable that always holds an int value\",\"A pointer holds a memory address, not an int value; int *p only says it points to an int.\"],[\"A function that returns an address\",\"A pointer is a variable (of a pointer data type), not a function. Its content is an address.\"],[\"An array of addresses\",\"One pointer holds one address. An array of addresses would be an array of pointers.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the swap slides, x is at 0x2000 and y at 0x2010. Inside trueSwap(&x, &y), what does parameter a hold?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"1 is the value of x, which trueSwap reaches as *a. The parameter a itself holds x's address.\"],[\"0x2038\",\"0x2038 is where the variable a itself is stored, not its content. a holds the address of x.\"],[\"0x2000, the address of x\",\"Correct. The call trueSwap(&x, &y) copies the address of x into a, so a holds 0x2000 (\\\"addr of x\\\" on the slide) and *a is x itself (L4, Solution 2).\"],[\"0x2010\",\"0x2010 is the address of y, which is passed as the second argument and held by b.\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":5,\"title\":\"Linked Lists\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"What is a linked list?\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"list\",\"init\":[10,20,30],\"ops\":[\"begin 5\",\"end 40\",\"search 30\"]},\"pts\":[\"A <b>linear</b> data structure that stores a collection of elements <b>dynamically</b>. It is a series of connected <b>nodes</b>; each node stores the <b>data</b> and the <b>address of the next node</b>.\",\"Each node has two fields: <b>data</b> and a <b>pointer (next)</b> to the next node. The <b>last node contains NULL</b> in its second field because it points to no node.\",\"The list is accessed through the <b>head</b>, which points to the first node; the last node (pointing to NULL) is the <b>tail</b>. A linked list can <b>grow and shrink</b> as required.\",\"Elements are <b>scattered randomly through memory</b> (not contiguous). It is easy to add or delete an element: just change the links.\"]},{\"h\":\"Types of linked lists\",\"table\":[[\"Type\",\"Description\"],[\"Singly linked list\",\"Each node references the next node; traversal only in the forward direction\"],[\"Doubly linked list\",\"Each node references both next and previous nodes; traversal forward and backward, but needs extra memory for the backward reference\"],[\"Circular linked list\",\"The last node points back to the head node; can be singly or doubly linked\"]]},{\"h\":\"Advantages and disadvantages\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Advantages</b>: (1) <b>Dynamic size</b>: grows/shrinks since memory is allocated at run time. (2) <b>Insertion and deletion</b> are efficient, especially for large lists. (3) <b>Flexibility</b>: easily reorganized without needing a contiguous block of memory.\",\"<b>Disadvantages</b>: (1) <b>Linear access</b>: no direct access by index; traversal is required to reach a node. (2) <b>Extra memory</b> for storing the pointers.\",\"Operations: <b>insertion</b> (beginning, end, any position), <b>deletion</b> (beginning, end, any position), <b>searching</b> (traverse from head until found or end).\"]},{\"h\":\"Node and Linkedlist classes, isEmpty, isFull\",\"code\":\"class Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n   int data;\\n   Node *next;\\n   Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\n\\nclass Linkedlist\\n{\\npublic:\\n   Node *head;\\n   Linkedlist() { head = NULL; }\\n\\n   bool isEmpty()\\n   {\\n      if (head == NULL) return true;\\n      else return false;\\n   }\\n   bool isFull()          // full only if new cannot allocate memory\\n   {\\n      Node *newnode = new Node();\\n      if (newnode == NULL) return true;\\n      else return false;\\n   }\\n};\"},{\"h\":\"Insert at beginning and at end (professor's code)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"list\",\"bug\":true,\"title\":\"The slide's insertatend on an empty list\",\"ops\":[\"end! 10\"]},\"code\":\"void insertatbeginning(int item)\\n{\\n   Node *newnode = new Node();\\n   newnode->data = item;\\n   if (isEmpty())\\n   { newnode->next = NULL; head = newnode; }\\n   else\\n   { newnode->next = head;      // 1. new node points at the old first node\\n     head = newnode; }           // 2. head points at the new node\\n}\\n\\nvoid insertatend(int item)\\n{\\n   Node *newnode = new Node();\\n   newnode->data = item;\\n   newnode->next = NULL;            // new last node\\n   Node *temp1 = head;\\n   while (temp1->next != NULL) temp1 = temp1->next;   // go to last node\\n   temp1->next = newnode;           // last node now points to new node\\n}\\n// Note: as written, insertatend crashes on an EMPTY list (temp1 is NULL).\\n// Safe version: if (isEmpty()) { head = newnode; return; } before the loop.\"},{\"h\":\"Insert in order (sorted list)\",\"viz\":[{\"type\":\"list\",\"init\":[10,20,40],\"ops\":[\"sorted 30\",\"sorted 5\",\"sorted 50\"]},{\"type\":\"list\",\"bug\":true,\"title\":\"The slide's insertsorted loop: fine in the middle, crashes at the ends\",\"init\":[10,20,30],\"ops\":[\"sorted! 25\",\"sorted! 5\"]}],\"pts\":[\"Steps: (1) locate the node <b>preceding</b> the insertion point (current points to it); (2) make the new node point to the node after the insertion point (<code>newnode-&gt;next = current-&gt;next</code>); (3) make current point to the new node (<code>current-&gt;next = newnode</code>). The order of steps 2 and 3 matters: doing step 3 first loses the rest of the list.\",\"The slide's <code>insertsorted</code> loop is <code>while ((temp1-&gt;data &gt; item &amp;&amp; temp1-&gt;next-&gt;data &gt; item) || (temp1-&gt;data &lt; item &amp;&amp; temp1-&gt;next-&gt;data &lt; item) &amp;&amp; temp1!=NULL)</code>. Tested with g++: it works for a value <b>between</b> two nodes (10,20,30 + 25 → 10 20 25 30) but <b>crashes</b> for a value smaller than the head or larger than the last node (temp1-&gt;next becomes NULL).\",\"In an exam, write the safe version below: it handles the empty list, a new smallest value and a new largest value.\"]},{\"h\":\"Safe insertsorted (tested with g++)\",\"code\":\"void insertsorted(int item)            // ascending order\\n{\\n   Node *newnode = new Node();\\n   newnode->data = item;\\n   if (isEmpty() || item < head->data)   // empty list or new smallest\\n   {\\n      newnode->next = head;\\n      head = newnode;\\n   }\\n   else\\n   {\\n      Node *temp = head;\\n      while (temp->next != NULL && temp->next->data < item)\\n         temp = temp->next;                // temp = node before insertion point\\n      newnode->next = temp->next;          // step 2\\n      temp->next = newnode;                // step 3\\n   }\\n}\\n// list 10 -> 20 -> 30, insertsorted(25), (5), (40)  =>  5 10 20 25 30 40\"},{\"h\":\"Delete first, last and a given item (professor's code)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"list\",\"init\":[10,20,30,40],\"ops\":[\"delfirst\",\"delete 30\",\"dellast\"]},\"code\":\"int deletefrombeginning()\\n{\\n   int value;\\n   Node *temp = head;\\n   if (isEmpty()) { cout << \\\"Empty\\\" << endl; return 0; }\\n   else\\n   { value = head->data;\\n     head = head->next;      // head points to the second node\\n     delete(temp);\\n     return (value); }\\n}\\n\\nint deletefromend()\\n{\\n   if (isEmpty()) { cout << \\\"Empty\\\" << endl; return 0; }\\n   else\\n   { int value;\\n     Node *temp1 = head, *temp2 = NULL;\\n     while (temp1->next != NULL) { temp2 = temp1; temp1 = temp1->next; }\\n     value = temp1->data;\\n     delete temp1;\\n     temp2->next = NULL;     // (if there was only one node, set head = NULL instead)\\n     return (value); }\\n}\\n\\nvoid deleteitem(int value)\\n{\\n   if (isEmpty()) cout << \\\"empty\\\";\\n   else if (search(value))\\n   {\\n      Node *temp1 = head, *temp2 = NULL;\\n      if (head->data == value) { head = head->next; delete temp1; }\\n      else\\n      {\\n         while ((temp1->data != value) && (temp1 != NULL))\\n         { temp2 = temp1; temp1 = temp1->next; }   // temp2 = previous, temp1 = current\\n         temp2->next = temp1->next;                 // bypass the node\\n         delete(temp1);\\n      }\\n   }\\n   else cout << \\\"not found\\\";\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"search, count, get, update (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"bool search(int item)\\n{\\n   Node *temp = head;\\n   while (temp != NULL)\\n   {\\n      if (temp->data == item) return (true);\\n      else temp = temp->next;\\n   }\\n   return (false);\\n}\\nint count()                // size of the list\\n{\\n   int c = 0;\\n   Node *temp = head;\\n   while (temp != NULL) { c++; temp = temp->next; }\\n   return (c);\\n}\\nint get()                  // first element\\n{\\n   if (head != NULL) return head->data;\\n   else { cout << \\\"Empty\\\"; return 0; }\\n}\\nbool update(int old_item, int new_item)\\n{\\n   Node *temp = head;\\n   while (temp != NULL)\\n   {\\n      if (temp->data == old_item) { temp->data = new_item; return true; }\\n      else temp = temp->next;\\n   }\\n   return false;\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Lab 5: struct-based list and insert at position\",\"code\":\"struct node { int data; node *next; };\\nnode *head = NULL;\\n\\nvoid InsertB(int item)          // insert at beginning\\n{\\n   node *temp = new node;       // (1) create new node\\n   temp->data = item;           // (2) fill the data\\n   temp->next = head;           // (3) link to old list\\n   head = temp;                 // (4) move start pointer to new node\\n}\\nvoid InsertAt(int item, int pos)  // positions start at 1\\n{\\n   int curr = 1;\\n   node *temp = new node;\\n   temp->data = item; temp->next = NULL;\\n   if (head == NULL) head = temp;\\n   else if (pos == 1) { temp->next = head; head = temp; }\\n   else\\n   {\\n      node *p = head;\\n      while (curr < pos-1) { curr++; p = p->next; }   // p = node at pos-1\\n      temp->next = p->next;\\n      p->next = temp;\\n   }\\n}\\n// main: InsertB(10); InsertE(30); InsertB(5); InsertAt(20,2);  =>  5 20 10 30\"}],\"cards\":[[\"Linked list\",\"A linear structure of nodes stored dynamically; each node holds data and the address of the next node.\"],[\"Node fields\",\"data and next (pointer to the next node).\"],[\"Last node's next\",\"NULL, because it points to no node.\"],[\"head\",\"Pointer to the first node of the list.\"],[\"Singly linked list traversal\",\"Forward direction only.\"],[\"Doubly linked list\",\"Nodes have next and prev; traverse both ways; extra memory for prev.\"],[\"Circular linked list\",\"The last node points back to the head node.\"],[\"Advantages of linked lists\",\"Dynamic size, efficient insertion/deletion, flexibility (no contiguous memory).\"],[\"Disadvantages of linked lists\",\"Linear access (no index) and extra memory for pointers.\"],[\"Insert at beginning (2 steps)\",\"newnode->next = head; head = newnode;\"],[\"Insert at end\",\"Go to the last node; last->next = newnode; newnode->next = NULL.\"],[\"Delete first node\",\"temp = head; head = head->next; delete temp;\"],[\"Delete interior node\",\"previous->next = current->next; delete current;\"],[\"isFull of a linked list\",\"True only if new fails to allocate (returns NULL).\"],[\"count()\",\"Traverse from head to NULL incrementing a counter.\"],[\"Order of the two link steps when inserting after current\",\"First newnode->next = current->next, then current->next = newnode.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Define a linked list and describe its node structure.\",\"A linked list is a linear data structure that stores a collection of data elements dynamically as a series of connected nodes. Each node has a data field (the value) and a next pointer that stores the address of the next node. The head points to the first node; the last node (tail) has NULL in its next field. It can grow and shrink as required.\"],[\"List the types of linked lists.\",\"Singly linked list: each node points to the next; forward traversal only. Doubly linked list: each node points to next and previous; traversal both ways but extra memory. Circular linked list: the last node points back to the head; can be singly or doubly linked.\"],[\"State the advantages and disadvantages of linked lists.\",\"Advantages: dynamic size (allocation at run time), efficient insertion and deletion, flexibility without needing contiguous memory. Disadvantages: linear access (must traverse, no index) and extra memory for pointers.\"],[\"Explain the steps of inserting a node in its order in a linked list.\",\"1) Locate the node preceding the insertion point (current). 2) Make the new node point to the node after the insertion point: newnode->next = current->next. 3) Make current point to the new node: current->next = newnode. Special cases: empty list or a value smaller than the head, where the new node becomes the head.\"],[\"Explain how to delete an interior node.\",\"Traverse so that current points to the node to delete and previous to the node before it; get the node following current (current->next); make previous point to it (previous->next = current->next); then delete current.\"],[\"Write node class, linked list class and insert-at-end, then build the list head → 7 → 5 → NULL (Midterm 2023/24).\",\"class Node{ public: int data; Node *next; Node(){ data=0; next=NULL; } };<br>class Linkedlist{ public: Node *head; Linkedlist(){ head=NULL; } bool isEmpty(){ return head==NULL; }<br>void insertatend(int item){ Node *newnode=new Node(); newnode->data=item; newnode->next=NULL; if(isEmpty()){ head=newnode; return; } Node *temp1=head; while(temp1->next!=NULL) temp1=temp1->next; temp1->next=newnode; } };<br>main: Linkedlist list; list.insertatend(7); list.insertatend(5);\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"The last node in a linked list contains null in its second field because it will point to no node.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Each node has two fields, data and next; the last node has no node after it, so its next field holds NULL, which marks the end of the list (the tail) (L5, What is a linked list?).\"],[\"False\",\"The statement is true: the last (tail) node has nothing to point to, so its next pointer is NULL; traversals stop when they reach it.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Traversing a singly linked list is done in a forward direction only.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. In a singly linked list each node holds a reference only to the next node, so traversal goes forward from head; going backward needs the prev pointer of a doubly linked list (L5, Singly linked list).\"],[\"False\",\"Backward traversal needs prev pointers (doubly linked list).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"In a circular linked list, the last node points back to the head node.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. In a circular linked list the last node points back to the head node instead of NULL, forming a circle; it can be singly or doubly linked (L5, Circular linked list).\"],[\"False\",\"That is the definition of a circular linked list.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"The following is a valid code of class Node of a singly linked list: class Node {int data, Node next;}\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The code is not valid: next must be a pointer (Node *next;), members are separated by ; not a comma, and the class needs a closing ; (and public:).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. next must be a pointer (Node *next;), the members must be separated by ; and the class needs a closing ; (and public: to be used outside).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"The disadvantage of a linked list is ______.\",\"o\":[[\"Fixed size\",\"A fixed size is the array's disadvantage. A linked list grows and shrinks at run time.\"],[\"Linear access and extra memory\",\"Correct (official model answer B). The lecture lists two disadvantages: linear access (no direct access by index, traversal is needed) and extra memory for storing the pointers (L5, Advantages and disadvantages).\"],[\"Slow insertion at the beginning\",\"Inserting at the beginning is fast: newnode->next = head; head = newnode; no traversal or shifting is needed.\"],[\"It cannot grow\",\"A linked list can grow and shrink as required, since nodes are allocated at run time with new. Dynamic size is its first advantage.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2023/24\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement pair inserts newnode at the beginning of a non-empty list?\",\"o\":[[\"head = newnode; newnode->next = head;\",\"Wrong order: newnode->next would point to itself and the list is lost.\"],[\"newnode->next = head; head = newnode;\",\"Correct. Link to the old first node, then move head.\"],[\"newnode->next = NULL; head = newnode;\",\"Setting newnode->next = NULL cuts the new node off from the old nodes, so after head = newnode the rest of the list is lost. That is only right for an empty list.\"],[\"head->next = newnode;\",\"This puts the new node second and loses the old second node.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"After Lab 5's main: InsertB(10); InsertE(30); InsertB(5); InsertAt(20,2); the list is:\",\"o\":[[\"5 20 10 30\",\"Correct. 10 → 10,30 → 5,10,30 → insert 20 at position 2 → 5,20,10,30.\"],[\"5 10 20 30\",\"InsertAt does not sort; it inserts at position 2.\"],[\"10 20 30 5\",\"InsertB(5) inserts at the beginning, so 5 becomes the first node, not the last. Then InsertAt(20, 2) puts 20 second: 5 20 10 30.\"],[\"20 5 10 30\",\"20 first would be InsertAt(20, 1). With pos = 2 the loop stops at node 1 (5) and links 20 after it: 5 20 10 30.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Lecture 5 example\"},{\"q\":\"What does count() return for the list head → 4 → 9 → 2 → NULL?\",\"o\":[[\"2\",\"2 is the data of the last node, not the number of nodes. count() increments c once per node: 3.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. count() starts temp at head and does c++ for each node until temp becomes NULL: 4, 9, 2 gives c = 3 (L5, Find the size of the list).\"],[\"4\",\"4 counts NULL as a node. The loop stops when temp == NULL, so only the three real nodes are counted.\"],[\"15\",\"15 = 4 + 9 + 2 is the sum of the data. count() adds 1 per node, not the node's value.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which operation is NOT efficient in a singly linked list compared with an array?\",\"o\":[[\"Inserting at the beginning\",\"Inserting at the beginning is cheap in a linked list (two pointer changes), while an array would have to shift every element.\"],[\"Accessing the 500th element directly\",\"Correct. Linked lists have linear access; they must traverse.\"],[\"Deleting the first node\",\"Deleting the first node is cheap: head = head->next; delete temp. An array would have to shift all remaining elements left.\"],[\"Growing beyond the initial size\",\"Growing is a linked list strength: memory is allocated at run time, while an array's size is fixed when it is declared.\"]],\"a\":1}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"Starting from an empty list: insertatbeginning(3); insertatbeginning(7); insertatend(9); deletefrombeginning(). The list is:\",\"o\":[[\"7 3\",\"9 was added at the end and 7 was deleted from the beginning.\"],[\"3 9\",\"Correct. 3 → insertatbeginning(7) gives 7, 3 → insertatend(9) gives 7, 3, 9 → deletefrombeginning removes the head (7), leaving 3, 9 (L5, Insertion and deletion).\"],[\"7 3 9\",\"7 3 9 is the list before the last call. deletefrombeginning moves head to the second node and deletes 7.\"],[\"3 7 9\",\"insertatbeginning(7) puts 7 before 3 (newnode->next = head), so 7 was the first node, and it is the one deleted.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What happens if the lecture's insertatend is called on an EMPTY list?\",\"o\":[[\"It inserts the first node normally\",\"temp1 = head is NULL, so temp1->next crashes.\"],[\"It returns false\",\"insertatend is a void function; it returns nothing. On an empty list it dereferences a NULL head and crashes.\"],[\"It crashes because temp1 is NULL when temp1->next is read\",\"Correct. Add an isEmpty() check that sets head = newnode.\"],[\"It inserts at the beginning\",\"insertatend has no isEmpty branch (unlike insertatbeginning), so it never falls back to inserting at the beginning; it reads NULL->next.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"To insert newnode after node current, which order is correct?\",\"o\":[[\"current->next = newnode; newnode->next = current->next;\",\"newnode->next would point to itself and the rest of the list is lost.\"],[\"newnode->next = current; current->next = newnode;\",\"newnode->next = current makes the new node point back to current, creating a loop, and the nodes after current are lost.\"],[\"newnode->next = current->next; current->next = newnode;\",\"Correct. Link the new node to the following node first.\"],[\"head = newnode; newnode->next = current;\",\"This moves head to the new node, making it the first node; it does not insert after current, and the nodes before current are lost.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Why does isFull() of a linked list create a new node?\",\"o\":[[\"To test whether memory can still be allocated; the list is full only if new returns NULL\",\"Correct. A linked list has no fixed size, so isFull() tries new Node(); if new returns NULL there is no memory left and the list is \\\"full\\\" (L5, The linked-list class).\"],[\"To count the nodes\",\"Counting nodes is done by count(), which walks the list; isFull() only tries to allocate memory.\"],[\"To insert a dummy node\",\"The test node is never linked into the list; isFull() only checks whether new returned NULL.\"],[\"Because the list has a fixed size\",\"A linked list has no fixed size; it grows while memory is available. That is why isFull() tests whether new can still allocate.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In deleteitem, temp2 and temp1 are used as:\",\"o\":[[\"head and tail\",\"They are not head and tail: both start at the front and move along the list while searching for the value.\"],[\"previous node and current node\",\"Correct. temp2 trails one node behind temp1 so it can be relinked.\"],[\"old and new values\",\"deleteitem moves pointers to nodes, not values. Replacing an old value with a new one is update(old_item, new_item).\"],[\"two different lists\",\"Both pointers walk the same list; temp2 just trails one node behind temp1 so temp2->next can skip the deleted node.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"update(4, 10) on the list 2 → 4 → 6 → 4 changes the list to:\",\"o\":[[\"2 → 10 → 6 → 10\",\"update returns true right after changing the first match, so the second 4 is never reached.\"],[\"2 → 4 → 6 → 10\",\"update scans from head and stops at the first match, so the first 4 (second node) changes, not the last one.\"],[\"10 → 4 → 6 → 4\",\"The first node holds 2, which is not old_item (4), so it is skipped; the first 4 is the second node.\"],[\"2 → 10 → 6 → 4\",\"Correct. Only the first node with 4 is updated.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT an advantage of linked lists?\",\"o\":[[\"Dynamic size\",\"Dynamic size is the lecture's first advantage: a linked list grows or shrinks at run time.\"],[\"Direct access to any element by index\",\"Correct. Linked lists have linear access; this is an array advantage.\"],[\"Efficient insertion and deletion\",\"Efficient insertion and deletion is a listed advantage: only pointers change, nothing is shifted.\"],[\"No need for a contiguous block of memory\",\"Not needing a contiguous block of memory is the flexibility advantage: nodes can be scattered in memory.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In deletefromend, which node does temp2 point to when the loop ends?\",\"o\":[[\"The head\",\"temp2 ends at the node before the last. That is the head only when the list has exactly two nodes.\"],[\"The last node\",\"temp1 is the one that stops at the last node (temp1->next == NULL); temp2 trails one node behind it.\"],[\"NULL always\",\"temp2 stays NULL only for a one-node list (and then temp2->next = NULL would crash). Otherwise it is the second-to-last node.\"],[\"The node before the last node\",\"Correct. Then temp2->next = NULL makes it the new last node.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Lab 5 InsertAt(item, pos) with list 5 → 10 → 30 and InsertAt(20, 3) gives:\",\"o\":[[\"5 → 10 → 20 → 30\",\"Correct. p moves to position 2 (node 10), then 20 is linked after it.\"],[\"5 → 20 → 10 → 30\",\"5 → 20 → 10 → 30 is the result of InsertAt(20, 2). With pos = 3, p moves to node 2 (10) and 20 goes after it.\"],[\"20 → 5 → 10 → 30\",\"20 at the front is the pos == 1 case (temp->next = head; head = temp). Here pos = 3.\"],[\"5 → 10 → 30 → 20\",\"Putting 20 after 30 is position 4. With pos = 3 the loop stops at the 2nd node (10) and 20 is linked after it.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"How does search(item) end when the item is not in the list?\",\"o\":[[\"It crashes at the last node\",\"It does not crash: the loop condition is while (temp != NULL), so it stops safely after the last node.\"],[\"It returns true\",\"search returns true only when temp->data == item. If no node matches, the loop ends and it returns false.\"],[\"It returns false after temp becomes NULL\",\"Correct. search walks temp = temp->next from head; if no node's data equals item, temp becomes NULL, the while loop stops and the function returns false (L5, search).\"],[\"It returns the last node\",\"search returns a bool (true/false), not a node pointer; when the item is missing it returns false.\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":6,\"title\":\"Linked Stacks\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Stack revised and the linked stack\",\"pts\":[\"A stack is a linear data structure that follows <b>Last In First Out</b> at one point (<b>top</b>). Two implementations: <b>static</b> (array) and <b>dynamic</b> (linked list).\",\"Major problem of the array stack: it works only for a <b>fixed number</b> of values, which must be specified at the beginning.\",\"The linked stack works for an <b>unlimited number</b> of values (variable size); no need to fix the size at the beginning.\",\"Every new element is inserted as the <b>top</b> element (pointed to by top). To remove, delete the node pointed to by top and <b>move top to its next node</b>.\",\"Figure: elements inserted in order 25, 32, 50, 99 → top → 99 → 50 → 32 → 25 → NULL. Last inserted = 99, first inserted = 25.\",\"Only <b>one pointer (top)</b> and one list are needed; push = insert at beginning, pop = delete from beginning.\"]},{\"h\":\"Creation, push and pop steps\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lstack\",\"ops\":[\"push 50\",\"push 70\",\"push 90\",\"pop\"]},\"pts\":[\"<b>Creation</b>: Step 1: define a Node class with members data and next. Step 2: define a Node pointer <b>top = NULL</b>. Step 3: main displays a menu and calls the functions.\",\"<b>Push</b>: (1) create newNode with the value; (2) check whether the stack is empty (top == NULL); (3) if empty, newNode→next = NULL; (4) if not empty, newNode→next = top; (5) finally top = newNode.\",\"<b>Pop</b>: (1) check whether empty (top == NULL); (2) if empty, terminate; (3) if not, define Node pointer temp = top; (4) top = top→next; (5) delete temp.\",\"<b>Display</b>: if empty print \\\"Stack is Empty\\\"; else temp = top, print temp→data and move to next until the first-inserted node; finally print \\\"--->NULL\\\".\"]},{\"h\":\"Linked stack class (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"class Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n   int data;\\n   Node *next;\\n   Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Stack\\n{\\n   Node *top;\\npublic:\\n   Stack() { top = NULL; }\\n\\n   bool isEmpty() { if (top == NULL) return true; else return false; }\\n   bool isFull()\\n   { Node *newnode = new Node();\\n     if (newnode == NULL) return true; else return false; }\\n\\n   void push(int item)\\n   {\\n      Node *newnode = new Node();\\n      newnode->data = item;\\n      if (isEmpty()) { newnode->next = NULL; top = newnode; }\\n      else           { newnode->next = top;  top = newnode; }\\n   }\\n   int pop()\\n   {\\n      int value;\\n      Node *temp = top;\\n      if (isEmpty()) { cout << \\\"Empty\\\" << endl; return 0; }\\n      else\\n      { value = top->data;\\n        top = top->next;\\n        delete(temp);\\n        return (value); }\\n   }\\n};\"},{\"h\":\"display, get, count, search (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"void display()\\n{\\n   if (isEmpty()) cout << \\\"Empty Stack\\\";\\n   else\\n   { Node *temp = top;\\n     while (temp != NULL) { cout << temp->data << \\\" \\\"; temp = temp->next; }\\n     cout << endl << \\\"END\\\" << endl; }\\n}\\nint get()            // top element without removing it\\n{\\n   if (top != NULL) return top->data;\\n   else { cout << \\\"Empty\\\"; return 0; }\\n}\\nint count()\\n{\\n   int c = 0; Node *temp = top;\\n   while (temp != NULL) { c++; temp = temp->next; }\\n   return (c);\\n}\\nbool search(int item)\\n{\\n   Node *temp = top;\\n   while (temp != NULL)\\n   { if (temp->data == item) return (true); else temp = temp->next; }\\n   return (false);\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Exam pattern: push 50 and 70, pop one, remove all (tested)\",\"code\":\"void removeAll()          // pop until empty\\n{\\n   while (!isEmpty())\\n      cout << \\\"popped \\\" << pop() << endl;\\n}\\nint main()\\n{\\n   Stack s;\\n   if (s.isEmpty()) cout << \\\"Stack is empty\\\" << endl;\\n   s.push(50);\\n   s.push(70);                          // top -> 70 -> 50 -> NULL\\n   cout << \\\"Removed \\\" << s.pop() << endl;   // Removed 70\\n   s.removeAll();                       // popped 50\\n   return 0;\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Lab 7: struct-based linked stack\",\"code\":\"struct node { int data; node *next; };\\nnode *top = NULL;\\nvoid push(int item)          // insert at beginning\\n{\\n   node *newnode = new node;\\n   newnode->data = item;\\n   newnode->next = top;\\n   top = newnode;\\n}\\nvoid pop()                   // delete from beginning\\n{\\n   node *delnode = top;\\n   if (top == NULL) cout << \\\"Stack Underflow\\\\n\\\";\\n   else\\n   { cout << \\\"The popped element is \\\" << top->data << endl;\\n     top = top->next;\\n     delete delnode; }\\n}\"}],\"cards\":[[\"Linked stack\",\"A stack implemented with a linked list; every new node becomes the top.\"],[\"Problem solved by the linked stack\",\"The array stack works only for a fixed number of values.\"],[\"Initial top of a linked stack\",\"top = NULL\"],[\"Linked stack empty condition\",\"top == NULL\"],[\"Push (linked)\",\"newnode->next = top; top = newnode;\"],[\"Pop (linked)\",\"temp = top; top = top->next; delete temp;\"],[\"Push = which list operation?\",\"Insert at the beginning.\"],[\"Pop = which list operation?\",\"Delete from the beginning.\"],[\"Pointers needed by a linked stack\",\"One: top.\"],[\"Inserted 25, 32, 50, 99: display order?\",\"99 50 32 25\"],[\"Can a linked stack overflow?\",\"Only if new fails to allocate memory (isFull).\"],[\"Steps of linked stack creation\",\"Define Node (data, next); define top = NULL; main menu calls functions.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Why is a linked stack better than an array stack?\",\"The array stack works only for a fixed number of values that must be specified at the start. A linked stack works for an unlimited, variable number of values, so there is no need to fix the size; memory is allocated per node at run time.\"],[\"List the steps of push in a linked stack.\",\"1) Create newNode with the given value. 2) Check whether the stack is empty (top == NULL). 3) If empty, set newNode->next = NULL. 4) If not empty, set newNode->next = top. 5) Set top = newNode.\"],[\"List the steps of pop in a linked stack.\",\"1) Check whether the stack is empty (top == NULL). 2) If empty, terminate the function. 3) If not, define Node pointer temp and set it to top. 4) Set top = top->next. 5) Delete temp.\"],[\"Write a node class and a linked stack class with a function to delete an item from the stack, and main to call it (Exam 2025/26).\",\"class Node{ public: int data; Node *next; Node(){ data=0; next=NULL; } };<br>class Stack{ Node *top; public: Stack(){ top=NULL; } bool isEmpty(){ return top==NULL; } void push(int item){ Node *n=new Node(); n->data=item; n->next=top; top=n; } int pop(){ if(isEmpty()){ cout&lt;&lt;\\\"Empty\\\"; return 0; } Node *temp=top; int value=top->data; top=top->next; delete temp; return value; } };<br>int main(){ Stack s; s.push(10); s.push(20); cout&lt;&lt;s.pop(); }  // prints 20\"],[\"Write a function to remove all the items from a linked stack.\",\"void removeAll(){ while(top != NULL){ Node *temp = top; top = top->next; delete temp; } }  (or: while(!isEmpty()) pop();)\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Two linked lists are needed in order to implement a linked stack.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"One list with one pointer (top) is enough.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. A linked stack is one singly linked list where push/pop happen at the top (the beginning).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"In a linked stack, which pointer is changed by push?\",\"o\":[[\"front\",\"front is a queue pointer (deletions happen there). A linked stack has only one pointer, top, and push moves it.\"],[\"rear\",\"rear is the queue's insertion pointer. A linked stack inserts at the top: newnode->next = top; top = newnode.\"],[\"top\",\"Correct. top = newnode after linking newnode->next = top.\"],[\"head->prev\",\"The lecture's linked stack is singly linked (Node has only data and next); there is no prev pointer to change.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Elements 25, 32, 50, 99 are pushed into a linked stack in this order. display() prints:\",\"o\":[[\"25 32 50 99\",\"That is insertion order (a queue would print this).\"],[\"99 50 32 25\",\"Correct. Display starts at top, the last pushed node.\"],[\"99 25 32 50\",\"display() walks temp = temp->next from top without jumping, so the order is strictly reverse of pushing: 99 50 32 25.\"],[\"25 99 50 32\",\"Nodes are linked top to bottom in reverse push order, and display() follows next one by one, so 25 can only come last.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Lecture 6 example\"},{\"q\":\"What is the linked stack empty condition?\",\"o\":[[\"top == -1\",\"top == −1 is the array stack's test, where top is an index. In a linked stack top is a pointer, so empty means top == NULL.\"],[\"top == NULL\",\"Correct. The linked stack sets top = NULL at creation and isEmpty() returns true when top == NULL: there is no node to point to (L6, Pop from linked stack).\"],[\"top->next == NULL\",\"top->next == NULL means the top node is the only node: exactly one element, not an empty stack.\"],[\"front == rear\",\"front and rear belong to a queue. A linked stack has just top, and it is empty when top == NULL.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The major problem of the stack implemented using an array is:\",\"o\":[[\"It cannot be traversed\",\"An array stack can be traversed: display() loops j = top down to 0. Its problem is the fixed size.\"],[\"It works only for a fixed number of data values\",\"Correct. The lecture says the major problem of the array stack is that it works only for a fixed number of values, set at the start; the linked stack works for an unlimited number (L6, Stack revised).\"],[\"It follows FIFO\",\"Both stack implementations follow LIFO; FIFO is the queue rule, so this is not a problem of the array stack.\"],[\"It needs two pointers\",\"The array stack needs only one index, top. Two pointers (front and rear) belong to a queue.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the linked stack pop, what is the correct order of statements after temp = top?\",\"o\":[[\"delete temp; top = top->next;\",\"Deleting temp first frees the node top still points to, so top->next would then read freed memory.\"],[\"top = top->next; delete temp;\",\"Correct. Move top first, then free the old top through temp.\"],[\"top = NULL; delete temp;\",\"top = NULL drops every node below the old top, so the rest of the stack is lost (and leaks). top must move to top->next.\"],[\"temp = temp->next; delete top;\",\"This frees the node top still points to and leaves top dangling; the lecture moves top = top->next and then deletes temp.\"]],\"a\":1}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"Linked stack: push(1), push(2), push(3), pop(), push(4). display() prints:\",\"o\":[[\"1 2 4\",\"1 2 4 is bottom-to-top order. display() starts at top (the last pushed node, 4) and follows next down to 1.\"],[\"4 2 1\",\"Correct. Stack after the pushes: 3 2 1 (top first). pop() removes 3, push(4) makes 4 the top, and display() prints from top: 4 2 1 (L6, Display).\"],[\"4 3 2 1\",\"3 was removed by pop() (top = top->next; delete temp), so it is not printed.\"],[\"3 2 1 4\",\"4 was pushed last, so it is the top and is printed first; and 3 was already popped.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What does the lecture's pop() return when the linked stack is empty?\",\"o\":[[\"-1\",\"The array stack returns -1; the linked stack returns 0.\"],[\"NULL\",\"pop() returns an int value, not a pointer; on an empty stack the lecture code prints \\\"Empty\\\" and returns 0.\"],[\"0 after printing \\\"Empty\\\"\",\"Correct. If isEmpty() is true, the lecture's pop() prints \\\"Empty\\\", keeps top = NULL and returns 0 (the array stack returned −1) (L6, Pop from linked stack).\"],[\"The last popped value\",\"The linked stack keeps no record of earlier pops; on an empty stack pop() just prints \\\"Empty\\\" and returns 0.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the push steps, what is set when the stack is empty (step 3)?\",\"o\":[[\"newNode->next = NULL\",\"Correct. Step 3: if the stack is empty (top == NULL), set newNode->next = NULL, since the new node will be the only one; step 5 then sets top = newNode (L6, Push).\"],[\"newNode->next = top->next\",\"When the stack is empty top is NULL, so reading top->next would crash. The empty case sets newNode->next = NULL.\"],[\"top->next = newNode\",\"top is NULL when the stack is empty, so top->next cannot be assigned; besides, push never links top to the new node this way.\"],[\"top = NULL\",\"top is already NULL when empty; step 5 sets top = newNode, not NULL. Step 3 sets newNode->next = NULL.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Linked stack: push(10), push(20), push(30). What does count() return after pop()?\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"3 is the count before pop(). pop() removes the top node (30), so count() then walks two nodes.\"],[\"1\",\"Only one pop() was done, removing 30; nodes 20 and 10 remain, so count() returns 2.\"],[\"0\",\"0 would mean an empty stack (top == NULL). After one pop, 20 and 10 are still linked.\"],[\"2\",\"Correct. Three pushes give 30 → 20 → 10; pop() removes 30, and count() does c++ for each remaining node until NULL: 2 (L6, Counting elements).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"search(25) on the linked stack 99 → 50 → 32 → 25 → NULL visits how many nodes?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"1 visit would find only the top, 99. search starts at top, and 25 is the bottom node, the 4th.\"],[\"4\",\"Correct. It starts at top (99) and finds 25 at the 4th node.\"],[\"3\",\"3 visits reach 99, 50, 32 only. 25 is the 4th node, so it is found on the 4th visit.\"],[\"5\",\"There are only four nodes; the search returns true at the 4th (25) and never moves on to NULL.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which statement about the linked stack in the lecture is true?\",\"o\":[[\"It needs MAXSIZE to be declared first\",\"MAXSIZE belongs to the array stack. The linked stack works for an unlimited number of values, with no fixed size.\"],[\"New nodes are added at the end of the list\",\"New nodes are inserted as the top, at the beginning of the list (newnode->next = top), not at the end.\"],[\"Every newly inserted element is pointed to by top\",\"Correct. The lecture says every new element is inserted as the top element, so every newly inserted element is pointed to by top; pop removes the node top points to (L6, Linked stack).\"],[\"It uses front and rear pointers\",\"front and rear are a queue's pointers. The linked stack uses a single pointer, top.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which code removes all the items of a linked stack?\",\"o\":[[\"top = NULL;\",\"This loses the nodes without freeing them (memory leak).\"],[\"while (top != NULL) { Node *t = top; top = top->next; delete t; }\",\"Correct. Each pass saves the top node in t, moves top to the next node and frees t with delete, until top is NULL; every node is released and the stack ends empty (same pattern as pop, L6).\"],[\"delete top;\",\"delete top frees only the top node and leaves top pointing at freed memory; the nodes below are never freed.\"],[\"while (top != NULL) top = top->next;\",\"This walks top down to NULL without delete, so the stack looks empty but every node stays allocated (memory leak).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What does get() do in a linked stack?\",\"o\":[[\"Removes and returns the top\",\"Removing and returning the top is pop(). get() only reads top->data and leaves the stack unchanged.\"],[\"Returns the bottom element\",\"get() reads top->data, the last pushed element, not the bottom one.\"],[\"Returns the number of nodes\",\"Counting the nodes is count(). get() returns the data of the top node.\"],[\"Returns the top element without removing it\",\"Correct. get() returns top->data if top != NULL (otherwise prints \\\"Empty\\\" and returns 0); it does not move top or delete anything (L6, Get the first node).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In Lab 7, push(item) is described as:\",\"o\":[[\"Insert to beginning\",\"Correct. The new node becomes the first node (top).\"],[\"Insert to end\",\"Inserting at the end is how a linked queue's enqueue works (at rear). A stack adds at the top, the beginning of the list.\"],[\"Insert in order\",\"A stack never orders its elements; push always puts the new node first, whatever its value.\"],[\"Insert at position 2\",\"push always inserts at the top (first position), never at a fixed position like 2.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Elements are pushed in the order 25, 32, 50, 99. Which node's next is NULL?\",\"o\":[[\"99\",\"99 was pushed last, so it is the top; its next points to 50, the node below it.\"],[\"50\",\"50 is in the middle; its next points to 32, the node pushed before it.\"],[\"25\",\"Correct. The first pushed node is at the bottom and points to NULL.\"],[\"32\",\"32's next points to 25, the first pushed node, which is the bottom and the one with next = NULL.\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":7,\"title\":\"Linked Queues\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Queue revised and the linked queue\",\"pts\":[\"A queue follows <b>First In First Out</b> at <b>two points</b>: <b>tail (rear) for insert</b>, <b>head (front) for retrieve</b>. Two implementations: static (array) and dynamic (linked list).\",\"The array queue works only for a <b>fixed number</b> of values. The linked queue works for an <b>unlimited (variable)</b> number; no fixed size.\",\"The <b>last inserted node is always pointed to by rear</b> and the <b>first node by front</b>.\",\"Figure: inserted 10, 15, 22, 50 → front → 10 → 15 → 22 → 50 ← rear, 50 → NULL.\",\"<b>Creation</b>: (1) define Node class with data and next; (2) define two Node pointers <b>front and rear, both NULL</b>; (3) main shows a menu and calls the functions.\"]},{\"h\":\"Enqueue, dequeue and display steps\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lqueue\",\"ops\":[\"enqueue 30\",\"enqueue 50\",\"enqueue 70\",\"dequeue\"]},\"pts\":[\"<b>Enqueue</b>: (1) create newNode with the value and set newNode→next = NULL; (2) check whether the queue is empty (rear == NULL); (3) if empty, set <b>front = newNode and rear = newNode</b>; (4) if not, set <b>rear→next = newNode and rear = newNode</b>.\",\"<b>Dequeue</b>: (1) check empty (front == NULL); (2) if empty display \\\"Queue is Empty!!! Deletion is not possible\\\" and terminate; (3) else temp = front; (4) front = front→next and delete temp.\",\"<b>Display</b>: if empty display \\\"Queue is Empty\\\"; else temp = front; display temp→data and move to the next node until temp reaches rear; finally display \\\"temp→data ---&gt; NULL\\\".\",\"Insertion is at the <b>rear (end)</b> of the list; deletion is from the <b>front (beginning)</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Linked queue class (professor's code)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lqueue\",\"bug\":true,\"title\":\"The lecture's dequeue, removing the last node\",\"ops\":[\"enqueue 30\",\"dequeue!\",\"enqueue 50\"]},\"code\":\"class Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n   int data;\\n   Node *next;\\n   Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Queue\\n{\\n   Node *front;\\n   Node *rear;\\npublic:\\n   Queue() { front = NULL; rear = NULL; }\\n\\n   bool isEmpty()\\n   { if (front == NULL && rear == NULL) return true; else return false; }\\n\\n   void enqueue(int item)\\n   {\\n      Node *newnode = new Node();\\n      newnode->data = item;\\n      newnode->next = NULL;\\n      if (isEmpty()) { front = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n      else           { rear->next = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n   }\\n   int dequeue()\\n   {\\n      int value;\\n      Node *temp = front;\\n      if (isEmpty()) { cout << \\\"Empty\\\" << endl; return 0; }\\n      else\\n      { value = front->data;\\n        front = front->next;\\n        delete(temp);\\n        return (value); }\\n   }\\n};\\n// Careful: after removing the LAST node front becomes NULL but rear still\\n// points to the deleted node, so isEmpty() (front==NULL && rear==NULL) is false.\\n// Add: if (front == NULL) rear = NULL;   (Lab 7 does front = rear = NULL.)\"},{\"h\":\"display, get, count, search (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"void display()\\n{\\n   Node *temp = front;\\n   while (temp != NULL) { cout << temp->data << \\\" \\\"; temp = temp->next; }\\n   cout << endl << \\\"END\\\" << endl;\\n}\\nint get()             // first node (head)\\n{\\n   if (front != NULL) return front->data;\\n   else { cout << \\\"Empty\\\"; return 0; }\\n}\\nint count()\\n{\\n   int c = 0; Node *temp = front;\\n   while (temp != NULL) { c++; temp = temp->next; }\\n   return (c);\\n}\\nbool search(int item)\\n{\\n   Node *temp = front;\\n   while (temp != NULL)\\n   { if (temp->data == item) return (true); else temp = temp->next; }\\n   return (false);\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Exam pattern: insert 30 and 50, remove all items (tested)\",\"code\":\"// safe dequeue + removeAll inside class Queue\\nint dequeue()\\n{\\n   if (isEmpty()) { cout << \\\"Queue is empty\\\" << endl; return 0; }\\n   Node *temp = front;\\n   int value = front->data;\\n   front = front->next;\\n   if (front == NULL) rear = NULL;   // queue became empty\\n   delete temp;\\n   return value;\\n}\\nvoid removeAll()\\n{\\n   while (!isEmpty())\\n      cout << \\\"dequeued \\\" << dequeue() << endl;\\n}\\nint main()\\n{\\n   Queue q;\\n   if (q.isEmpty()) cout << \\\"Queue is empty\\\" << endl;\\n   q.enqueue(30);\\n   q.enqueue(50);        // front -> 30 -> 50 <- rear\\n   q.removeAll();        // dequeued 30, dequeued 50\\n   return 0;\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Lab 7: struct-based linked queue dequeue\",\"code\":\"node *front = NULL;\\nnode *rear = NULL;\\nvoid dequeue()                          // remove from beginning\\n{\\n   if (front == NULL && rear == NULL) cout << \\\"Queue Underflow\\\\n\\\";\\n   else\\n   {\\n      node *temp;\\n      if (front->next != NULL)          // more than 1 item\\n      { temp = front; front = front->next;\\n        cout << \\\"The deleted element is \\\" << temp->data << endl;\\n        delete (temp); }\\n      else                              // only one item\\n      { cout << \\\"The deleted element is \\\" << front->data << endl;\\n        front = rear = NULL; }\\n   }\\n}\"}],\"cards\":[[\"Linked queue\",\"A queue implemented with a linked list; insert at rear, delete at front.\"],[\"Pointers of a linked queue\",\"front (head) and rear (tail), both NULL initially.\"],[\"Enqueue (non-empty)\",\"rear->next = newnode; rear = newnode;\"],[\"Enqueue (empty)\",\"front = newnode; rear = newnode;\"],[\"Dequeue\",\"temp = front; front = front->next; delete temp;\"],[\"Linked queue empty condition\",\"front == NULL (lecture: front == NULL && rear == NULL)\"],[\"Inserted 10, 15, 22, 50: which node is front?\",\"10 (rear points to 50).\"],[\"Where is insertion done in a linked queue?\",\"At the rear (end of the list).\"],[\"What must happen when the last node is dequeued?\",\"Set rear = NULL too (front = rear = NULL).\"],[\"Advantage over the array queue\",\"No fixed size; works for an unlimited number of values.\"],[\"Display order of a linked queue\",\"From front to rear (insertion order).\"],[\"newnode->next in enqueue\",\"NULL, since the new node becomes the last node.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Explain the enqueue steps in a linked queue.\",\"1) Create newNode with the value and set newNode->next = NULL. 2) Check whether the queue is empty (rear == NULL). 3) If empty, set front = newNode and rear = newNode. 4) If not empty, set rear->next = newNode and rear = newNode.\"],[\"Explain the dequeue steps in a linked queue.\",\"1) Check whether the queue is empty (front == NULL). 2) If empty, display \\\"Queue is Empty!!! Deletion is not possible\\\" and terminate. 3) If not, define temp = front. 4) Set front = front->next and delete temp (and if front became NULL, set rear = NULL).\"],[\"Write a node class, a linked queue class and a function to insert 25 and 20, with main (Exam 2025/26).\",\"class Node{ public: int data; Node *next; Node(){ data=0; next=NULL; } };<br>class Queue{ Node *front, *rear; public: Queue(){ front=rear=NULL; } bool isEmpty(){ return front==NULL; } void enqueue(int item){ Node *n=new Node(); n->data=item; n->next=NULL; if(isEmpty()){ front=rear=n; } else { rear->next=n; rear=n; } } };<br>int main(){ Queue q; q.enqueue(25); q.enqueue(20); }  // front -> 25 -> 20 &lt;- rear\"],[\"Compare a linked stack and a linked queue.\",\"Linked stack: one pointer (top); push and pop both at the beginning; LIFO. Linked queue: two pointers (front and rear); enqueue at the rear (end), dequeue at the front (beginning); FIFO. Both have no fixed size.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Insertion is done at the front of a linked queue.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The statement is false: enqueue links the new node after rear (rear->next = newnode; rear = newnode). The front is where dequeue deletes.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Enqueue inserts at the rear; deletion is at the front.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"In a linked queue the last inserted node is always pointed to by:\",\"o\":[[\"front\",\"front (head) points to the first inserted node, the next one to be dequeued, not the last inserted.\"],[\"top\",\"top is the only pointer of a linked stack. A linked queue has front and rear, and the newest node is at rear.\"],[\"rear\",\"Correct. The lecture says the last inserted node is always pointed to by tail or rear, and the first node by head or front (L7, Linked queue).\"],[\"head\",\"head is another name for front, which points to the first inserted node; the last inserted is at rear (tail).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which statements enqueue newnode into a NON-empty linked queue?\",\"o\":[[\"newnode->next = front; front = newnode;\",\"Linking the new node before front and moving front inserts at the beginning, which is a stack push, not enqueue.\"],[\"rear->next = newnode; rear = newnode;\",\"Correct. Step 4 of enqueue: if the queue is not empty, link the old last node to the new one (rear->next = newnode) and then move rear to it (rear = newnode) (L7, Enqueue).\"],[\"rear = newnode; rear->next = newnode;\",\"Wrong order: the old last node is never linked and newnode points to itself.\"],[\"front->next = newnode;\",\"This links the new node right after the first node and drops the link to the old second node, so nodes are lost; rear is never updated.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Enqueue 10, 15, 22, 50; dequeue twice; enqueue 7. Display prints:\",\"o\":[[\"22 50 7\",\"Correct. 10 and 15 leave first (FIFO), 7 joins at the rear.\"],[\"7 22 50\",\"enqueue adds at the rear, so 7 comes after 22 and 50, not before them.\"],[\"10 15 7\",\"Dequeue removes from the front, i.e. 10 and 15.\"],[\"50 22 7\",\"A queue keeps insertion order from front to rear: 22 then 50 then 7. Reversed order is how a stack displays.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"How many pointers does a linked queue keep?\",\"o\":[[\"One (top)\",\"A single top pointer is the linked stack. A queue works at two ends, so it needs front and rear.\"],[\"Two (front and rear)\",\"Correct. Step 2 of creating a linked queue defines two Node pointers, front and rear, both set to NULL: front for dequeue and rear for enqueue (L7, Linked queue creation).\"],[\"Three (front, rear, top)\",\"There is no top in a queue; top belongs to a stack. The linked queue has only front and rear.\"],[\"None\",\"A linked structure is reached only through pointers; the queue keeps two, front and rear (both NULL when empty).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the lecture's dequeue, after the only node is deleted, what goes wrong with isEmpty() (front==NULL && rear==NULL)?\",\"o\":[[\"Nothing, it returns true\",\"rear was not changed, so it still points to the deleted node.\"],[\"It returns false because rear still points to the deleted node\",\"Correct. Set rear = NULL when front becomes NULL (Lab 7 does front = rear = NULL).\"],[\"It crashes immediately\",\"isEmpty() only compares front and rear with NULL; it does not dereference anything, so it does not crash. It just returns the wrong answer.\"],[\"front is not NULL\",\"front does become NULL: front = front->next copies the deleted node's next, which was NULL. The problem is rear.\"]],\"a\":1}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"Linked queue: enqueue 5, 6, 7; dequeue; enqueue 8. What do count() and get() return?\",\"o\":[[\"3 and 6\",\"Correct. After the enqueues: 5, 6, 7; dequeue removes the front (5); enqueue(8) adds at rear → 6, 7, 8. count() = 3 and get() returns front->data = 6 (L7).\"],[\"4 and 5\",\"5 was removed by the dequeue, so it cannot be returned by get(), and only 3 nodes remain (6, 7, 8).\"],[\"3 and 8\",\"The count is right but get() returns front->data (6), not the rear value 8.\"],[\"2 and 7\",\"Three nodes remain (6, 7, 8): one dequeue after three enqueues and then one more enqueue. And get() returns 6, the front.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In enqueue, why is newnode->next set to NULL?\",\"o\":[[\"Because it becomes the first node\",\"The new node joins at the rear, so it is the last node, not the first. Its next must be NULL to end the list.\"],[\"Because the new node becomes the last node of the list\",\"Correct. Step 1 of enqueue sets newNode->next = NULL because the new node is linked after rear and becomes the last node, whose next must be NULL (L7, Enqueue).\"],[\"To delete the old rear\",\"Setting newnode->next = NULL deletes nothing; enqueue never deletes. It only marks the new node as the end of the list.\"],[\"It is optional\",\"It is required: display, count and search stop at NULL, so the last node's next must be NULL (the Node constructor also sets it).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"When the queue is empty, enqueue sets:\",\"o\":[[\"rear->next = newnode\",\"When the queue is empty rear is NULL, so rear->next cannot be used. Step 3 sets front = newNode and rear = newNode.\"],[\"front = NULL and rear = newnode\",\"With front left NULL, dequeue and display would see no node. A single node is both first and last, so front = rear = newNode.\"],[\"front = newnode and rear = newnode\",\"Correct. Step 3: if the queue is empty, the new node is both the first and the last node, so front = newNode and rear = newNode (L7, Enqueue).\"],[\"top = newnode\",\"top is a stack pointer; a linked queue has front and rear only.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which display order is produced by a linked queue holding 10, 15, 22, 50 (inserted in that order)?\",\"o\":[[\"50 22 15 10\",\"50 22 15 10 is reverse insertion order, which is what a linked stack displays. A queue displays front to rear.\"],[\"10 50 15 22\",\"display() follows next from front without skipping, so the order must be the insertion order 10 15 22 50.\"],[\"22 50 10 15\",\"Starting at 22 would skip the front. display() starts at front (10) and follows next to rear.\"],[\"10 15 22 50\",\"Correct. display() starts at temp = front (the first inserted, 10) and follows next until NULL, so it prints in FIFO insertion order (L7, Display).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which step list describes dequeue correctly?\",\"o\":[[\"Check empty; temp = rear; rear = rear->next; delete temp\",\"Deletion in a queue is at the front. Also rear->next is NULL, so rear = rear->next would just lose the queue's end.\"],[\"Check empty; temp = front; front = front->next; delete temp\",\"Correct. The lecture's dequeue: check empty (front == NULL); set temp = front; move front = front->next; then delete temp (L7, Dequeue).\"],[\"Check full; temp = front; delete front\",\"Dequeue checks empty, and front must move first.\"],[\"temp = front; delete temp; front = front->next\",\"Deleting temp first frees the front node, and front->next is then read from freed memory. front must move before delete.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What is the main advantage of the linked queue over the array queue?\",\"o\":[[\"It works for an unlimited (variable) number of values\",\"Correct. The array queue works only for a fixed number of values set at the start; the linked queue works for an unlimited (variable) number with no size fixed (L7, Linked queue).\"],[\"It is LIFO\",\"Both queue implementations are FIFO. LIFO is the stack rule.\"],[\"It needs no pointers\",\"The linked queue does need pointers: every node has next, and the queue keeps front and rear.\"],[\"It allows direct index access\",\"Direct index access is an array property. Linked structures need traversal (linear access).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Lab 7's dequeue handles a queue with only one item by:\",\"o\":[[\"Deleting rear only\",\"Deleting only rear leaves front pointing at freed memory. With one item, both pointers must be reset to NULL.\"],[\"Moving rear back one node\",\"A singly linked node has no prev pointer, so rear cannot move back; with one item the queue simply becomes empty (front = rear = NULL).\"],[\"Printing the item and setting front = rear = NULL\",\"Correct. When front->next == NULL (only one item), Lab 7's dequeue prints it and sets front = rear = NULL, so the queue is properly empty and isEmpty() works again (Lab 7, Linked queue).\"],[\"Calling enqueue\",\"Dequeue never calls enqueue. For one item, Lab 7 prints it and sets front = rear = NULL.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Linked queue: enqueue 1, 2; dequeue; dequeue; enqueue 3 (using the safe dequeue that resets rear). The queue is:\",\"o\":[[\"1 2 3\",\"Both dequeues removed nodes from the front: first 1, then 2. Only 3 is left.\"],[\"2 3\",\"The second dequeue removed 2 as well, emptying the queue before 3 was added.\"],[\"Empty\",\"The queue was empty after two dequeues, but enqueue(3) then made 3 the only node (front = rear = newnode).\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. After two dequeues the queue is empty (front = rear = NULL); 3 becomes the only node.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Where does a linked queue insert and where does it delete, in list terms?\",\"o\":[[\"Insert at the end, delete from the beginning\",\"Correct. enqueue links the new node after rear (end of the list) and dequeue removes the node at front (beginning), so the first in is the first out (L7, Linked queue).\"],[\"Insert at the beginning, delete from the beginning\",\"Inserting and deleting at the beginning is a linked stack (LIFO), not a queue.\"],[\"Insert at the beginning, delete from the end\",\"That would still be FIFO but is not the lecture's design (deleting the end needs a traversal).\"],[\"Insert in order, delete anywhere\",\"A queue never orders by value or deletes from the middle; it inserts at rear and deletes at front.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"How many nodes does search(22) visit in the queue 10 → 15 → 22 → 50?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"One visit checks only the front (10). 22 is the third node from front.\"],[\"4\",\"search returns true as soon as temp->data == 22, so it never visits 50.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. search starts at front and checks 10, 15, then 22, where temp->data == item and it returns true: 3 nodes visited (L7, Searching).\"],[\"2\",\"Two visits check only 10 and 15; 22 is the third node.\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":8,\"title\":\"Doubly Linked Lists\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Singly vs doubly linked list\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"dlist\",\"init\":[20,30],\"ops\":[\"begin 10\",\"end 40\",\"delete 30\"]},\"pts\":[\"A linked list consists of connected nodes. In a <b>singly</b> linked list each node has data and a pointer to the next node only, so we travel in <b>one direction</b> (forward).\",\"A <b>doubly linked list</b> node has <b>data</b>, a pointer to the <b>next</b> node and a pointer to the <b>previous</b> node, so we can travel in <b>both directions</b>, forward and backward.\",\"Node fields: <b>Data</b> (value), <b>prev</b> (pointer to the previous node), <b>next</b> (pointer to the next node). The first node's prev and the last node's next are NULL.\",\"Cost: extra memory for the prev pointer, and every insert/delete must update <b>both</b> next and prev links.\"]},{\"h\":\"Node and list classes (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"class Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n   int data;\\n   Node *next;\\n   Node *prev;\\n   Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; prev = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Linkedlist\\n{\\npublic:\\n   Node *head;\\n   Linkedlist() { head = NULL; }\\n   bool isEmpty() { if (head == NULL) return true; else return false; }\\n   bool isFull()\\n   { Node *newnode = new Node();\\n     if (newnode == NULL) return true; else return false; }\\n};\"},{\"h\":\"Insertion: beginning and end (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"void insertatbeginning(int item)\\n{\\n   Node *newnode = new Node();\\n   newnode->data = item;\\n   if (isEmpty())\\n   { newnode->next = NULL; newnode->prev = NULL; head = newnode; }\\n   else\\n   { newnode->next = head;\\n     newnode->prev = NULL;\\n     head->prev = newnode;      // old first node points back to new node\\n     head = newnode; }\\n}\\nvoid insertatend(int item)\\n{\\n   Node *newnode = new Node();\\n   newnode->data = item;\\n   newnode->next = NULL;\\n   Node *temp1 = head;\\n   while (temp1->next != NULL) temp1 = temp1->next;   // last node\\n   temp1->next = newnode;\\n   newnode->prev = temp1;\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Insert in order (safe version, tested with g++)\",\"code\":\"// The slide's insertsorted uses the same loop as the singly list and then:\\n//   newnode->next = temp1->next; temp1->next = newnode;\\n//   newnode->prev = temp1;       newnode->next->prev = newnode;\\n// It fails when the new value is smaller than the head or larger than the last node.\\nvoid insertsorted(int item)\\n{\\n   Node *newnode = new Node();\\n   newnode->data = item;\\n   if (isEmpty())\\n      head = newnode;\\n   else if (item < head->data)          // new first node\\n   {\\n      newnode->next = head;\\n      head->prev = newnode;\\n      head = newnode;\\n   }\\n   else\\n   {\\n      Node *temp = head;\\n      while (temp->next != NULL && temp->next->data < item)\\n         temp = temp->next;\\n      newnode->next = temp->next;\\n      newnode->prev = temp;\\n      if (temp->next != NULL) temp->next->prev = newnode;\\n      temp->next = newnode;\\n   }\\n}\\n// insert 30,10,20,5,40,25  => forward 5 10 20 25 30 40, backward 40 30 25 20 10 5\"},{\"h\":\"Deletion (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"int deletefrombeginning()\\n{\\n   int value;\\n   Node *temp = head;\\n   if (isEmpty()) { cout << \\\"Empty\\\" << endl; return 0; }\\n   else\\n   { value = head->data;\\n     head = head->next;\\n     head->prev = NULL;      // (crashes if it was the only node: check head != NULL)\\n     delete(temp);\\n     return (value); }\\n}\\n// deletefromend is the same as in the singly list (temp2->next = NULL)\\n\\nvoid deleteitem(int value)\\n{\\n   if (isEmpty()) cout << \\\"empty\\\";\\n   else if (search(value))\\n   {\\n      Node *temp1 = head, *temp2 = NULL;\\n      if (head->data == value)\\n      {\\n         if (head->next == NULL) { head = NULL; delete (temp1); }\\n         else { head = head->next; head->prev = NULL; delete temp1; }\\n      }\\n      else\\n      {\\n         while ((temp1->data != value) && (temp1 != NULL))\\n         { temp2 = temp1; temp1 = temp1->next; }\\n         if (temp1->next == NULL)              // deleting the last node\\n         { temp2->next = NULL; delete temp1; }\\n         else                                  // interior node\\n         { temp2->next = temp1->next;\\n           temp1->next->prev = temp1->prev;\\n           delete(temp1); }\\n      }\\n   }\\n   else cout << \\\"not found\\\";\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Remove all items (exam pattern, tested)\",\"code\":\"void removeAll()\\n{\\n   if (isEmpty()) { cout << \\\"List is empty\\\" << endl; return; }\\n   while (head != NULL)\\n   {\\n      Node *temp = head;\\n      head = head->next;\\n      if (head != NULL) head->prev = NULL;\\n      delete temp;\\n   }\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Lab 8: list with head and tail\",\"pts\":[\"The lab keeps <b>two pointers, head and tail</b>, so <code>insertLast</code> does not need a loop: <code>newnode-&gt;prev = tail; tail-&gt;next = newnode; tail = newnode;</code>\",\"<code>removeLast</code>: <code>delnode = tail; tail-&gt;prev-&gt;next = NULL; tail = tail-&gt;prev; delete delnode;</code>\",\"<code>displayBackward</code> starts at tail and follows prev; only possible because of the prev pointers.\",\"Lab main: insertFirst(10), insertLast(20), insertLast(30), insertFirst(100), insertFirst(200) → 200 100 10 20 30; removeFirst → 100 10 20 30; removeLast → 100 10 20; insertAtPosition(1000,2) → 100 1000 10 20; insertAtPosition(2000,4) → 100 1000 10 2000 20; insertAtPosition(5,1) → 5 100 1000 10 2000 20 (assuming <code>current</code> starts at 1; the lab code forgets to initialize it).\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Doubly linked list node fields\",\"data, prev, next.\"],[\"Advantage of a doubly linked list\",\"Traversal in both directions (forward and backward).\"],[\"Disadvantage of a doubly linked list\",\"Extra memory for the prev pointer; more links to update.\"],[\"Insert at beginning (non-empty)\",\"newnode->next = head; newnode->prev = NULL; head->prev = newnode; head = newnode;\"],[\"Insert at end\",\"last->next = newnode; newnode->prev = last; newnode->next = NULL;\"],[\"Insert after temp (interior)\",\"newnode->next = temp->next; newnode->prev = temp; temp->next->prev = newnode; temp->next = newnode;\"],[\"Delete first node\",\"head = head->next; head->prev = NULL; delete old head.\"],[\"Delete interior node temp1 (previous = temp2)\",\"temp2->next = temp1->next; temp1->next->prev = temp1->prev; delete temp1;\"],[\"First node's prev\",\"NULL\"],[\"Three insertion places\",\"Beginning, end, a position within the list.\"],[\"Three deletion places\",\"Beginning, end, a specific node.\"],[\"Why does Lab 8 keep tail?\",\"To insert/remove at the end and display backward without traversing.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Define a doubly linked list and compare it with a singly linked list.\",\"A doubly linked list is made of nodes holding data, a pointer to the next node and a pointer to the previous node, so it can be traversed forward and backward. A singly linked list node has only data and next, so it is traversed forward only. The doubly list uses extra memory for prev and needs more pointer updates on insert/delete.\"],[\"Write the function to insert a node at the beginning of a doubly linked list.\",\"void insertatbeginning(int item){ Node *newnode=new Node(); newnode->data=item; if(isEmpty()){ newnode->next=NULL; newnode->prev=NULL; head=newnode; } else { newnode->next=head; newnode->prev=NULL; head->prev=newnode; head=newnode; } }\"],[\"Write a function to insert a new node in its order in a doubly linked list.\",\"if empty: head = newnode. else if item &lt; head->data: newnode->next = head; head->prev = newnode; head = newnode. else: temp = head; while(temp->next != NULL &amp;&amp; temp->next->data &lt; item) temp = temp->next; newnode->next = temp->next; newnode->prev = temp; if(temp->next != NULL) temp->next->prev = newnode; temp->next = newnode;\"],[\"Explain the deletion of an interior node in a doubly linked list.\",\"Traverse until temp1 is the node to delete and temp2 the node before it. Link around it in both directions: temp2->next = temp1->next and temp1->next->prev = temp1->prev (= temp2). Then delete temp1. If temp1 is the last node, only temp2->next = NULL is needed.\"],[\"Write a program to remove all the items from a doubly linked list, checking if it is empty (Exam 2024/25).\",\"void removeAll(){ if(isEmpty()){ cout&lt;&lt;\\\"List is empty\\\"; return; } while(head != NULL){ Node *temp = head; head = head->next; if(head != NULL) head->prev = NULL; delete temp; } }\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"On inserting a new node N in a doubly linked list, N->next = NULL is a must.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"N->next = NULL is needed only when N becomes the last node (insert at end or into an empty list). At the beginning, N->next = head.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. When inserting at the beginning or in the middle, N->next points to the following node; it is NULL only when inserting at the end (or into an empty list).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"A doubly linked list node contains:\",\"o\":[[\"data and next\",\"data and next only is a singly linked node, which can be traversed forward only. A doubly linked node adds prev.\"],[\"data, next and prev\",\"Correct. Each doubly linked node holds the data, prev (pointer to the previous node) and next (pointer to the next node), so the list can be traveled both ways (L8, Representation).\"],[\"data and prev only\",\"Without next the list could not be traversed forward. A doubly linked node has both prev and next, plus the data.\"],[\"next and prev only\",\"A node must hold the data value; next and prev are only the links. The node class has int data, Node *next and Node *prev.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which statement is missing when inserting at the beginning of a non-empty doubly list? <code>newnode->next = head; newnode->prev = NULL; ____; head = newnode;</code>\",\"o\":[[\"head->next = newnode;\",\"That would make the old head point forward to the new node.\"],[\"head->prev = newnode;\",\"Correct. The old first node must point back to the new node.\"],[\"newnode->next->next = NULL;\",\"newnode->next is the old head; setting its next to NULL cuts off every node after it. The missing step links the old head back: head->prev = newnode.\"],[\"head = NULL;\",\"head = NULL achieves nothing (head = newnode overwrites it), and the old first node's prev stays NULL. The missing link is head->prev = newnode.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which traversal is possible in a doubly linked list but not in a singly linked list?\",\"o\":[[\"Forward from head\",\"Forward traversal from head works in both lists, since both have next pointers.\"],[\"Backward from the last node\",\"Correct. A singly linked node has only next, so it can travel forward only; the doubly linked node's prev pointer lets you travel backward from the last node too (L8, Doubly linked list).\"],[\"Counting nodes\",\"Counting is a forward traversal with c++ per node, which both lists support.\"],[\"Searching for a value\",\"Searching walks forward comparing data, which works in both singly and doubly linked lists.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In deleteitem for an interior node temp1 (temp2 is before it), which pair of statements relinks the list?\",\"o\":[[\"temp2->next = temp1->next; temp1->next->prev = temp1->prev;\",\"Correct. temp2->next = temp1->next makes the previous node skip temp1 forward, and temp1->next->prev = temp1->prev makes the following node point back to temp2; then temp1 is deleted (L8, Deleting within a doubly linked list).\"],[\"temp2->prev = temp1; temp1->prev = temp2;\",\"These lines point temp2 back to temp1 and temp1 back to temp2, so temp1 is still in the chain and nothing is bypassed.\"],[\"temp1->next = temp2; temp2->next = NULL;\",\"temp2->next = NULL ends the list at temp2, losing every node after temp1 instead of skipping just temp1.\"],[\"head = temp1->next;\",\"Moving head is used only when the node to delete is the first one (head->data == value); temp1 here is an interior node.\"]],\"a\":0}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"Lab 8 main: insertFirst(10), insertLast(20), insertLast(30), insertFirst(100), insertFirst(200), removeFirst(), removeLast(). The list is:\",\"o\":[[\"200 100 10 20\",\"removeFirst() deleted the head (200), so 200 is gone; and removeLast() deleted 30.\"],[\"100 10 20\",\"Correct. 200 100 10 20 30 → remove first → 100 10 20 30 → remove last → 100 10 20.\"],[\"100 10 20 30\",\"removeLast() deletes the tail node (30): tail->prev->next = NULL; tail = tail->prev.\"],[\"10 20 30\",\"100 was inserted at the front after 10 and is not removed: removeFirst() removed 200 only.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Continuing Lab 8: insertAtPosition(1000, 2), insertAtPosition(2000, 4), insertAtPosition(5, 1). displayBackward prints:\",\"o\":[[\"5 100 1000 10 2000 20\",\"That is the forward order (displayForward). displayBackward starts from tail and follows prev, so it prints the reverse.\"],[\"20 10 2000 1000 100 5\",\"2000 was inserted at position 4, after 10, so backward it comes right after 20 and before 10.\"],[\"20 2000 10 1000 100 5\",\"Correct. Forward is 5 100 1000 10 2000 20 (run with g++, current initialized to 1).\"],[\"20 2000 1000 10 100 5\",\"1000 was inserted at position 2, right after 100, so backward it is just before 100: … 10 1000 100 5.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which statement must be added when deleting the first node of a doubly linked list (with more than one node)?\",\"o\":[[\"head->next = NULL;\",\"head->next = NULL would cut the new first node off from the rest of the list.\"],[\"tail = head;\",\"Deleting the first node does not move the tail (with more than one node); only head and the new head's prev change.\"],[\"head->prev = head;\",\"A first node's prev must be NULL, not point to itself; head->prev = head would create a self-loop when traveling backward.\"],[\"head->prev = NULL; after head = head->next;\",\"Correct. The new first node must not point back to the deleted node.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"What goes wrong in the lecture's deletefrombeginning if the list has only one node?\",\"o\":[[\"head becomes NULL and head->prev = NULL crashes\",\"Correct. Check head != NULL before setting prev.\"],[\"Nothing\",\"With one node, head = head->next makes head NULL, and head->prev = NULL then dereferences NULL and crashes.\"],[\"It deletes two nodes\",\"Only one node exists and only temp is deleted; the problem is the head->prev access on a NULL head.\"],[\"It returns the wrong value\",\"value = head->data is read correctly before head moves; the failure is the crash at head->prev = NULL.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A doubly linked list node uses more memory than a singly linked node because:\",\"o\":[[\"It stores the data twice\",\"Each node stores its data once, like a singly linked node. The extra memory is the backward reference.\"],[\"It has an extra prev pointer\",\"Correct. Besides data and next, each doubly linked node has a prev pointer; the lecture notes this allows backward travel but requires additional memory (L5/L8, Doubly linked list).\"],[\"It is always allocated as an array\",\"Nodes are created one at a time with new Node(), not as an array; the extra memory per node is the prev pointer.\"],[\"It stores the head inside each node\",\"Nodes do not store head; head is a single pointer in the list class. Each node only has data, next and prev.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Insert 15 between nodes 10 (temp) and 20 in a doubly list. Which statement is part of the correct linking?\",\"o\":[[\"newnode->prev = temp->next;\",\"temp->next is 20; 15's prev must point to the node before it, temp (10).\"],[\"temp->prev = newnode;\",\"10's prev points to the node before 10 and does not change; 20's prev is the one that must become 15.\"],[\"temp->next->prev = newnode;\",\"Correct. temp->next is 20, and 20 must point back to the new node, so its prev becomes 15; the lecture writes it as newnode->next->prev = newnode, with newnode->prev = temp and newnode->next = 20 (L8, insertsorted).\"],[\"newnode->next = temp;\",\"newnode->next = temp would point 15 back to 10. Its next must be the following node, 20.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"What does insertatend set as the new node's prev?\",\"o\":[[\"NULL\",\"prev = NULL is only for the first node. A node added at the end has a node before it, the old last node.\"],[\"head\",\"prev is head only if the list had one node. In general it is temp1, the old last node found by the loop.\"],[\"newnode itself\",\"A node never points back to itself; newnode->prev must be the node before it, temp1.\"],[\"The old last node (temp1)\",\"Correct. insertatend walks temp1 to the last node, links temp1->next = newnode, then sets newnode->prev = temp1 so the new last node points back to the old one (L8, Inserting at the end).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Lab 8 removeLast() uses which statements?\",\"o\":[[\"delnode = tail; tail->prev->next = NULL; tail = tail->prev; delete delnode;\",\"Correct. Lab 8 keeps a tail pointer: save delnode = tail, cut the link from the previous node (tail->prev->next = NULL), move tail back to it, and delete delnode (Lab 8, removeLast).\"],[\"tail = NULL; delete tail;\",\"tail = NULL throws away the only pointer to the last node, and delete tail then deletes NULL; the node before still points to it.\"],[\"head = head->next; delete head;\",\"This moves head forward, so it works on the first node, and it then deletes the new head instead of the old one.\"],[\"tail->next = NULL; delete tail;\",\"tail->next is already NULL; the previous node must be updated.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Forward display of a doubly list gives 3 6 9. What is head->next->prev->data?\",\"o\":[[\"6\",\"head->next is 6, but the question then goes back one step with prev, which returns to 3.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. Going forward then back returns to the head (3).\"],[\"9\",\"9 is the last node, reached by head->next->next. Going forward one and back one stays at the head.\"],[\"NULL\",\"Only the first node's prev is NULL. 6 has a previous node, 3.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"How many pointer fields change when inserting a node in the middle of a doubly linked list (counting the new node's two fields)?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"Changing one pointer would leave the new node unlinked on one side. Both neighbors and both new fields change.\"],[\"2\",\"2 changes (newnode->next and previous->next) is the singly-linked case. A doubly list must also set two prev pointers.\"],[\"4\",\"Correct. newnode->next, newnode->prev, previous->next and following->prev.\"],[\"6\",\"Only four links change: the new node's next and prev, the previous node's next and the following node's prev.\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":9,\"title\":\"Trees and Binary Search Trees\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Parts of a tree\",\"pts\":[\"A tree is a <b>non-linear (hierarchical)</b> data structure of <b>nodes</b>.\",\"<b>Root node</b>: the top node with no parent. <b>Parent</b> node and its <b>child</b> nodes. <b>Leaf (terminal) nodes</b>: nodes with no children. <b>Internal node</b>: a node with at least one child. <b>Sub-tree</b>: a node together with all its descendants.\",\"<b>Binary tree</b>: each node has <b>at most 2 children</b> (left and right).\",\"<b>Binary Search Tree (BST)</b>: a binary tree such that (1) every node has a <b>unique key</b>; (2) all keys in the <b>left subtree</b> of a node are <b>less</b> than the node's key; (3) all keys in the <b>right subtree</b> are <b>greater</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Traversals\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"bst\",\"init\":[50,25,65,12,33,70,55,60,53],\"ops\":[\"inorder\",\"preorder\",\"postorder\"]},\"table\":[[\"Traversal\",\"Order\",\"Memory aid\"],[\"Inorder\",\"Left subtree → parent → right subtree\",\"L P R; on a BST gives the keys in ascending (sorted) order\"],[\"Preorder\",\"Parent → left subtree → right subtree\",\"P L R; root printed first\"],[\"Postorder\",\"Left subtree → right subtree → parent\",\"L R P; root printed last\"],[\"(Reverse inorder)\",\"Right → parent → left\",\"On a BST gives descending order\"]]},{\"h\":\"Lecture example trees (checked with g++)\",\"formula\":[\"Tree 1: 43; 31(20(-,28), 40(33,-)); 64(56(47,59), 89)\",\"Inorder:   20 28 31 33 40 43 47 56 59 64 89\",\"Preorder:  43 31 20 28 40 33 64 56 47 59 89\",\"Postorder: 28 20 33 40 31 47 59 56 89 64 43\",\"Tree 2 (insert 50,25,65,12,33,70,55,60,53): 50; 25(12,33); 65(55(53,60),70)\",\"Preorder:  50 25 12 33 65 55 53 60 70\",\"Inorder:   12 25 33 50 53 55 60 65 70\",\"Postorder: 12 33 25 53 60 55 70 65 50\"]},{\"h\":\"Node, Tree and traversal code (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"class Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n   int data;\\n   Node *left;\\n   Node *right;\\n   Node() { data = 0; left = right = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Tree\\n{\\npublic:\\n   Node *root;\\n   Tree() { root = NULL; }\\n};\\nvoid inorder(Node *temp)      // sorted order for a BST\\n{\\n   if (temp == NULL) { return; }\\n   inorder(temp->left);\\n   cout << temp->data << \\\"\\\\t\\\";\\n   inorder(temp->right);\\n}\\nvoid preorder(Node *temp)\\n{\\n   if (temp == NULL) { return; }\\n   cout << temp->data << \\\"\\\\t\\\";\\n   preorder(temp->left);\\n   preorder(temp->right);\\n}\\nvoid postorder(Node *temp)\\n{\\n   if (temp == NULL) { return; }\\n   postorder(temp->left);\\n   postorder(temp->right);\\n   cout << temp->data << \\\"\\\\t\\\";\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Insertion\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"bst\",\"ops\":[\"insert 50,25,65,12,33,70,55,60,53\"]},\"pts\":[\"Steps: create a new node for the item; find the appropriate parent node; attach the new node as a <b>leaf</b> (left or right according to its key).\",\"Recursive version: parameters are a pointer to the current node (initially the root) and the item. If the current node is NULL, create a new node and return it. Else if the item is less (greater) than the current key, recurse on the left (right) child and set the parent's left (right) link to the result.\",\"Example: inserting 57 into tree 1: 57 &gt; 43 → right; 57 &lt; 64 → left; 57 &gt; 56 → right; 57 &lt; 59 → becomes the <b>left child of 59</b>.\",\"Example: inserting 0.9 into the tree with root 1.0: 0.9 &lt; 1.0 → left (0.6); 0.9 &gt; 0.6 → right (0.8); 0.9 &gt; 0.8 → right child of 0.8.\",\"The <b>shape</b> depends on the insertion order; inserting already sorted keys gives a degenerate (linked-list-like) tree.\"]},{\"h\":\"insert, search, minimum, maximum (professor's code)\",\"code\":\"Node* insert(Node *temp, int item)\\n{\\n   if (temp == NULL)\\n   {\\n      Node *newnode = new Node();\\n      newnode->data = item;\\n      temp = newnode;\\n   }\\n   else if (item < temp->data) temp->left = insert(temp->left, item);\\n   else temp->right = insert(temp->right, item);\\n   return temp;\\n}\\n// call: list.root = list.insert(list.root, a[i]);\\n\\nbool search(Node *temp, int item)\\n{\\n   if (temp == NULL) return false;\\n   else if (item == temp->data) return true;\\n   else if (item < temp->data) return search(temp->left, item);\\n   else return search(temp->right, item);\\n}   // (the slide omits \\\"return\\\" before the recursive calls; it is needed)\\n\\nNode* minimum(Node *temp)       // leftmost node\\n{\\n   if (temp == NULL) return NULL;\\n   else if (temp->left == NULL) return temp;\\n   else return minimum(temp->left);\\n}\\nNode* maximum(Node *temp)       // rightmost node\\n{\\n   if (temp == NULL) return NULL;\\n   else if (temp->right == NULL) return temp;\\n   else return maximum(temp->right);\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Searching and deletion\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"bst\",\"init\":[50,25,65,12,33,70,55,60,53],\"ops\":[\"search 60\",\"search 30\",\"delete 12\",\"delete 25\",\"delete 65\"]},\"pts\":[\"<b>Search</b>: if the target key is less than the current key, search the left subtree; if greater, search the right subtree; if equal, found (return the node / true); if NULL is reached, not found.\",\"Find 0.7 in the 1.0 tree: 1.0 → 0.6 → 0.8 → 0.7 found. Find 0.5: 1.0 → 0.6 → 0.3 → 0.4 → NULL, not found.\",\"<b>Delete</b> cases: (1) leaf: just remove it; (2) one child: replace the node by its child; (3) two children: replace the key by its inorder successor (minimum of the right subtree) or predecessor (maximum of the left subtree), then delete that node.\",\"The slide's <code>deleteitem</code> handles a leaf and a node with one child by copying the child's data and deleting the child (correct only if that child is a leaf); it does not handle two children. In an exam, describe the three standard cases.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Root\",\"The top node of a tree, with no parent.\"],[\"Leaf\",\"A node with no children (terminal node).\"],[\"Binary tree\",\"Each node has at most 2 children.\"],[\"BST rule\",\"Left subtree keys &lt; node key &lt; right subtree keys; keys unique.\"],[\"Inorder\",\"Left, parent, right.\"],[\"Preorder\",\"Parent, left, right.\"],[\"Postorder\",\"Left, right, parent.\"],[\"Inorder of a BST gives\",\"Keys in ascending (sorted) order.\"],[\"Right, root, left on a BST gives\",\"Descending order.\"],[\"Pre-order = post-order output when\",\"The tree has only one node.\"],[\"Where is a new key inserted in a BST?\",\"As a leaf, found by moving left (smaller) or right (larger) from the root.\"],[\"Minimum of a BST\",\"The leftmost node.\"],[\"Maximum of a BST\",\"The rightmost node.\"],[\"Tree node fields\",\"data, left, right.\"],[\"Search 0.5 in the 1.0 tree path\",\"1.0 → 0.6 → 0.3 → 0.4 → NULL (not found).\"],[\"Delete a node with two children\",\"Replace with inorder successor (min of right subtree) or predecessor, then delete that node.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Define a binary search tree.\",\"A binary tree (each node at most two children) in which every node has a unique key, all keys in the left subtree of a node are less than the node's key, and all keys in the right subtree are greater.\"],[\"Explain the three traversal methods and give the result for the tree 50(25(12,33), 65(55(53,60),70)).\",\"Inorder: left, parent, right → 12 25 33 50 53 55 60 65 70. Preorder: parent, left, right → 50 25 12 33 65 55 53 60 70. Postorder: left, right, parent → 12 33 25 53 60 55 70 65 50. The name depends on when the parent is visited.\"],[\"Explain insertion in a BST.\",\"Create a new node for the item, find the appropriate parent by comparing with keys from the root (go left if smaller, right if larger), and attach the new node as a leaf. Recursively: if current is NULL create and return a new node; else set current->left = insert(current->left, item) or current->right = insert(current->right, item) and return current.\"],[\"Write C++ functions for preorder, inorder and postorder traversal.\",\"void preorder(Node *t){ if(t==NULL) return; cout&lt;&lt;t->data; preorder(t->left); preorder(t->right); }<br>void inorder(Node *t){ if(t==NULL) return; inorder(t->left); cout&lt;&lt;t->data; inorder(t->right); }<br>void postorder(Node *t){ if(t==NULL) return; postorder(t->left); postorder(t->right); cout&lt;&lt;t->data; }\"],[\"Explain how to search for a key in a BST and how to find the minimum and maximum.\",\"Compare the key with the current node: equal → found; smaller → search the left subtree; larger → search the right subtree; NULL → not found. Minimum: keep going left until left is NULL. Maximum: keep going right until right is NULL.\"],[\"Explain the cases of deleting a node from a BST.\",\"Leaf: remove it and set the parent's link to NULL. One child: link the parent directly to that child. Two children: copy the inorder successor (smallest key in the right subtree) into the node and delete the successor (which has at most one child).\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"If a Binary Search Tree is traversed in the order right, root, left recursively, the output sequence will be in:\",\"o\":[[\"Ascending order\",\"Ascending order comes from inorder (left, root, right), because smaller keys are in the left subtree. Visiting right first reverses it.\"],[\"Descending order\",\"Correct. Larger keys are on the right, so visiting right first gives descending order.\"],[\"No specific order\",\"The BST property (left keys smaller, right keys larger) fixes the order: right, root, left always lists keys from largest to smallest.\"],[\"Right, root, left cannot be done\",\"Any visiting order can be coded recursively; right, root, left is just inorder with the two recursive calls swapped (reverse inorder).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"When the pre-order and post-order traversals of a binary tree generate the same output, the tree has:\",\"o\":[[\"Two nodes\",\"With two nodes the root is first in preorder and last in postorder, so they differ.\"],[\"Only one node\",\"Correct. Only a single node is both first and last.\"],[\"Three nodes\",\"With three nodes the root is printed first in preorder and last in postorder, so the two outputs differ.\"],[\"Any number of nodes\",\"With two or more nodes the root is first in preorder but last in postorder, so the outputs differ; only a single node (or empty tree) gives the same output.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"What are the numbers of nodes in the left and right subtrees of the BST if the data is inserted in the order: 45, 15, 8, 56, 5, 65, 47, 12, 18, 10, 73, 50, 16, 61?\",\"o\":[[\"8 left and 5 right\",\"8 and 5 miscounts. Keys less than 45 (left subtree): 15, 8, 5, 12, 18, 10, 16 = 7; greater (right): 56, 65, 47, 73, 50, 61 = 6.\"],[\"6 left and 7 right\",\"6 and 7 swaps the two counts: 7 keys are less than 45 (left subtree) and 6 are greater (right subtree).\"],[\"7 left and 6 right\",\"Correct. Less than 45: 15, 8, 5, 12, 18, 10, 16 (7). Greater: 56, 65, 47, 73, 50, 61 (6). Verified with g++.\"],[\"5 left and 8 right\",\"5 and 8 miscounts. All keys less than the root 45 go left (7 of them) and all greater keys go right (6).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"To print the keys of a binary search tree in ascending order, you may apply inorder or postorder traversals.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Postorder does not give sorted output.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The lecture says inorder traversal of a BST always gives the sorted (ascending) order of the keys; postorder visits the parent after both subtrees, so it is not sorted (L9, Inorder traverse code).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Left-Right-Parent represents the postorder traversal of a binary tree.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The traversal is named by when the parent is visited: postorder traverses the left subtree, then the right subtree, and visits the parent last (L9, Postorder traversal).\"],[\"False\",\"The statement is true: postorder = traverse left subtree, traverse right subtree, then visit the parent (L R P).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"For the tree 43; 31(20(-,28), 40(33,-)); 64(56(47,59), 89), the preorder traversal is:\",\"o\":[[\"43 31 20 28 40 33 64 56 47 59 89\",\"Correct. Preorder visits the parent first, then the left subtree, then the right: 43, then 31 20 28 40 33, then 64 56 47 59 89 — the lecture's own example (L9, Preorder).\"],[\"20 28 31 33 40 43 47 56 59 64 89\",\"That is the inorder output (left, parent, right), which comes out sorted for a BST. Preorder starts with the root 43.\"],[\"28 20 33 40 31 47 59 56 89 64 43\",\"That is the postorder output (left, right, parent), ending with the root 43. Preorder starts with 43.\"],[\"43 31 64 20 40 56 89 28 33 47 59\",\"That lists the tree level by level (level order), which the lecture does not teach. Preorder goes deep into the left subtree first: 43 31 20 28 …\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2023/24 Summer\"},{\"q\":\"For the tree 50; 25(12,33); 65(55(53,60),70), the postorder traversal is:\",\"o\":[[\"12 25 33 50 53 55 60 65 70\",\"That is inorder (left, parent, right), which is sorted for a BST. Postorder ends with the root 50.\"],[\"50 25 12 33 65 55 53 60 70\",\"That is preorder (parent first, root 50 at the start). Postorder visits the parent last.\"],[\"12 33 25 53 60 55 70 65 50\",\"Correct. Left, right, then parent; the root 50 is last.\"],[\"12 33 25 50 53 60 55 70 65\",\"Here 50 appears in the middle, as in inorder. In postorder the root is printed after both subtrees, so 50 must be last.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2023/24 Summer\"},{\"q\":\"For the tree F; B(A, D(C, E)); G(-, I(H, -)), the postorder traversal is:\",\"o\":[[\"A B C D E F G H I\",\"A B C … I is the inorder output (left, parent, right), which here is alphabetical. Postorder ends with the root F.\"],[\"F B A D C E G I H\",\"That is preorder (parent, left, right), starting with the root F. Postorder prints F last.\"],[\"A C E D B H I G F\",\"Correct. Left subtree of F: A, then D's children C, E, then D, then B. Right subtree: G has only a right child I, whose left is H: H, I, G. Finally the root: A C E D B H I G F (L9, Postorder).\"],[\"A C D E B H I G F\",\"This prints D before its right child E. In postorder a parent comes after both children: C, E, then D.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Inserting 57 into the BST 43; 31(…); 64(56(47,59), 89) makes 57:\",\"o\":[[\"The right child of 56\",\"56 already has a right child (59), so we continue down to 59.\"],[\"The left child of 59\",\"Correct. 57 &gt; 43 → 64; 57 &lt; 64 → 56; 57 &gt; 56 → 59; 57 &lt; 59 → left of 59.\"],[\"The left child of 89\",\"89 is in 64's right subtree; since 57 is less than 64, the search goes left to 56 and never reaches 89.\"],[\"The new root\",\"Inserting into a non-empty tree never changes the root: the new node is attached as a leaf where the search reaches NULL (L9, Insertion). Only the first insert into an empty tree creates the root.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Lecture 9 example\"},{\"q\":\"Which node holds the minimum key of a BST?\",\"o\":[[\"The root\",\"The root is only the minimum if it has no left child.\"],[\"The leftmost node\",\"Correct. minimum() keeps moving left until left is NULL.\"],[\"The rightmost node\",\"The rightmost node holds the maximum key, since larger keys are on the right; maximum() follows right links.\"],[\"Any leaf\",\"Leaves can hold any key (in the lecture tree 28, 33, 47, 59 and 89 are all leaves); only the leftmost node is guaranteed to be the minimum.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In a binary tree, each node has:\",\"o\":[[\"Exactly 2 children\",\"Exactly 2 is too strict: leaves have no children and some nodes have only one. The rule is at most two.\"],[\"At most 2 children\",\"Correct. The lecture defines a binary tree as a tree where each node has at most 2 children (a left and a right child, either may be NULL) (L9, Binary tree).\"],[\"At least 2 children\",\"Leaves have no children at all, so \\\"at least 2\\\" cannot hold for any finite tree.\"],[\"Any number of children\",\"Any number of children describes a general tree; a binary tree limits each node to at most two.\"]],\"a\":1}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"Insert 30, 20, 40, 10, 25, 35, 50 into an empty BST. The preorder traversal is:\",\"o\":[[\"10 20 25 30 35 40 50\",\"That is the inorder output, which is sorted for a BST. Preorder prints the root 30 first.\"],[\"30 20 10 25 40 35 50\",\"Correct. Root, left subtree (20, 10, 25), right subtree (40, 35, 50).\"],[\"10 25 20 35 50 40 30\",\"That is the postorder output (left, right, parent), ending with the root 30. Preorder starts with 30.\"],[\"30 20 40 10 25 35 50\",\"That lists the tree level by level, which happens to equal the insertion order. Preorder finishes the whole left subtree (20, 10, 25) before 40.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"For the same BST (30, 20, 40, 10, 25, 35, 50), the postorder traversal is:\",\"o\":[[\"10 25 20 35 50 40 30\",\"Correct. The tree is 30 with children 20 (10, 25) and 40 (35, 50). Postorder: left subtree 10 25 20, right subtree 35 50 40, then the root 30 (L9, Postorder).\"],[\"30 40 50 35 20 25 10\",\"This starts with the root 30, but postorder prints the root last. It is preorder with right visited before left.\"],[\"10 20 25 30 35 40 50\",\"That is the inorder output (sorted keys). Postorder prints each parent after its children, so 30 comes last.\"],[\"25 10 20 50 35 40 30\",\"Within a subtree the left child is listed before the right.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Inserting 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 in this order into an empty BST gives:\",\"o\":[[\"A balanced tree with root 3\",\"The first inserted key, 1, becomes the root, and each later key is larger, so the tree is not balanced.\"],[\"A tree where every node has two children\",\"Each key is larger than all before it, so every node gets only a right child; no node has two children.\"],[\"An error, because keys must be inserted unsorted\",\"insert() accepts keys in any order; sorted input just produces a tall, unbalanced tree.\"],[\"A degenerate tree where each node has only a right child (like a linked list)\",\"Correct. 1 is the root; 2 &gt; 1 goes right; 3 goes right of 2, and so on. Every node has only a right child, so the BST degenerates into a chain like a linked list (L9, Insertion).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the lecture tree 43; 31(20, 40); 64(56, 89) with leaves 28, 33, 47, 59, what does maximum(root) return?\",\"o\":[[\"64\",\"64 is not the maximum: it has a right child 89, and maximum() keeps moving right until right is NULL.\"],[\"59\",\"59 is in 64's left subtree (child of 56), so it is smaller than 64; maximum() only follows right links.\"],[\"89\",\"Correct. maximum() calls itself on temp->right until right is NULL: 43 → 64 → 89, and 89 has no right child, so it is the largest key (L9, Get maximum & minimum).\"],[\"43\",\"43 is the root; it has a right child 64, so it is not the maximum.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Search for 0.5 in the tree with root 1.0 (left 0.6, whose left is 0.3 with right child 0.4). The search:\",\"o\":[[\"Finds 0.5 at the root\",\"The root holds 1.0, not 0.5. Since 0.5 is less than 1.0, the search moves to the left subtree.\"],[\"Goes 1.0 → 0.6 → 0.3 → 0.4 → NULL and returns false\",\"Correct. 0.5 &lt; 1.0 → left to 0.6; 0.5 &lt; 0.6 → left to 0.3; 0.5 &gt; 0.3 → right to 0.4; 0.5 &gt; 0.4 → right child is NULL, so search returns false (L9, Searching example).\"],[\"Goes 1.0 → 1.9 and stops\",\"1.9 is the right child, taken only for keys greater than 1.0. 0.5 is smaller, so the search goes left.\"],[\"Goes 1.0 → 0.6 → 0.8 and returns false\",\"At 0.6 the key 0.5 is smaller, so the search goes left to 0.3, not right to 0.8.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the recursive insert, what happens when temp is NULL?\",\"o\":[[\"A new node is created with the item and returned\",\"Correct. The caller links it as a left or right child.\"],[\"The function returns false\",\"insert returns a Node* (the subtree root), not a bool; at NULL it returns the newly created node.\"],[\"The item is ignored\",\"NULL is exactly where the new item belongs: a node is created there and returned to be linked as a leaf.\"],[\"The root is deleted\",\"insert never deletes. Reaching NULL means the right place was found, and the new node is created there.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Why does the slide's search need <code>return</code> before the recursive calls?\",\"o\":[[\"To make it faster\",\"Speed is not the issue: without return the recursive call still runs, but its true/false result is thrown away.\"],[\"Because recursion is not allowed without it\",\"The recursion is allowed and still runs without return; the problem is that its result is discarded.\"],[\"Without it the result of the recursive call is discarded and the function may return garbage\",\"Correct. On the slide, search(temp->left, item) is called but its result is not returned, so the outer call falls off the end of a bool function and returns an undefined value; writing return search(…) passes the answer up (L9, Searching).\"],[\"To print the result\",\"return passes the value back to the caller; it does not print anything. Printing is done by main with cout.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which traversal visits the root first?\",\"o\":[[\"Inorder\",\"Inorder traverses the left subtree first, visiting the parent in the middle.\"],[\"Postorder\",\"Postorder visits the parent last, after both subtrees.\"],[\"Reverse inorder\",\"Reverse inorder (right, root, left) traverses the right subtree before the root.\"],[\"Preorder\",\"Correct. The traversals are named by when the parent is visited; preorder visits the parent first, then the left subtree, then the right (L9, Preorder traversal).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Deleting a node with TWO children in a BST is usually done by:\",\"o\":[[\"Deleting the whole subtree\",\"Deleting the whole subtree would also remove every key below the node, not just the one requested.\"],[\"Replacing its key with the inorder successor (minimum of the right subtree) and deleting that node\",\"Correct. The standard method copies the inorder successor (the minimum of the right subtree) into the node and deletes that successor, which has at most one child. Note: the lecture's deleteitem code handles only the leaf and one-child cases.\"],[\"Setting the parent's link to NULL\",\"Setting the parent's link to NULL only works for a leaf; here it would drop both subtrees of the node.\"],[\"Swapping it with the root\",\"Swapping with the root puts keys on the wrong side and breaks the BST rule (left smaller, right larger).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"How many leaves does the BST built from 50, 25, 65, 12, 33, 70, 55, 60, 53 have?\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"3 misses some leaves. The tree is 50; 25(12, 33); 65(55(53, 60), 70): nodes with no children are 12, 33, 53, 60, 70.\"],[\"4\",\"4 misses 70: it is 65's right child and has no children, so it is also a leaf (with 12, 33, 53, 60).\"],[\"5\",\"Correct. 12, 33, 53, 60 and 70 have no children.\"],[\"9\",\"9 is the total number of nodes. Leaves are only the nodes with no children: 12, 33, 53, 60, 70.\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":10,\"title\":\"Hashing\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"What is hashing?\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>hash table</b> is a data structure that can be considered as an <b>array of fixed size</b>; each element is identified by an index from 0 to (table size - 1). It is sometimes called a <b>scatter table</b>, because data is scattered throughout the table.\",\"Each key is mapped into a hash number in the range 0 to (table size - 1) and placed in that cell. This mapping is the <b>hash function H</b>; it should give different keys different cells and <b>distribute elements evenly</b>.\",\"A hash function H takes a <b>key as input</b> and transforms it into a hash table <b>index</b>. The same hash value is used for both <b>storing and retrieving</b>.\",\"<b>Collision</b>: when the hash function maps two different keys k and j to the <b>same cell</b>.\",\"Hash tables do <b>not</b> keep keys in sorted order: positions depend on the hash function (Lecture 10 is titled \\\"Searching Techniques: Hashing\\\").\"]},{\"h\":\"Types of hash function\",\"table\":[[\"Method\",\"How\",\"Example\"],[\"Truncation\",\"Take only part of the key (e.g. rightmost or leftmost 3 digits for a table of size 1000). Simplest, but more collisions\",\"987456, 125978, 963294 → 456, 978, 294\"],[\"Folding\",\"Break the key into groups of digits (say 3) and add them; truncate if the table is small (digits may also be multiplied)\",\"143765980 → 143 + 765 + 980 = 1888\"],[\"Mid square\",\"Square the key and take the middle 2 or 3 digits\",\"456² = 207936 → 79; 978² = 956484 → 64\"],[\"Division\",\"Key mod table size (remainder) is the address; one of the best methods\",\"5, 17, 37, 20, 42, 3 mod 9 → 5, 8, 1, 2, 6, 3\"]]},{\"h\":\"Collision resolution techniques\",\"pts\":[\"Five techniques: <b>linear probing</b>, <b>quadratic probing</b>, <b>double hashing</b>, <b>rehashing</b>, <b>extendible hashing</b> (the first three are studied), plus <b>separate chaining</b>.\",\"<b>Linear probing</b>: place the colliding record in the <b>next available empty position</b>, searching sequentially: <b>h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i) mod m</b>, i = 0, 1, 2, … where m = table size and h1 = primary hash function. To search, start at the hash address and continue linearly.\",\"<b>Quadratic probing</b>: on the 1st collision look h+1 ahead, 2nd h+4, 3rd h+9 … (h + i²). The lecture's formula and worked example use <b>h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i + i²) mod m</b> (i = 1 → +2, i = 2 → +6). Use the lecture's formula in the exam and state it.\",\"<b>Double hashing</b>: two hash functions; when the first collides the second resolves it: <b>h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i·h2(k)) mod m</b>. Lecture example: h1 = k mod 13, h2 = 1 + (k mod 11).\",\"<b>Separate chaining</b>: an <b>array of pointers</b>; each points to a <b>linked list</b> of the keys with the same hash address. On a collision a new link is added to the chain. Handles an <b>unlimited number of collisions</b> and needs <b>no prior knowledge</b> of how many elements will be stored.\"]},{\"h\":\"Linear probing example (keys 18, 72, 65, 34, 13; size 7)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"hash\",\"size\":7,\"method\":\"linear\",\"keys\":[18,72,65,34,13]},\"formula\":[\"h1(k) = k mod 7\",\"18: h(18,0) = 4 → slot 4\",\"72: h(72,0) = 2 → slot 2\",\"65: h(65,0) = 2 collision; h(65,1) = (2+1) mod 7 = 3 → slot 3\",\"34: h(34,0) = 6 → slot 6\",\"13: h(13,0) = 6 collision; h(13,1) = (6+1) mod 7 = 0 → slot 0\",\"Table: [0]=13, [1]=–, [2]=72, [3]=65, [4]=18, [5]=–, [6]=34\"]},{\"h\":\"Quadratic probing example (same keys, lecture formula h1 + i + i²)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"hash\",\"size\":7,\"method\":\"quadratic\",\"keys\":[18,72,65,34,13]},\"formula\":[\"18 → 4; 72 → 2\",\"65: i=0 → 2 collision; i=1 → (2+1+1) mod 7 = 4 collision; i=2 → (2+2+4) mod 7 = 1 → slot 1\",\"34 → 6\",\"13: i=0 → 6 collision; i=1 → (6+2) mod 7 = 1 collision; i=2 → (6+6) mod 7 = 5 → slot 5\",\"Table: [0]=–, [1]=65, [2]=72, [3]=–, [4]=18, [5]=13, [6]=34\"]},{\"h\":\"Double hashing example (69, 79, 72, 98, 14; size 13)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"hash\",\"size\":13,\"method\":\"double\",\"h2\":11,\"keys\":[69,79,72,98,14]},\"formula\":[\"h(k,i) = (k mod 13 + i·(1 + k mod 11)) mod 13\",\"69 → 69 mod 13 = 4; 79 → 1; 72 → 7\",\"98: i=0 → 7 collision; h2 = 1 + 98 mod 11 = 11; i=1 → (7+11) mod 13 = 5 → slot 5\",\"14: i=0 → 1 collision; h2 = 1 + 14 mod 11 = 4; i=1 → 5 collision; i=2 → (1+8) mod 13 = 9 → slot 9\",\"Table: [1]=79, [4]=69, [5]=98, [7]=72, [9]=14\"]},{\"h\":\"Separate chaining example (18, 72, 65, 34, 13; size 7)\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"hash\",\"size\":7,\"method\":\"chain\",\"keys\":[18,72,65,34,13]},\"formula\":[\"h(k) = k mod n: addresses 4, 2, 2, 6, 6\",\"[0] → NULL\",\"[1] → NULL\",\"[2] → 72 → 65 → NULL\",\"[3] → NULL\",\"[4] → 18 → NULL\",\"[5] → NULL\",\"[6] → 34 → 13 → NULL\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Hash table\",\"A fixed-size array where each key is stored at the index computed by a hash function (also called a scatter table).\"],[\"Hash function\",\"A function H that transforms a key into a table index in 0..size-1.\"],[\"Collision\",\"When the hash function maps two different keys to the same cell.\"],[\"Four types of hash function\",\"Truncation, folding, mid square, division.\"],[\"Truncation\",\"Use only part of the key (e.g. last 3 digits). More collisions.\"],[\"Folding\",\"Split the key into digit groups and add them: 143765980 → 1888.\"],[\"Mid square\",\"Square the key and take the middle digits: 456 → 207936 → 79.\"],[\"Division method\",\"Address = key mod table size; one of the best methods.\"],[\"Linear probing formula\",\"h(k,i) = (h1(k) + i) mod m\"],[\"Quadratic probing (lecture formula)\",\"h(k,i) = (h1(k) + i + i²) mod m\"],[\"Double hashing formula\",\"h(k,i) = (h1(k) + i·h2(k)) mod m\"],[\"Five collision resolution techniques\",\"Linear probing, quadratic probing, double hashing, rehashing, extendible hashing.\"],[\"Separate chaining\",\"Array of pointers to linked lists; keys with the same address are chained.\"],[\"Advantage of separate chaining\",\"Unlimited collisions per address; no prior knowledge of the number of elements needed.\"],[\"Why use a prime table size (7, 11, 13)?\",\"Spreads keys more evenly with the mod function.\"],[\"Do hash tables store keys in order?\",\"No; positions depend on the hash function.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Define hashing, hash function and collision.\",\"Hashing stores elements in a fixed-size array (hash table, scatter table) at an index computed from the key. The hash function H takes a key and transforms it into an index from 0 to table size - 1, ideally distributing keys evenly; the same function is used to store and retrieve. A collision occurs when the hash function maps two different keys to the same cell.\"],[\"Explain the four types of hash functions with examples.\",\"Truncation: take part of the key (987456 → 456 for size 1000), simple but many collisions. Folding: split into groups and add (143765980 → 143+765+980 = 1888). Mid square: square and take the middle digits (456² = 207936 → 79). Division: key mod table size (17 mod 9 = 8), one of the best methods.\"],[\"List the collision resolution techniques and explain linear probing.\",\"Linear probing, quadratic probing, double hashing, rehashing, extendible hashing (and separate chaining). Linear probing places a colliding key in the next available empty cell searching sequentially: h(k,i) = (h1(k)+i) mod m, i = 0,1,2,…; searching also starts at the hash address and continues linearly.\"],[\"Compare linear probing, quadratic probing and double hashing.\",\"All are open addressing. Linear probing steps 1, 2, 3 … cells ahead ((h1+i) mod m), which causes clustering. Quadratic probing jumps further each time (lecture: (h1+i+i²) mod m), reducing clustering. Double hashing uses a second hash function for the step size ((h1 + i·h2) mod m), so different keys get different probe sequences.\"],[\"Explain separate chaining and its advantages.\",\"The table is an array of pointers, each pointing to a linked list holding all keys with that hash address. On a collision the key is added to that chain. It handles an unlimited number of collisions at the same address and needs no prior knowledge of how many elements will be stored.\"],[\"Store 5, 17, 37, 20, 42, 3 in an array of size 9 using the division method, then insert 11.\",\"5 mod 9 = 5, 17 mod 9 = 8, 37 mod 9 = 1, 20 mod 9 = 2, 42 mod 9 = 6, 3 mod 9 = 3. Table: [1]=37, [2]=20, [3]=3, [5]=5, [6]=42, [8]=17. 11 mod 9 = 2 is occupied by 20: a collision, which must be resolved (e.g. linear probing puts 11 in slot 4; chaining links it after 20).\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"Hash tables store keys in ordered locations.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The statement is false: the hash function scatters keys over the table (it is also called a scatter table), so cells are not in key order.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Keys are scattered according to the hash function, not sorted (hence \\\"scatter table\\\").\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"True or False: \\\"When a hash function computes the same location for two or more keys, this is a collision situation.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. This is the definition of collision.\"],[\"False\",\"Same location for different keys is a collision.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"Using the division method with table size 11, key 25 hashes to:\",\"o\":[[\"2\",\"2 is the quotient, not the remainder: 25 = 2 × 11 + 3, so 25 mod 11 = 3.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. The division method takes key mod table size: 25 mod 11 = 25 − 2 × 11 = 3, so 25 goes to index 3 (L10, Division method).\"],[\"11\",\"11 is the table size; valid indices are 0 to 10, and key mod 11 can never equal 11.\"],[\"25\",\"25 is the key itself, which is outside the table (indices 0..10). The address is 25 mod 11 = 3.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2022/23\"},{\"q\":\"Keys 14, 17, 25, 37, 34, 16 are stored in a table of size 11 with division and linear probing. Where is 16 stored?\",\"o\":[[\"5\",\"16 mod 11 = 5 is only the first try: 37 already sits in slot 5 (37 → 4 taken by 25 → 5), so 16 must probe on.\"],[\"6\",\"Slot 6 is the second try, but it holds 17 (17 mod 11 = 6). Linear probing moves on to slot 7, which is free.\"],[\"7\",\"Correct. 5 (37) and 6 (17) are taken, so i = 2 gives slot 7.\"],[\"9\",\"That would be standard quadratic probing (5 + 4).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2023/24 Summer\"},{\"q\":\"Keys 12, 15, 21, 31, 90, table size 9, division + linear probing. Where does 31 go?\",\"o\":[[\"4\",\"31 mod 9 = 4 is the first try, but 21 is already there (21 → 3 collided with 12 → moved to 4). Probe on to 5.\"],[\"5\",\"Correct. 12→3, 15→6, 21→3 collides → 4, 31→4 collides → 5.\"],[\"3\",\"Slot 3 holds 12 (12 mod 9 = 3), and 31 hashes to 4 anyway; linear probing only moves forward from there.\"],[\"0\",\"Linear probing for 31 starts at 4 and moves forward, finding 5 free; slot 0 is later taken by 90 (90 mod 9 = 0).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2023/24 Summer\"},{\"q\":\"Keys 25, 42, 96, 101, 102, 162, 197 hashed by division with size 5 and separate chaining. How long is the chain at index 2?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"Only one key would mean a single key with remainder 2, but 42, 102, 162 and 197 all give 2 mod 5.\"],[\"2\",\"2 counts only 42 and 102. 162 mod 5 = 2 and 197 mod 5 = 2 too, so the chain has 4 nodes.\"],[\"4\",\"Correct. 42, 102, 162 and 197 all give remainder 2.\"],[\"3\",\"3 misses one key: 197 = 39 × 5 + 2, so it also joins index 2 with 42, 102 and 162.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Keys 18, 72, 65, 34, 13 in a table of size 7 with linear probing. Where is 13 stored?\",\"o\":[[\"6\",\"Slot 6 is 13's home address (13 mod 7 = 6), but 34 is already there (34 mod 7 = 6), so 13 must probe.\"],[\"0\",\"Correct. 13 mod 7 = 6 collides, (6+1) mod 7 = 0.\"],[\"5\",\"5 is where the lecture's quadratic probing puts 13 (6 + 1 + 1 = 8 → 1 taken, then 6 + 2 + 4 = 12 → 5). Linear probing tries (6 + 1) mod 7 = 0.\"],[\"1\",\"Linear probing tries h + 1 first: (6 + 1) mod 7 = 0, and slot 0 is empty, so it stops there before reaching 1.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"Which hash function squares the key and takes the middle digits?\",\"o\":[[\"Truncation\",\"Truncation takes the rightmost (or leftmost) digits of the key directly, with no squaring.\"],[\"Folding\",\"Folding breaks the key into groups of digits and adds them (e.g. 143 + 765 + 980 = 1888); it does not square.\"],[\"Mid square\",\"Correct. The mid square method squares the key and uses the middle 2 or 3 digits as the address: 456² = 207936 → middle digits 79 (L10, Mid square method).\"],[\"Division\",\"The division method uses the remainder key mod table size; it does not square the key.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which collision technique uses an array of pointers to linked lists?\",\"o\":[[\"Linear probing\",\"Linear probing stores keys in the array itself.\"],[\"Separate chaining\",\"Correct. In separate chaining the hash table is an array of pointers, each pointing to a linked list that holds all keys with that hash address (L10, Separate chaining).\"],[\"Double hashing\",\"Double hashing uses a second hash function in the same array.\"],[\"Truncation\",\"Truncation is a hash function, not a collision technique.\"]],\"a\":1}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"Using the truncation method with a table of size 100 (rightmost digits), key 123456 maps to:\",\"o\":[[\"12\",\"12 takes the leftmost two digits. With the rightmost-digits rule, size 100 uses the last two digits: 56.\"],[\"56\",\"Correct. Size 100 needs 2 digits: the last two are 56.\"],[\"456\",\"456 is three digits, which is what a table of size 1000 uses (indices 0..999). Size 100 needs only 2 digits.\"],[\"34\",\"34 are the middle digits of 123456; truncation keeps the rightmost digits (56), not the middle.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Folding key 123456789 into groups of 3 digits gives:\",\"o\":[[\"1368\",\"Correct. Folding splits the key into groups of 3 digits and adds them: 123 + 456 + 789 = 1368 (L10, Folding method).\"],[\"1888\",\"1888 is the lecture's folding result for a different key, 143765980 (143 + 765 + 980). Here: 123 + 456 + 789.\"],[\"579\",\"579 = 123 + 456 adds only the first two groups; the third group 789 must be added too: 1368.\"],[\"369\",\"369 is the last digit of each group (3, 6, 9). Folding adds the whole groups: 123 + 456 + 789 = 1368.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Mid square for key 345 (345² = 119025), taking the middle 2 digits:\",\"o\":[[\"11\",\"11 are the first two digits of 119025; mid square takes the middle digits.\"],[\"25\",\"25 are the last two digits (that would be truncation). Mid square takes the middle two: 90.\"],[\"19\",\"19 is shifted one digit left of center. 119025 has six digits, so the middle two are the 3rd and 4th: 9 and 0.\"],[\"90\",\"Correct. 11|90|25: the middle two digits are 90.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Keys 10, 17, 24 into a table of size 7 using the lecture's quadratic probing h(k,i) = (k mod 7 + i + i²) mod 7. Where does 24 go?\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"Slot 3 is 24's home address (24 mod 7 = 3), but 10 already occupies it (10 mod 7 = 3).\"],[\"5\",\"Slot 5 is the i = 1 try (3 + 1 + 1), but 17 was placed there after colliding at 3. So 24 goes on to i = 2.\"],[\"2\",\"Correct. i=0 → 3 taken, i=1 → 5 taken, i=2 → (3 + 2 + 4) mod 7 = 2.\"],[\"4\",\"4 is h + 1, the linear probing step. The lecture's quadratic formula adds i + i², so the tries are 3, 5, then 2.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Double hashing with h1 = k mod 13 and h2 = 1 + (k mod 11): slot 1 is occupied by 27. Where does 40 go?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"Slot 1 is 40's primary address (40 mod 13 = 1), but 27 is there (27 mod 13 = 1), so double hashing must step.\"],[\"2\",\"Slot 2 = 1 + 1 is the linear probing step. Double hashing steps by h2 = 1 + (40 mod 11) = 8.\"],[\"8\",\"8 is the step size h2(40) = 1 + 7. The slot is (h1 + 1 · h2) mod 13 = (1 + 8) mod 13 = 9.\"],[\"9\",\"Correct. h2 = 1 + 40 mod 11 = 8; (1 + 1·8) mod 13 = 9.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which collision method needs no prior knowledge of how many elements will be stored?\",\"o\":[[\"Separate chaining\",\"Correct. The lecture says separate chaining handles an unlimited number of collisions at one address and needs no prior knowledge of how many elements will be stored, because chains are linked lists that grow (L10, Separate chaining).\"],[\"Linear probing\",\"Linear probing stores every key in the fixed-size array itself, so the table size limits how many keys fit.\"],[\"Quadratic probing\",\"Quadratic probing also stores keys in the array itself, so it is limited by the table size.\"],[\"Double hashing\",\"Double hashing is open addressing too: keys go in the array cells, so the number of keys is limited by the table size.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Why does the truncation method cause more collisions?\",\"o\":[[\"Because it squares the key\",\"Squaring the key is the mid square method; truncation just cuts off digits.\"],[\"Because it uses only part of the key, so keys sharing those digits collide\",\"Correct. Truncation keeps only some digits (e.g. the last 3 for size 1000), so all keys that share those digits get the same address; the lecture says the chances of collision are more in this method (L10, Truncation method).\"],[\"Because it uses a prime number\",\"Truncation does not use a prime; the prime modulus belongs to the division examples, and it spreads keys better.\"],[\"Because it adds digit groups\",\"Adding groups of digits is the folding method, not truncation.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Keys 7, 14, 21 in a table of size 7 with linear probing. Final positions?\",\"o\":[[\"0, 1, 2\",\"Correct. All hash to 0; 14 moves to 1, 21 moves to 2.\"],[\"0, 0, 0\",\"Open addressing stores one key per cell; colliding keys move to the next empty cell (1, then 2), they do not share 0.\"],[\"7, 14, 21\",\"7, 14 and 21 are the keys, not addresses; the table has indices 0..6, and all three keys hash to 0.\"],[\"0, 2, 4\",\"0, 2, 4 steps by 2. Linear probing steps by 1: (0 + 1) mod 7 = 1 for 14, then 2 for 21.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"To search for a key with linear probing, you:\",\"o\":[[\"Check only the hash address\",\"Checking only the hash address misses keys that collided and were placed further on. The search continues linearly.\"],[\"Search the whole table from index 0\",\"The search does not start at index 0; it starts at the key's hash address and moves forward from there.\"],[\"Start at the hash address and continue linearly until found or an empty cell\",\"Correct. The lecture checks the hash address of the key first; if the key is not there, it searches linearly in the following positions (stopping at the key or an empty cell) (L10, Linear probing).\"],[\"Use a second hash function\",\"A second hash function is used by double hashing; linear probing just steps one cell at a time.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT one of the five collision resolution techniques listed in the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"Rehashing\",\"Rehashing is listed: the five techniques are linear probing, quadratic probing, double hashing, rehashing and extendible hashing.\"],[\"Extendible hashing\",\"Extendible hashing is the fifth technique in the lecture's list of collision resolution techniques.\"],[\"Double hashing\",\"Double hashing is the third technique in the list, and one of the three the lecture covers in detail.\"],[\"Mid square\",\"Correct. Mid square is a hash function, not a collision resolution technique.\"]],\"a\":3}]}],\"exams\":[{\"title\":\"Final Exam 2025/2026\",\"meta\":\"First term · 2 hours · 60 marks · CS201 · Dr. Ahmed Ibrahim, Dr. Lamiaa Hassaan · 2 questions\",\"note\":\"Transcribed from two copies of the same paper (two phone photos and the \\\"2026 final exam\\\" PDF scan). One copy has a student's traversal answers and grader marks (-1 on Q1 parts 1 to 3 and -2 on the tree), which we ignored. All answers below are ours; code was compiled and run with g++ (the lectures use Visual Studio style <code>void main()</code> and <code>stdafx.h</code>, which g++ does not accept). MCQ 1 is ambiguous: the expected answer is Queue (the lecture says a queue is \\\"open at both ends\\\"), but strictly \\\"insertion and deletion at both ends\\\" is a deque.\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Question 1 · Answer five only of the following questions\",\"marks\":\"30 marks (6 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Using array of struct write a program to calculate the total salary of each employee after adding the bonus and subtracting the deduction. Table: Dina 1000 / 100 / 50; Mohamed 2000 / 0 / 0; Hoda 3000 / 0 / 200 (Salary / Bonus / Deduction).\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\n#include &lt;string&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nstruct Employee {\\n    string name;\\n    float salary, bonus, deduction, total;\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    const int N = 3;\\n    Employee e[N];\\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; i++)\\n    {\\n        cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Enter name, salary, bonus, deduction: \\\";\\n        cin &gt;&gt; e[i].name &gt;&gt; e[i].salary &gt;&gt; e[i].bonus &gt;&gt; e[i].deduction;\\n        e[i].total = e[i].salary + e[i].bonus - e[i].deduction;\\n    }\\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; i++)\\n        cout &lt;&lt; e[i].name &lt;&lt; \\\" total salary = \\\" &lt;&lt; e[i].total &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>Output: Dina 1050, Mohamed 2000, Hoda 2800 (1000+100-50 = 1050; 2000+0-0 = 2000; 3000+0-200 = 2800). You may also initialize the array directly: <code>Employee e[3] = {{\\\"Dina\\\",1000,100,50},{\\\"Mohamed\\\",2000,0,0},{\\\"Hoda\\\",3000,0,200}};</code> (L1).\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lqueue\",\"title\":\"Insert 25 and 20 into the linked queue\",\"ops\":[\"enqueue 25\",\"enqueue 20\"]},\"q\":\"You have a Linked-Queue, write a program that contains: node class, linked queue class, a function to insert values 25 and 20, and main() to call the above function.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nclass Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node *next;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Queue\\n{\\n    Node *front, *rear;\\npublic:\\n    Queue() { front = NULL; rear = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return (front == NULL &amp;&amp; rear == NULL); }\\n    void enqueue(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        newnode-&gt;next = NULL;\\n        if (isEmpty()) { front = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n        else { rear-&gt;next = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n    }\\n    void display()\\n    {\\n        for (Node *t = front; t != NULL; t = t-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; t-&gt;data &lt;&lt; \\\" \\\";\\n        cout &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Queue q;\\n    q.enqueue(25);\\n    q.enqueue(20);\\n    q.display();      // 25 20   (front -&gt; 25 -&gt; 20 &lt;- rear)\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>(L7)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lstack\",\"title\":\"Delete an item from the linked stack (example values)\",\"ops\":[\"push 50\",\"push 70\",\"pop\"]},\"q\":\"You have a Linked-Stack, write a program which contains: node class and linked stack class, a function to delete an item from the stack, and main() to call the above function.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nclass Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node *next;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Stack\\n{\\n    Node *top;\\npublic:\\n    Stack() { top = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return top == NULL; }\\n    void push(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        newnode-&gt;next = top;\\n        top = newnode;\\n    }\\n    int pop()                       // delete an item\\n    {\\n        if (isEmpty()) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Stack is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return 0; }\\n        Node *temp = top;\\n        int value = top-&gt;data;\\n        top = top-&gt;next;\\n        delete temp;\\n        return value;\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Stack s;\\n    s.push(10);\\n    s.push(20);\\n    cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Deleted \\\" &lt;&lt; s.pop() &lt;&lt; endl;   // Deleted 20\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>(L6) Pop removes the top node (the last pushed): LIFO.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"You have a Double Linked-List, write a program that contains: node class and D-linked list class, a function to insert a value in its order, and main() to call the above functions.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nclass Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node *next, *prev;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; prev = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass DList\\n{\\npublic:\\n    Node *head;\\n    DList() { head = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return head == NULL; }\\n    void insertsorted(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        if (isEmpty())\\n            head = newnode;\\n        else if (item &lt; head-&gt;data)           // new first node\\n        {\\n            newnode-&gt;next = head;\\n            head-&gt;prev = newnode;\\n            head = newnode;\\n        }\\n        else\\n        {\\n            Node *temp = head;\\n            while (temp-&gt;next != NULL &amp;&amp; temp-&gt;next-&gt;data &lt; item)\\n                temp = temp-&gt;next;\\n            newnode-&gt;next = temp-&gt;next;\\n            newnode-&gt;prev = temp;\\n            if (temp-&gt;next != NULL) temp-&gt;next-&gt;prev = newnode;\\n            temp-&gt;next = newnode;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    void display()\\n    {\\n        for (Node *t = head; t != NULL; t = t-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; t-&gt;data &lt;&lt; \\\" \\\";\\n        cout &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    DList d;\\n    d.insertsorted(30); d.insertsorted(10); d.insertsorted(20);\\n    d.insertsorted(5);  d.insertsorted(40);\\n    d.display();          // 5 10 20 30 40\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>(L8) Tested with g++; it handles empty list, new smallest, middle and new largest values.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"bst\",\"title\":\"The three traversals of this tree\",\"tree\":\"F(B(A,D(C,E)),G(,I(H,)))\",\"ops\":[\"inorder\",\"preorder\",\"postorder\"]},\"q\":\"Traverse this tree using Inorder, Preorder and Postorder. Tree: F is the root; B is the left child of F with children A (left) and D (right); D has children C (left) and E (right); G is the right child of F; G has a right child I; I has a left child H.\",\"ans\":\"<b>Inorder</b> (left, root, right): A B C D E F G H I<br><b>Preorder</b> (root, left, right): F B A D C E G I H<br><b>Postorder</b> (left, right, root): A C E D B H I G F<br>(L9; verified with g++. Tip: in postorder the root F is last, in preorder it is first.)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"hash\",\"title\":\"Chaining, array size 5\",\"size\":5,\"method\":\"chain\",\"keys\":[25,42,96,101,102,162,197]},\"q\":\"Store the values 25, 42, 96, 101, 102, 162, 197 using division method of hashing. Apply chaining in collision situations. Array size = 5.\",\"ans\":\"h(k) = k mod 5: 25→0, 42→2, 96→1, 101→1, 102→2, 162→2, 197→2.<br><table><tr><th>Index</th><th>Chain</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>25 → NULL</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>96 → 101 → NULL</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>42 → 102 → 162 → 197 → NULL</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>NULL</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>NULL</td></tr></table>Collisions: 101 (with 96), 102, 162, 197 (with 42). Each is appended to the linked list of its slot (L10).\"}]},{\"title\":\"Question 2 · Choose the correct answer\",\"marks\":\"15 marks (1.5 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following data structures allows insertion and deletion from both ends?\",\"o\":[\"Stack\",\"Queue\",\"Linked list\",\"Array\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Expected answer: Queue, which the lecture describes as \\\"open at both ends\\\" (insert at the rear, delete at the front) (L3). Strictly, insertion and deletion at both ends is a deque, which is not among the options.\",\"ow\":[\"A stack inserts and deletes at one end only, the top (L2), so it is not open at both ends.\",null,\"A linked list can insert and delete at any position, but the expected answer is the queue, which the lecture calls \\\"open at both ends\\\".\",\"A plain array has no insertion or deletion ends; stacks and queues are built on it with TOP or front/rear indices.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Assuming int is of 4 bytes, what is the size of int arr[15];?\",\"o\":[\"15\",\"19\",\"11\",\"60\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"An array occupies number of elements × size of one element: 15 × 4 bytes = 60 bytes (L1, Array-based representation).\",\"ow\":[\"15 is the number of elements, not the number of bytes; each int takes 4 bytes.\",\"19 adds the count and the element size (15 + 4); the size is count × element size.\",\"11 subtracts the element size from the count (15 − 4), which has no meaning; the size is 15 × 4.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"In general, the index of the first element in an array is ____\",\"o\":[\"1\",\"0\",\"2\",\"-1\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"C++ arrays are zero-based: the ith element is at position i − 1, so indices run from 0 to size − 1 and the first element is at index 0 (L1, Array-based representation).\",\"ow\":[\"Matrices in math are indexed from 1, but C++ arrays are zero-based: location(i) = i − 1.\",null,\"No C++ array starts at index 2; the first element is at index 0.\",\"−1 is the \\\"empty\\\" value of TOP or front/rear in stacks and queues, not a valid array index.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following data structures is a linear type?\",\"o\":[\"Graph\",\"Binary Tree\",\"Tree\",\"Linked list\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Graphs and trees are non-linear; a linked list is linear (L1).\",\"ow\":[\"A graph is non-linear: its elements form a network, not a single sequence.\",\"A binary tree is a tree, so its nodes form a hierarchy (non-linear).\",\"A tree arranges elements hierarchically (parent and children), so it is non-linear.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Process of removing an element from stack is called ____\",\"o\":[\"Create\",\"Push\",\"Evaluation\",\"Pop\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Pop is the process of deleting the element at the top of the stack; after each pop, top is decremented by one (L2, Operations on stack).\",\"ow\":[\"Create sets up a new empty stack (top = −1); it does not remove anything.\",\"Push is the opposite: it adds a new element at the top and increments top.\",\"Evaluation is not one of the stack operations; the basic operations are push and pop.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following data structures is a non-linear type?\",\"o\":[\"Array\",\"Stack\",\"Tree\",\"Queue\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"In a tree the elements are not in a single sequence but in a hierarchy (root, children, subtrees), so it is non-linear like a graph; arrays, stacks and queues are linear (L1, L9).\",\"ow\":[\"An array is linear (and static): its elements sit in one sequence.\",\"A stack is a linear dynamic structure: one sequence, accessed at the top.\",null,\"A queue is a linear dynamic structure: one sequence with a front and a rear.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What are the advantages of arrays?\",\"o\":[\"Easier to store elements of same data type\",\"Elements in an array cannot be sorted\",\"Index of first element of an array is 1\",\"Objects of mixed data types can be stored\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"B is false (arrays can be sorted), C is false (index starts at 0), D describes structs (L1).\",\"ow\":[null,\"False: arrays can be sorted (L1 has a sort example), and this would not be an advantage anyway.\",\"False: C++ arrays start at index 0, not 1.\",\"False: storing mixed data types under one name describes a struct, not an array.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Process of inserting an element in stack is called ____\",\"o\":[\"Create\",\"Push\",\"Evaluation\",\"Pop\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Push is the process of adding a new element to the top of the stack: top is first incremented, then the item is stored at stack[top] (L2, Operations on stack).\",\"ow\":[\"Create only sets up an empty stack with top = −1; it does not insert.\",null,\"Evaluation is not one of the stack operations; insertion is push.\",\"Pop is the opposite: it deletes the top element and decrements top.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"When search starts at the beginning of a list to check every element in that list, it is ……\",\"o\":[\"linear search\",\"binary search\",\"hash search\",\"binary tree search\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Linear (sequential) search checks elements one by one from the start (Lab 1, Lab 2).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Binary search does not check every element from the start; it repeatedly halves a sorted list by comparing with the middle element.\",\"Hash search computes the key's address with a hash function and goes there directly (L10), instead of scanning from the start.\",\"Binary tree search follows one path from the root, going left or right by comparison (L9); it does not check every element.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Linked list is considered as an example of ____ type of memory allocation.\",\"o\":[\"Static\",\"Dynamic\",\"Compile time\",\"Heap\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Nodes are created with new at run time: dynamic allocation (L4, L5).\",\"ow\":[\"Static allocation fixes the size before the program runs, as with arrays; linked list nodes are created at run time.\",null,\"Compile-time allocation is static allocation; linked list nodes are allocated with new at run time.\",\"Nodes do live on the heap, but the type of allocation asked for is dynamic (run-time) allocation.\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Question 2 · Choose true or false\",\"marks\":\"15 marks (1.5 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Insertions and deletions in the Stack follow FIFO.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Insertions and deletions in a stack both happen at the top, so the last element added is the first removed: LIFO, not FIFO (FIFO is the queue rule) (L2).\",\"ow\":[\"FIFO is the queue rule. In a stack both insertion and deletion happen at the top, so it is LIFO.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"The circular queue is the same as the regular queue.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A circular queue connects the last position to the first and reuses freed cells (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"A circular queue is not the same: its last position connects to the first, so cells freed by dequeue can be reused, unlike the regular (linear) queue.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Enqueue() inserts an element at the rear of the Queue.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"All insertions to a queue are made at one end, the tail (rear): enqueue increments tail and stores the item at queue[tail] (L3, What is a queue?).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: a queue inserts only at the rear (tail): tail = tail + 1; queue[tail] = item.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"The array is a group of data elements grouped together under one name with different types.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"That defines a struct; array elements have the same type (L1).\",\"ow\":[\"A group of elements under one name with different types is a struct. Array elements must all have the same type.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Dequeue(): removes the element at the front of the Queue.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"All deletions from a queue are made at the head (front): dequeue reads queue[head] and moves head forward, so the first element in is the first out (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: dequeue always removes the element at the front (head), the oldest one (FIFO).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"The last node in linked-list contains null in its second field because it will point to no node.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"This is Lecture 5's own sentence: each node has two fields (data and next), and the last node holds NULL in its next field because it points to no node; it marks the end of the list (L5).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: each node has data and next; the last node has no next node, so its next field is NULL.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Function push() in Stack used to delete item.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"push() inserts a new item at the top of the stack; deleting the top item is done by pop(), so the statement is false (L2, Push and Pop functions).\",\"ow\":[\"push() adds an item at the top (top = top + 1; stack[top] = item). Deleting is pop().\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Traversing a singly linked list is done in a forward direction only.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"In a singly linked list each node holds a reference only to the next node, so traversal goes forward from head; backward travel needs a doubly linked list's prev pointer (L5).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: a singly linked node has only a next pointer, so you can only move forward; backward needs the prev pointer of a doubly linked list.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"In a circular linked list, the last node points back to the head node.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The lecture defines a circular linked list as one where the last node points back to the head node instead of NULL, forming a circle; it can be singly or doubly linked (L5).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: that is exactly the lecture's definition; the last node points back to the head instead of NULL.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Insertions and deletions in Stack follow FILO.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"A stack inserts and deletes only at the top, so the first element pushed sits at the bottom and is the last to be popped: First-In-Last-Out, the same as LIFO (L2).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: First-In-Last-Out means the first element pushed is the last popped, which is the same as LIFO.\"]}]}]},{\"title\":\"Final Exam 2024/2025\",\"meta\":\"First term · 2 hours · 60 marks · CS201 · Dr. Ahmed Ibrahim, Dr. Lamiaa Hassaan · answer 5 of 6 (12 marks each)\",\"note\":\"Transcribed from a phone photo. A student wrote traversals for Q5 on the paper (pre A-B-D-G-H-E-I-C-F, in G-D-H-B-E-I-A-C-F, post G-H-D-I-E-B-F-C-A); they agree with our answer, which was also checked with g++. The tree structure was read from the drawing: I is the right child of E and F is the right child of C (consistent with the student's inorder).\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Answer five only of the following questions\",\"marks\":\"60 marks (12 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Using array of struct write a program to take the values of this table from the user and calculate the total salary of each employee after adding the bonus and subtracting the deduction. Dina 1000/50/30; Mohamed 2000/70/0; Abas 2000/40/20; Hoda 3000/0/300 (Salary/Bonus/Deduction).\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\n#include &lt;string&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nstruct Employee {\\n    string name;\\n    float salary, bonus, deduction, total;\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    const int N = 4;\\n    Employee e[N];\\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; i++)\\n    {\\n        cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Enter name, salary, bonus, deduction: \\\";\\n        cin &gt;&gt; e[i].name &gt;&gt; e[i].salary &gt;&gt; e[i].bonus &gt;&gt; e[i].deduction;\\n        e[i].total = e[i].salary + e[i].bonus - e[i].deduction;\\n    }\\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; i++)\\n        cout &lt;&lt; e[i].name &lt;&lt; \\\" total salary = \\\" &lt;&lt; e[i].total &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>With the table values: Dina 1000+50-30 = <b>1020</b>, Mohamed 2000+70-0 = <b>2070</b>, Abas 2000+40-20 = <b>2020</b>, Hoda 3000+0-300 = <b>2700</b> (run with g++) (L1).\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Write a program to remove all the items from a Double Linked-List, check if it is empty.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nclass Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node *next, *prev;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; prev = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass DList\\n{\\npublic:\\n    Node *head;\\n    DList() { head = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return head == NULL; }\\n    void insertatbeginning(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        newnode-&gt;next = head;\\n        newnode-&gt;prev = NULL;\\n        if (!isEmpty()) head-&gt;prev = newnode;\\n        head = newnode;\\n    }\\n    void removeAll()\\n    {\\n        if (isEmpty()) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"List is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return; }\\n        while (head != NULL)\\n        {\\n            Node *temp = head;\\n            head = head-&gt;next;\\n            if (head != NULL) head-&gt;prev = NULL;\\n            cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Deleted \\\" &lt;&lt; temp-&gt;data &lt;&lt; endl;\\n            delete temp;\\n        }\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    DList d;\\n    d.insertatbeginning(10); d.insertatbeginning(20); d.insertatbeginning(30);\\n    d.removeAll();      // Deleted 30, Deleted 20, Deleted 10\\n    d.removeAll();      // List is empty\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>(L8)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lstack\",\"title\":\"Push 50 and 70, then pop\",\"ops\":[\"push 50\",\"push 70\",\"pop\"]},\"q\":\"You have a Linked-Stack; check if it is empty, write a program to: insert two new nodes with values = 50 and 70; remove a node from the stack.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nclass Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node *next;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Stack\\n{\\n    Node *top;\\npublic:\\n    Stack() { top = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return top == NULL; }\\n    void push(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        if (isEmpty()) newnode-&gt;next = NULL;\\n        else newnode-&gt;next = top;\\n        top = newnode;\\n    }\\n    int pop()\\n    {\\n        if (isEmpty()) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Stack is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return 0; }\\n        Node *temp = top;\\n        int value = top-&gt;data;\\n        top = top-&gt;next;\\n        delete temp;\\n        return value;\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Stack s;\\n    if (s.isEmpty()) cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Stack is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    s.push(50);\\n    s.push(70);                                // top -&gt; 70 -&gt; 50 -&gt; NULL\\n    cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Removed \\\" &lt;&lt; s.pop() &lt;&lt; endl;    // Removed 70\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>(L6)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lqueue\",\"title\":\"Enqueue 30 and 50, then remove all\",\"ops\":[\"enqueue 30\",\"enqueue 50\",\"dequeue\",\"dequeue\"]},\"q\":\"You have a Linked-Queue; check if it is empty, write a program to: insert two new nodes with values = 30 and 50; remove all the items of the queue.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nclass Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node *next;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Queue\\n{\\n    Node *front, *rear;\\npublic:\\n    Queue() { front = rear = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return front == NULL; }\\n    void enqueue(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        newnode-&gt;next = NULL;\\n        if (isEmpty()) { front = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n        else { rear-&gt;next = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n    }\\n    int dequeue()\\n    {\\n        if (isEmpty()) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Queue is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return 0; }\\n        Node *temp = front;\\n        int value = front-&gt;data;\\n        front = front-&gt;next;\\n        if (front == NULL) rear = NULL;      // queue became empty\\n        delete temp;\\n        return value;\\n    }\\n    void removeAll()\\n    {\\n        while (!isEmpty())\\n            cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Dequeued \\\" &lt;&lt; dequeue() &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Queue q;\\n    if (q.isEmpty()) cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Queue is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    q.enqueue(30);\\n    q.enqueue(50);\\n    q.removeAll();       // Dequeued 30, Dequeued 50 (FIFO)\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>(L7)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"bst\",\"title\":\"The three traversals of this tree\",\"tree\":\"A(B(D(G,H),E(,I)),C(,F))\",\"ops\":[\"inorder\",\"preorder\",\"postorder\"]},\"q\":\"Traverse this tree using Inorder, Preorder and Postorder. Tree: A root; B left child of A, C right child of A; D left and E right children of B; F right child of C; G left and H right children of D; I right child of E.\",\"ans\":\"<b>Inorder</b> (L, root, R): G D H B E I A C F<br><b>Preorder</b> (root, L, R): A B D G H E I C F<br><b>Postorder</b> (L, R, root): G H D I E B F C A<br>(L9; verified with g++.)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":[{\"type\":\"hash\",\"title\":\"Linear probing, array size 7\",\"size\":7,\"method\":\"linear\",\"keys\":[18,72,65,34,13]},{\"type\":\"hash\",\"title\":\"Separate chaining, array size 7\",\"size\":7,\"method\":\"chain\",\"keys\":[18,72,65,34,13]}],\"q\":\"Store the values 18, 72, 65, 34 and 13 using division method. Apply using linear probe and separate chaining in collision situations, where array size = 7.\",\"ans\":\"Division: h1(k) = k mod 7 → 18→4, 72→2, 65→2, 34→6, 13→6.<br><b>Linear probing</b> h(k,i) = (h1(k)+i) mod 7:<br>18: i=0 → 4. 72: i=0 → 2. 65: i=0 → 2 collision, i=1 → 3. 34: i=0 → 6. 13: i=0 → 6 collision, i=1 → (6+1) mod 7 = 0.<br><table><tr><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>–</td><td>72</td><td>65</td><td>18</td><td>–</td><td>34</td></tr></table><b>Separate chaining</b>: [0] NULL, [1] NULL, [2] 72 → 65, [3] NULL, [4] 18, [5] NULL, [6] 34 → 13. (This is exactly the lecture example, L10.)\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Summer Exam 2023/2024 (60 marks)\",\"meta\":\"Summer term · 2 hours · 60 marks · CS201 · Dr. Ahmed Ibrahim, Dr. Lamiaa Hassaan · answer 5 of 6 (12 marks each)\",\"note\":\"Transcribed from a phone photo. A student wrote traversals and the start of a linear probing answer on the paper; the left child of 50 is hidden under the handwriting, but it is 25 (the student's inorder 12, 25, 33, 50 … and the lecture's example tree 50, 25, 65, 12, 33, 70, 55, 60, 53 confirm it). The student wrote h(12,0) = ((12 % 9)+1) % 9, which is wrong for i = 0 (it should be + 0); the final slots they wrote (3, 6, 4, 5, 0) are correct.\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Answer five only of the following questions\",\"marks\":\"60 marks (12 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Using array of struct write a program to take the values of this table from the user and calculate the average value of the subject degrees (sub1, sub2). Ahmed 60/70; Mohamed 80/60; Abas 50/40.5; Hoda 70.5/75.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\n#include &lt;string&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nstruct Student {\\n    string name;\\n    float sub1, sub2;\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    const int N = 4;\\n    Student s[N];\\n    float sum1 = 0, sum2 = 0;\\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; i++)\\n    {\\n        cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Enter name, sub1, sub2: \\\";\\n        cin &gt;&gt; s[i].name &gt;&gt; s[i].sub1 &gt;&gt; s[i].sub2;\\n        sum1 += s[i].sub1;\\n        sum2 += s[i].sub2;\\n        cout &lt;&lt; s[i].name &lt;&lt; \\\" average = \\\" &lt;&lt; (s[i].sub1 + s[i].sub2) / 2 &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    }\\n    cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Average of sub1 = \\\" &lt;&lt; sum1 / N &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Average of sub2 = \\\" &lt;&lt; sum2 / N &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>Subject averages: sub1 = (60+80+50+70.5)/4 = 260.5/4 = <b>65.125</b>; sub2 = (70+60+40.5+75)/4 = 245.5/4 = <b>61.375</b>. Since the question can be read as the average of each student too, the program also prints Ahmed 65, Mohamed 70, Abas 45.25, Hoda 72.75 (L1).\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Write the functions to remove all the items from a Linked-List.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>// inside class Linkedlist (Node has data and next)\\nint deletefrombeginning()\\n{\\n    if (isEmpty()) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return 0; }\\n    Node *temp = head;\\n    int value = head-&gt;data;\\n    head = head-&gt;next;\\n    delete temp;\\n    return value;\\n}\\nvoid removeAll()\\n{\\n    if (isEmpty()) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"List is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return; }\\n    while (!isEmpty())\\n        cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Deleted \\\" &lt;&lt; deletefrombeginning() &lt;&lt; endl;\\n}</pre>Each step moves head to the next node and frees the old first node, until head is NULL (L5).\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lstack\",\"title\":\"Remove all the items (example values)\",\"ops\":[\"push 10\",\"push 20\",\"push 30\",\"pop\",\"pop\",\"pop\"]},\"q\":\"You have a Linked-Stack; write a program to remove all the items of the stack.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nclass Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node *next;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Stack\\n{\\n    Node *top;\\npublic:\\n    Stack() { top = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return top == NULL; }\\n    void push(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        newnode-&gt;next = top;\\n        top = newnode;\\n    }\\n    int pop()\\n    {\\n        if (isEmpty()) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return 0; }\\n        Node *temp = top;\\n        int value = top-&gt;data;\\n        top = top-&gt;next;\\n        delete temp;\\n        return value;\\n    }\\n    void removeAll()\\n    {\\n        while (!isEmpty())\\n            cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Popped \\\" &lt;&lt; pop() &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Stack s;\\n    s.push(1); s.push(2); s.push(3);\\n    s.removeAll();      // Popped 3, Popped 2, Popped 1\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>(L6)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lqueue\",\"title\":\"Enqueue 50 and 70, then dequeue\",\"ops\":[\"enqueue 50\",\"enqueue 70\",\"dequeue\"]},\"q\":\"You have a Linked-Queue, write a program to: insert two new nodes with values = 50 and 70; remove a node from the queue.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>// Node class as in L7 (data, next)\\nclass Queue\\n{\\n    Node *front, *rear;\\npublic:\\n    Queue() { front = rear = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return front == NULL; }\\n    void enqueue(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        newnode-&gt;next = NULL;\\n        if (isEmpty()) { front = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n        else { rear-&gt;next = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n    }\\n    int dequeue()\\n    {\\n        if (isEmpty()) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return 0; }\\n        Node *temp = front;\\n        int value = front-&gt;data;\\n        front = front-&gt;next;\\n        if (front == NULL) rear = NULL;\\n        delete temp;\\n        return value;\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Queue q;\\n    q.enqueue(50);\\n    q.enqueue(70);\\n    cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Removed \\\" &lt;&lt; q.dequeue() &lt;&lt; endl;   // Removed 50 (FIFO)\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>(L7)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"bst\",\"title\":\"The three traversals of this tree\",\"init\":[50,25,65,12,33,70,55,60,53],\"ops\":[\"inorder\",\"preorder\",\"postorder\"]},\"q\":\"Traverse this tree using Inorder, Preorder and Postorder. Tree: 50 root; 25 (children 12, 33); 65 (children 55, 70); 55 (children 53, 60).\",\"ans\":\"<b>Inorder</b>: 12, 25, 33, 50, 53, 55, 60, 65, 70<br><b>Preorder</b>: 50, 25, 12, 33, 65, 55, 53, 60, 70<br><b>Postorder</b>: 12, 33, 25, 53, 60, 55, 70, 65, 50<br>(Lecture 9 example; verified with g++.)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Store the values 12, 15, 21, 31, 90 using division method. Apply linear probe in collision situations, where array size = 9.\",\"ans\":\"h(k,i) = ((k mod 9) + i) mod 9<br>12: i=0 → 3.<br>15: i=0 → 6.<br>21: i=0 → 3 collision (12); i=1 → 4.<br>31: i=0 → 4 collision (21); i=1 → 5.<br>90: i=0 → 0.<br><table><tr><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th></tr><tr><td>90</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>12</td><td>21</td><td>31</td><td>15</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr></table>(L10; computed with a script.)\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Summer Exam 2023/2024 (50 marks)\",\"meta\":\"Summer term · 2 hours · 50 marks · CS201 · Dr. Ahmed Ibrahim, Dr. Lamiaa Hassaan · answer 5 of 6 (10 marks each)\",\"note\":\"Transcribed from the scanned PDF \\\"2023 2024\\\". The header says Summer Term 2023-2024 with 50 marks; it is a different form from the 60-mark summer paper above. Q6 is cut at the right edge (\\\"Apply linear prob…\\\"); it clearly asks for linear probing.\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Answer five only of the following questions\",\"marks\":\"50 marks (10 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Using array of struct write a program to take the values of this table from the user and calculate the total salary of each employee after adding the bonus and subtracting the deduction. Dina 1000/100/50; Mohamed 2000/0/0; Abas 2000/100/0; Hoda 3000/0/200.\",\"ans\":\"Same program as in Final 2024/25 Q1 (struct Employee {name, salary, bonus, deduction, total}; loop reading N = 4 records; total = salary + bonus - deduction; print).<br>Results: Dina 1000+100-50 = <b>1050</b>; Mohamed <b>2000</b>; Abas 2000+100-0 = <b>2100</b>; Hoda 3000-200 = <b>2800</b> (run with g++) (L1).\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Write a function to insert a new node in its order of a Linked-List.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>void insertsorted(int item)             // ascending order\\n{\\n    Node *newnode = new Node();\\n    newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n    if (isEmpty() || item &lt; head-&gt;data)   // empty list or new smallest\\n    {\\n        newnode-&gt;next = head;\\n        head = newnode;\\n    }\\n    else\\n    {\\n        Node *temp = head;                  // find node before insertion point\\n        while (temp-&gt;next != NULL &amp;&amp; temp-&gt;next-&gt;data &lt; item)\\n            temp = temp-&gt;next;\\n        newnode-&gt;next = temp-&gt;next;         // new node points to the following node\\n        temp-&gt;next = newnode;               // previous node points to new node\\n    }\\n}</pre>Steps (L5): locate the node preceding the insertion point, make the new node point to the node after it, then make the preceding node point to the new node. The slide's loop version crashes when the value is smaller than the head or larger than the last node, so this safe version is preferred (tested with g++: 10, 20, 30 + 25, 5, 40 → 5 10 20 25 30 40).\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lstack\",\"title\":\"Push 50 and 70, then pop\",\"ops\":[\"push 50\",\"push 70\",\"pop\"]},\"q\":\"You have a Linked-Stack, write a program to: insert two new nodes with values = 50 and 70; remove a node from the linked-stack.\",\"ans\":\"Use the linked stack class of L6 (push: newnode->next = top; top = newnode; pop: temp = top; top = top->next; delete temp).<pre>int main()\\n{\\n    Stack s;\\n    s.push(50);\\n    s.push(70);                               // top -&gt; 70 -&gt; 50\\n    cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Removed \\\" &lt;&lt; s.pop() &lt;&lt; endl;   // Removed 70\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>See Final 2024/25 Q3 for the full class.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lqueue\",\"title\":\"Remove all the items (example values)\",\"ops\":[\"enqueue 10\",\"enqueue 20\",\"enqueue 30\",\"dequeue\",\"dequeue\",\"dequeue\"]},\"q\":\"You have a Linked-Queue, write a program to remove all the items of the queue.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>void removeAll()            // inside class Queue (front, rear)\\n{\\n    if (front == NULL) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Queue is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return; }\\n    while (front != NULL)\\n    {\\n        Node *temp = front;\\n        cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Deleted \\\" &lt;&lt; temp-&gt;data &lt;&lt; endl;\\n        front = front-&gt;next;\\n        delete temp;\\n    }\\n    rear = NULL;            // queue is now empty\\n}</pre>Items are removed from the front in FIFO order (L7). Full class: see Final 2024/25 Q4.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"bst\",\"title\":\"The three traversals of this tree\",\"init\":[43,31,64,20,40,56,89,28,33,47,59],\"ops\":[\"inorder\",\"preorder\",\"postorder\"]},\"q\":\"Traverse this tree using Inorder, Preorder and Postorder. Tree: 43; 31 (20 with right child 28, 40 with left child 33); 64 (56 with children 47, 59; 89).\",\"ans\":\"<b>Inorder</b>: 20 28 31 33 40 43 47 56 59 64 89<br><b>Preorder</b>: 43 31 20 28 40 33 64 56 47 59 89<br><b>Postorder</b>: 28 20 33 40 31 47 59 56 89 64 43<br>(Lecture 9 example; verified with g++.)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Store the values 14, 17, 25, 37, 34, 16 using division method. Apply linear probe in collision situations, where array size = 11.\",\"ans\":\"h(k,i) = ((k mod 11) + i) mod 11<br>14 → 3. 17 → 6. 25 → 3 collision; i=1 → 4. 37 → 4 collision; i=1 → 5. 34 → 1. 16 → 5 collision; i=1 → 6 collision; i=2 → 7.<br><table><tr><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th><th>9</th><th>10</th></tr><tr><td>–</td><td>34</td><td>–</td><td>14</td><td>25</td><td>37</td><td>17</td><td>16</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr></table>(L10)\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Midterm 2023/2024 (Models A and B)\",\"meta\":\"First term midterm · 1 hour · 20 marks · CS201 · Dr. Ahmed Ibrahim, Dr. Lamia Hassaan · official model answers\",\"note\":\"Transcribed from the official model answer PDF (text). Fill-in-the-blank items are shown as MCQs with the model answer as the correct option. Problems we found in the official key: (1) Model A Q2c defines insertatend but main calls list.insertatbeginning(7) and (5), which would not compile, and calling insertatbeginning(7) then (5) would give 5 → 7, not the required 7 → 5. Also insertatend as written crashes on an empty list. Our answer uses insertatend with an empty-list check. (2) Model B Q2c makes Node::data a float but insertatbeginning(int item) truncates 8.5 and 5.3 to 8 and 5, and main calls insertatend, which is not defined; inserting at the front 8.5 then 5.3 gives head → 5.3 → 8.5 as required. (3) Model B writes \\\"FILO\\\" for the stack; FILO and LIFO mean the same.\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Model A · First Question\",\"marks\":\"5 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Removing an element occurs at the ______ of the Queue.\",\"o\":[\"front\",\"rear\",\"top\",\"middle\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"A queue deletes at the head (front): the first entry ready to be served, which is the one that came in first (FIFO); dequeue reads queue[head] and moves head forward (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The rear (tail) is where enqueue adds new elements; removal happens at the other end, the front.\",\"Top is a stack term (stacks add and remove at the top); a queue removes at the front.\",\"A queue never removes from the middle; it deletes only at the front to keep FIFO order.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The disadvantage of array is ______.\",\"o\":[\"fixed size\",\"linear access\",\"extra memory for pointers\",\"slow index access\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Model answer: fixed size. Linear access and extra memory are linked list disadvantages (L1, L5).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Linear access (no direct index access) is a linked list disadvantage; arrays reach any element directly by index.\",\"Extra memory for pointers is a linked list disadvantage; an array stores only the data.\",\"Index access in an array is direct and fast; it is one of the array's strengths.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Function ______ in Stack is used to insert item.\",\"o\":[\"pop()\",\"push()\",\"get()\",\"count()\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"push() inserts a new item at the top of the stack: it checks isFull, then does top = top + 1; stack_arr[top] = item (L2, Push function).\",\"ow\":[\"pop() deletes the top item and decrements top; it does not insert.\",null,\"get() only shows the top element without changing the stack.\",\"count() only returns how many items are stored (top + 1).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Queue is a data structure in which insertions and deletions are restricted to one end.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Model answer: False; insertions at the rear and deletions at the front (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"Restricting both insertion and deletion to one end describes a stack. A queue inserts at the rear and deletes at the front.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"A Stack, in other words, is called a LIFO list.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"All insertions and deletions happen at the top, so the last element added is the first one removed; that is why the lecture calls a stack a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) list (L2).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: since insertion and deletion are both at the top, the last element added is the first removed (LIFO).\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Model A · Second Question\",\"marks\":\"15 marks (5 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"stack\",\"title\":\"pop() until the stack is empty\",\"size\":4,\"ops\":[\"push 10\",\"push 20\",\"pop\",\"pop\",\"pop\"]},\"q\":\"Write C++ code for POP and IsEmpty Stack functions.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>bool isEmpty(int top)\\n{\\n    if (top == -1) return true;\\n    else return false;\\n}\\nint pop()\\n{\\n    if (isEmpty(top)) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Stack underflow\\\"; return -1; }\\n    else\\n    { int item = stack_arr[top]; top = top - 1; return item; }\\n}</pre>(Official model answer, L2.)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"queue\",\"title\":\"enqueue() until isFull\",\"size\":3,\"ops\":[\"enqueue 10\",\"enqueue 20\",\"enqueue 30\",\"enqueue 40\"]},\"q\":\"Write C++ code for Enqueue and IsFull Queue functions.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>bool isFull(int tail)\\n{\\n    if (tail == maxsize - 1) return true;\\n    else return false;\\n}\\nvoid enqueue(int item)\\n{\\n    if (isFull(tail)) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"\\\\nQueue is full\\\"; return; }\\n    else\\n    {\\n        if (head == -1) head = 0;\\n        tail = tail + 1;\\n        queue[tail] = item;\\n    }\\n}</pre>(Official model answer, L3.)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Write node class, linked list class, insertion at the end function then use them to create the linked list: head → 7 → 5 → NULL (insert value 7 then value 5).\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nclass Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node *next;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Linkedlist\\n{\\npublic:\\n    Node *head;\\n    Linkedlist() { head = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return head == NULL; }\\n    void insertatend(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        newnode-&gt;next = NULL;\\n        if (isEmpty()) { head = newnode; return; }   // first node\\n        Node *temp1 = head;\\n        while (temp1-&gt;next != NULL) temp1 = temp1-&gt;next;\\n        temp1-&gt;next = newnode;\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Linkedlist list;\\n    list.insertatend(7);\\n    list.insertatend(5);       // head -&gt; 7 -&gt; 5 -&gt; NULL\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>Note: the official key calls insertatbeginning in main (a mismatch) and has no empty-list check in insertatend (L5).\"}]},{\"title\":\"Model B · First Question\",\"marks\":\"5 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"enqueue(): inserts an element at the ______ of the Queue.\",\"o\":[\"front\",\"rear\",\"top\",\"head\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"All insertions to a queue are made at the tail (rear): enqueue does tail = tail + 1 and stores the item at queue[tail]; deletions happen at the front (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"The front (head) is where dequeue removes elements, not where they are inserted.\",null,\"Top is the single end of a stack; a queue inserts at the rear.\",\"Head is another name for the front, where elements are removed.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The disadvantage of Linked List is ______.\",\"o\":[\"fixed size\",\"linear access and extra memory\",\"cannot grow\",\"cannot delete\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Model answer: linear access and extra memory (L5).\",\"ow\":[\"A fixed size is the array's disadvantage; a linked list grows and shrinks at run time.\",null,\"A linked list can grow as needed, since nodes are created with new at run time (dynamic size is its advantage).\",\"Deletion is easy in a linked list: only the neighbouring pointers change (deletion at the beginning, end or middle).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Function ______ in Stack is used to delete item.\",\"o\":[\"push()\",\"pop()\",\"enqueue()\",\"search()\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"pop() deletes the top item of the stack: it checks isEmpty, then reads stack_arr[top] and does top = top − 1, returning the item (L2, Pop function).\",\"ow\":[\"push() inserts an item at the top; it does not delete.\",null,\"enqueue() is a queue operation that inserts at the rear; a stack has no enqueue.\",\"search() only looks for an item from top to bottom; it does not remove it.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Stack is a data structure in which insertions and deletions are restricted to one end.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"In a stack, addition of new items and deletion of existing items are both done from only one end, the top; this is what makes it LIFO (L2, What is a stack?).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: a stack adds and removes elements only at one end, the top.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"A Stack is called a FIFO list.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A stack is LIFO (the key writes FILO, which means the same); FIFO is a queue (L2).\",\"ow\":[\"FIFO is the queue rule (insert at rear, delete at front). A stack inserts and deletes at the top, so it is LIFO/FILO.\",null]}]},{\"title\":\"Model B · Second Question\",\"marks\":\"15 marks (5 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"stack\",\"title\":\"push() until isFull\",\"size\":4,\"ops\":[\"push 10\",\"push 20\",\"push 30\",\"push 40\",\"push 50\"]},\"q\":\"Write C++ code for Push and IsFull Stack functions.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>bool isFull(int top)\\n{\\n    if (top == MAXSIZE - 1) return true;\\n    else return false;\\n}\\nvoid push(int item)\\n{\\n    if (isFull(top)) cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Stack overflow\\\";\\n    else { top = top + 1; stack_arr[top] = item; }\\n}</pre>(Official model answer, L2.)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"queue\",\"title\":\"dequeue() until isEmpty\",\"size\":4,\"ops\":[\"enqueue 10\",\"enqueue 20\",\"dequeue\",\"dequeue\",\"dequeue\"]},\"q\":\"Write C++ code for Dequeue and IsEmpty Queue functions.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>bool isEmpty(int head)\\n{\\n    if (head == -1 || head &gt; tail) return true;\\n    else return false;\\n}\\nint dequeue()\\n{\\n    int element;\\n    if (isEmpty(head)) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Queue Underflow \\\"; return -1; }\\n    else { element = queue[head]; head++; }\\n    return element;\\n}</pre>(Official model answer, L3.)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Write node class, linked list class, insertion at the front function then use them to create the linked list: head → 5.3 → 8.5 → NULL (insert value 8.5 then value 5.3).\",\"ans\":\"<pre>class Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    float data;\\n    Node *next;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Linkedlist\\n{\\npublic:\\n    Node *head;\\n    Linkedlist() { head = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return head == NULL; }\\n    void insertatbeginning(float item)      // float, not int\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        if (isEmpty()) { newnode-&gt;next = NULL; head = newnode; }\\n        else { newnode-&gt;next = head; head = newnode; }\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Linkedlist list;\\n    list.insertatbeginning(8.5);\\n    list.insertatbeginning(5.3);   // head -&gt; 5.3 -&gt; 8.5 -&gt; NULL\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>The official key uses <code>insertatbeginning(int item)</code> (which would store 8 and 5) and calls insertatend in main; both are fixed here (L5).\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Summer Exam 2022/2023\",\"meta\":\"Summer final term · 1 hour · 60 marks · CS201 · Dr. Ahmed Ibrahim, Dr. Lamiaa Hassaan · 4 questions (15 marks each)\",\"note\":\"Transcribed from the first page of \\\"2022-2023 data structure exam\\\" (phone photo, readable). Pages 2 to 5 of that file are a student's typed solutions (with Arabic comments); our answers are independent and were compiled with g++. In the table, Dina's Subject 2 is printed as \\\"-80\\\"; we read it as 80 (a printing mark).\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Questions\",\"marks\":\"60 marks (15 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"First Question: You have the matrix which represents the degrees of 6 students in 4 subjects (Ahmed 70, 97, 50, 75; Mohamed 60, 90, 80, 79; Halaa 50, 45.5, 60, 81; Dina 80, 80, 70, 60; Abas 40, 70, 75, 55; Hoda 75, 60.5, 85, 50). Using array of struct write a program to: calculate the average value of each subject; calculate the total degree of each student.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\n#include &lt;string&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nstruct Student {\\n    string name;\\n    float degree[4];\\n    float total;\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Student s[6];\\n    float avg[4] = {0, 0, 0, 0};\\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 6; i++)\\n    {\\n        cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Enter name and 4 degrees of student \\\" &lt;&lt; i + 1 &lt;&lt; \\\": \\\";\\n        cin &gt;&gt; s[i].name;\\n        s[i].total = 0;\\n        for (int j = 0; j &lt; 4; j++)\\n        {\\n            cin &gt;&gt; s[i].degree[j];\\n            s[i].total += s[i].degree[j];   // total of each student\\n            avg[j] += s[i].degree[j];       // sum of each subject\\n        }\\n    }\\n    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 4; j++)\\n        cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Average of subject \\\" &lt;&lt; j + 1 &lt;&lt; \\\" = \\\" &lt;&lt; avg[j] / 6 &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 6; i++)\\n        cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Total of \\\" &lt;&lt; s[i].name &lt;&lt; \\\" = \\\" &lt;&lt; s[i].total &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>Output with the table (run with g++): subject averages 62.5, 73.8333, 70, 66.6667; totals Ahmed 292, Mohamed 309, Halaa 236.5, Dina 290, Abas 240, Hoda 270.5 (L1).\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lqueue\",\"title\":\"Dequeue, then enqueue 50 (example starting queue)\",\"init\":[10,20,30],\"ops\":[\"dequeue\",\"enqueue 50\"]},\"q\":\"Second Question: You have the Linked-Queue, write a program to: remove an item from the queue; insert a new item whose value = 50.\",\"ans\":\"Use the Node and Queue classes of L7:<pre>void enqueue(int item)\\n{\\n    Node *newnode = new Node();\\n    newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n    newnode-&gt;next = NULL;\\n    if (front == NULL) { front = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n    else { rear-&gt;next = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n}\\nint dequeue()\\n{\\n    if (front == NULL) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Queue is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return 0; }\\n    Node *temp = front;\\n    int value = front-&gt;data;\\n    front = front-&gt;next;\\n    if (front == NULL) rear = NULL;\\n    delete temp;\\n    return value;\\n}\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Queue q;\\n    q.enqueue(10); q.enqueue(20);                // some existing items\\n    cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Removed \\\" &lt;&lt; q.dequeue() &lt;&lt; endl;  // removes 10 (front)\\n    q.enqueue(50);                               // 50 joins at the rear: 20 50\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lstack\",\"title\":\"Push 50 and 70, then pop\",\"ops\":[\"push 50\",\"push 70\",\"pop\"]},\"q\":\"Third Question: You have the Linked-Stack, write a program to: insert two new nodes with values = 50 and 70; remove a node from the stack.\",\"ans\":\"Linked stack class of L6; main: <code>s.push(50); s.push(70); cout &lt;&lt; s.pop();</code> prints 70 because the last pushed node is on top (top → 70 → 50 → NULL, after pop top → 50). Full code: see Final 2024/25 Q3.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Fourth Question: Write a program to insert a new node in its order of this linked list: head → 10 → 20 → 30 → NULL.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nclass Node\\n{\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node *next;\\n    Node() { data = 0; next = NULL; }\\n};\\nclass Linkedlist\\n{\\npublic:\\n    Node *head;\\n    Linkedlist() { head = NULL; }\\n    bool isEmpty() { return head == NULL; }\\n    void insertsorted(int item)\\n    {\\n        Node *newnode = new Node();\\n        newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n        if (isEmpty() || item &lt; head-&gt;data)\\n        { newnode-&gt;next = head; head = newnode; }\\n        else\\n        {\\n            Node *temp = head;\\n            while (temp-&gt;next != NULL &amp;&amp; temp-&gt;next-&gt;data &lt; item)\\n                temp = temp-&gt;next;\\n            newnode-&gt;next = temp-&gt;next;\\n            temp-&gt;next = newnode;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    void display()\\n    {\\n        for (Node *t = head; t != NULL; t = t-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; t-&gt;data &lt;&lt; \\\" \\\";\\n        cout &lt;&lt; endl;\\n    }\\n};\\nint main()\\n{\\n    Linkedlist list;\\n    list.insertsorted(10); list.insertsorted(20); list.insertsorted(30);\\n    list.insertsorted(25);\\n    list.display();          // 10 20 25 30\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>(L5; tested with g++.)\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Final Exam 2022/2023\",\"meta\":\"First term · 2 hours · 60 marks · CS201 (Credit 2022) · Dr. Ahmed Ibrahim, Dr. Lamia Hassaan · 2 questions\",\"note\":\"Transcribed from a two-page scan (clear). No answer key. Tree in Q2b read from the drawing: H is the right child of D, L is the left child of K, P is the left child of R, Z is the right child of X. For Q2a the lecture's quadratic formula h(k,i) = (h1(k) + i + i²) mod m is used; the textbook form (h1 + i²) is also shown. All traces were checked with g++/node.\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Question One · I. Choose the correct answer\",\"marks\":\"10 marks (1 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Pushing an element into stack already having five elements and stack size is 5, this condition is called ……\",\"o\":[\"Crash\",\"Overflow\",\"Underflow\",\"User flow\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Pushing onto a full stack is stack overflow (L2).\",\"ow\":[\"\\\"Crash\\\" is not a stack condition; pushing onto a full stack is called stack overflow.\",null,\"Underflow is popping from an empty stack (top == −1); here the stack is full.\",\"\\\"User flow\\\" is not a stack term; a push onto a full stack is overflow.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"In a linear queue, if implemented using an array of size MAX, gets full when?\",\"o\":[\"Front = (rear + 1) mod MAX\",\"Front = rear + 1\",\"Rear = MAX - 1\",\"Rear = front\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"The lecture's isFull returns true when tail == maxsize − 1: the rear is at the last cell and a linear queue cannot move past it, even if cells at the front are free (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"front == (rear + 1) mod MAX is the circular queue's full test, where rear wraps around; a linear queue never wraps.\",\"In the lecture's linear queue, head having passed tail (head &gt; tail) means empty, not full.\",null,\"rear == front means head and tail are on the same cell: exactly one element, not full.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is not a correct statement for a stack data structure?\",\"o\":[\"Stack follows FIFO\",\"Arrays can be used to implement the stack\",\"Elements are stored in a sequential manner\",\"Top of the stack has the last inserted element\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"\\\"Stack follows FIFO\\\" is the wrong statement: a stack inserts and deletes only at the top, so it is LIFO; FIFO is the queue rule (L2).\",\"ow\":[null,\"This is a correct statement: the static implementation of a stack uses an array.\",\"This is a correct statement: a stack is a linear ordered list, so elements are stored in sequence.\",\"This is a correct statement: all insertions are at the top, so the top holds the last inserted element (LIFO).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following data structure is non-linear type?\",\"o\":[\"Linked List\",\"Stack\",\"Tree\",\"Circular queue\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A tree stores its elements in a hierarchy (root, children, subtrees), not one sequence, so it is non-linear like a graph; linked lists, stacks and queues are linear (L1, L9).\",\"ow\":[\"A linked list is linear: each node points to the next, forming a single sequence.\",\"A stack is linear: its elements form one sequence, accessed at the top.\",null,\"A circular queue is still linear; only its indexing wraps around from the last cell to the first.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following determines the need for the circular queue?\",\"o\":[\"Avoid memory wastage\",\"Access queue using priority\",\"Follows the FIFO principle\",\"None\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The linear queue cannot reuse cells freed by dequeue (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Serving elements by priority is what a priority queue does; a circular queue is still FIFO.\",\"Both linear and circular queues follow FIFO, so FIFO cannot be the reason a circular queue is needed.\",\"There is a reason: the circular queue exists to reuse cells the linear queue wastes (option a).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"If a Binary Search Tree is traversed in the order right, root, left recursively, the output sequence will be in:\",\"o\":[\"ascending order\",\"descending order\",\"no specific order\",\"right, root, left cannot be done\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Reverse inorder visits larger keys first (L9).\",\"ow\":[\"Ascending order comes from inorder (left, root, right); visiting right first reverses it.\",null,\"The BST property (left smaller, right larger) fixes the order, so right, root, left always gives descending keys.\",\"It can be done: it is inorder with the two recursive calls swapped (reverse inorder).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"If circular queue is implemented using array having size MAX_SIZE, which of the following conditions used to specify that the circular queue is empty?\",\"o\":[\"front = rear = 0\",\"front = rear + 1\",\"front = rear = -1\",\"none\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"The lecture initializes and resets front and rear to -1 (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"front = rear = 0 means one element is stored at index 0, not an empty queue.\",\"front = rear + 1 is not the lecture's circular test; its isEmpty checks front == −1.\",null,\"One of the options is right: the lecture starts with and resets to front = rear = −1.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"When the pre-order and post-order traversal of a Binary Tree generates the same output, then the tree has:\",\"o\":[\"two nodes\",\"only one node\",\"three nodes\",\"any number of nodes\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The root is first in preorder and last in postorder; they coincide only with one node (L9).\",\"ow\":[\"With two nodes the root is first in preorder but last in postorder, so the outputs differ.\",null,\"With three nodes the root is first in preorder and last in postorder, so the outputs differ.\",\"Only a single node (or empty tree) gives the same output; with more nodes the root's position differs.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"To insert an item into a stack, identify the correct set of statements:\",\"o\":[\"top++; stack[top]=item;\",\"item = stack[top]; top--;\",\"stack[item] = top; item++;\",\"stack[top] = item; top++;\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Top starts at -1, so increment then store (L2).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Reading stack[top] then decrementing top is the pop operation, not insertion.\",\"This uses the item as an index and stores top in it, so the item is never placed in the stack.\",\"With top starting at −1, this writes to stack[−1] first; top must be incremented before storing.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What are the number of nodes of left and right subtree of the binary tree if the data is inserted in the following order: 45, 15, 8, 56, 5, 65, 47, 12, 18, 10, 73, 50, 16, 61\",\"o\":[\"8 nodes to the left and 5 nodes to the right\",\"6 nodes to the left and 7 nodes to the right\",\"7 nodes to the left and 6 nodes to the right\",\"5 nodes to the left and 8 nodes to the right\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Keys &lt; 45: 15, 8, 5, 12, 18, 10, 16 (7). Keys &gt; 45: 56, 65, 47, 73, 50, 61 (6). Verified with g++ (L9).\",\"ow\":[\"8 and 5 is a miscount: 7 keys are less than 45 and 6 are greater.\",\"6 and 7 swaps the two counts: 7 keys go left (less than 45) and 6 go right.\",null,\"5 and 8 is a miscount: all keys smaller than 45 go left (7), larger ones right (6).\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Question One · II. Select True or False\",\"marks\":\"10 marks (1 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"The condition if (front == 0 && rear == MAX_SIZE - 1) is enough to check the fullness of a circular queue.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"It is also full when rear is just one behind front (wrapped case) (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"That condition covers only Case 1 (front 0, rear at the end); it misses Case 2, where rear has wrapped and is just one behind front.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Hash tables store keys in ordered locations.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Keys are scattered by the hash function (scatter table) (L10).\",\"ow\":[\"The hash function scatters keys over the table (it is also called a scatter table), so cells are not in key order.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"The value of REAR is increased by 1 when an element is inserted in the queue.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Every insertion is made at the rear: enqueue increments it (tail = tail + 1, or (rear + 1) % MAX_SIZE in a circular queue) and stores the item there (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: enqueue does tail = tail + 1 (circularly in a circular queue) before storing the item.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"The following is a valid code of class Node of a singly linked list: class Node {int data, Node next;}\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"next must be a pointer (Node *next;), members need ; separators, and the class needs a closing ; (and public:) (L5).\",\"ow\":[\"The code is not valid: next must be a pointer (Node *next;), members are separated by ; not a comma, and the class needs a closing ;.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"When a hash function computes the same location for two or more keys, this is a collision situation.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The lecture defines a collision as the situation where the hash function maps two different keys k and j to the same cell, e.g. 20 and 11 both give 2 mod 9 (L10, Collision).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: the lecture defines collision as the hash function mapping two different keys to the same cell.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"To print the keys of a binary search tree in an ascending order, you may apply inorder or postorder traversals.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Inorder traversal of a BST always gives the sorted (ascending) order of the keys; postorder visits the parent last, after both subtrees, so its output is not sorted (L9).\",\"ow\":[\"Only inorder (left, parent, right) gives ascending order in a BST; postorder visits each parent after both subtrees, so it is not sorted.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Two linked lists are needed in order to implement a linked stack.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A linked stack is one linked list: every new node is inserted as the top (newnode->next = top; top = newnode) and pop removes the node top points to, so one list and one pointer suffice (L6).\",\"ow\":[\"A linked stack is one singly linked list with a single pointer, top; push and pop both work at the top.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"On inserting a new node N in a doubly linked list, N->next = Null is a must.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"N->next is NULL only when N becomes the last node (insert at the end or into an empty list). Inserting at the beginning sets N->next = head, and in the middle it points to the following node (L8).\",\"ow\":[\"N->next = NULL is needed only when N becomes the last node (insert at the end or into an empty list); at the beginning N->next = head.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Left-Right-Parent represents the Postorder Traversal of a Binary Tree.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Traversals are named by when the parent is visited: postorder traverses the left subtree, then the right subtree, and visits the parent last (L9, Postorder traversal).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is true: postorder traverses the left subtree, then the right subtree, and visits the parent last.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Insertion is done at the front of a linked queue.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Insertion is at the rear; deletion at the front (L7).\",\"ow\":[\"Enqueue links the new node after rear (rear->next = newnode; rear = newnode); the front is where dequeue deletes.\",null]}]},{\"title\":\"Question Two · Answer ONLY FIVE of the following\",\"marks\":\"40 marks (8 each)\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"a- Store the values 14, 17, 25, 37, 34, 16 using division method. Apply linear probe and quadratic probe in collision situations. Array size = 11.\",\"ans\":\"Home addresses k mod 11: 14→3, 17→6, 25→3, 37→4, 34→1, 16→5.<br><b>Linear probing</b> (h1 + i) mod 11: 14→3; 17→6; 25: 3 taken → 4; 37: 4 taken → 5; 34→1; 16: 5 taken, 6 taken → 7.<br><table><tr><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th><th>9</th><th>10</th></tr><tr><td>–</td><td>34</td><td>–</td><td>14</td><td>25</td><td>37</td><td>17</td><td>16</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr></table><b>Quadratic probing</b> (lecture formula (h1 + i + i²) mod 11): 14→3; 17→6; 25: i=0 → 3 taken, i=1 → 3+1+1 = 5; 37: i=0 → 4 (free); 34→1; 16: i=0 → 5 taken (25), i=1 → 5+2 = 7.<br><table><tr><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th><th>9</th><th>10</th></tr><tr><td>–</td><td>34</td><td>–</td><td>14</td><td>37</td><td>25</td><td>17</td><td>16</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr></table>If the textbook form (h1 + i²) is used instead: 25 → 4, 37 → 4 taken → 5, 16 → 5 taken, 6 taken, 5+4 = 9 → slot 9 (table: 1:34, 3:14, 4:25, 5:37, 6:17, 9:16). State the formula you use (L10).\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"bst\",\"title\":\"The traversals of this tree\",\"tree\":\"A(B(D(,H),K(L,)),M(R(P,),X(,Z)))\",\"ops\":[\"inorder\",\"postorder\",\"preorder\"]},\"q\":\"b- Consider the tree: A root; B (left of A) with children D and K; D has right child H; K has left child L; M (right of A) with children R and X; R has left child P; X has right child Z. (i) Write the output of inorder and postorder traversals. (ii) Write C++ function of preorder traverse.\",\"ans\":\"(i) <b>Inorder</b>: D H B L K A P R M X Z<br><b>Postorder</b>: H D L K B P R Z X M A<br>(Preorder for reference: A B D H K L M R P X Z; verified with g++.)<br>(ii)<pre>void preorder(Node *temp)\\n{\\n    if (temp == NULL) { return; }\\n    cout &lt;&lt; temp-&gt;data &lt;&lt; \\\"\\\\t\\\";   // visit parent\\n    preorder(temp-&gt;left);           // then left subtree\\n    preorder(temp-&gt;right);          // then right subtree\\n}</pre>(L9)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"cqueue\",\"title\":\"The circular queue trace\",\"size\":5,\"names\":[\"head\",\"tail\"],\"ops\":[\"enqueue 10\",\"enqueue 20\",\"enqueue 30\",\"dequeue\",\"dequeue\"]},\"q\":\"c- Consider a circular queue of size = 5, show the output of: enqueue(10); enqueue(20); cout&lt;&lt;\\\"Head = \\\"&lt;&lt;head&lt;&lt;\\\"Tail = \\\"&lt;&lt;tail&lt;&lt;\\\"\\\\n\\\"; enqueue(30); cout&lt;&lt;dequeue()&lt;&lt;\\\"\\\\n\\\"; int x = dequeue(); cout&lt;&lt;\\\"Head = \\\"&lt;&lt;head&lt;&lt;\\\"Tail = \\\"&lt;&lt;tail&lt;&lt;\\\"\\\\n\\\";\",\"ans\":\"Trace: enqueue(10) → head = 0, tail = 0. enqueue(20) → tail = 1. Print <b>Head = 0Tail = 1</b>. enqueue(30) → tail = 2. dequeue() returns 10, head = 1 → prints <b>10</b>. x = dequeue() = 20, head = 2. Print <b>Head = 2Tail = 2</b>.<br>Output:<pre>Head = 0Tail = 1\\n10\\nHead = 2Tail = 2</pre>If the lecture's enQueue/deQueue (which print their own messages) are used, the full output is (run with g++):<pre>Enqueued element: 10\\nEnqueued element: 20\\nHead = 0Tail = 1\\nEnqueued element: 30\\nDequeued element: 10\\n10\\nDequeued element: 20\\nHead = 2Tail = 2</pre>(L3)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lqueue\",\"title\":\"Dequeue, then enqueue 50 (example starting queue)\",\"init\":[10,20],\"ops\":[\"dequeue\",\"enqueue 50\"]},\"q\":\"d- You have a Linked Queue, write C++ functions to: remove an item from the queue; insert a new item which value = 50.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>void enqueue(int item)              // call enqueue(50)\\n{\\n    Node *newnode = new Node();\\n    newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n    newnode-&gt;next = NULL;\\n    if (front == NULL &amp;&amp; rear == NULL) { front = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n    else { rear-&gt;next = newnode; rear = newnode; }\\n}\\nint dequeue()\\n{\\n    if (front == NULL) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Queue is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return 0; }\\n    Node *temp = front;\\n    int value = front-&gt;data;\\n    front = front-&gt;next;\\n    if (front == NULL) rear = NULL;\\n    delete temp;\\n    return value;\\n}</pre>(L7)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"e- You have a Doubly Linked List, write C++ function to insert a new node in its order.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>void insertsorted(int item)\\n{\\n    Node *newnode = new Node();     // Node has data, next, prev\\n    newnode-&gt;data = item;\\n    if (head == NULL)\\n        head = newnode;\\n    else if (item &lt; head-&gt;data)\\n    {\\n        newnode-&gt;next = head;\\n        head-&gt;prev = newnode;\\n        head = newnode;\\n    }\\n    else\\n    {\\n        Node *temp = head;\\n        while (temp-&gt;next != NULL &amp;&amp; temp-&gt;next-&gt;data &lt; item)\\n            temp = temp-&gt;next;\\n        newnode-&gt;next = temp-&gt;next;\\n        newnode-&gt;prev = temp;\\n        if (temp-&gt;next != NULL) temp-&gt;next-&gt;prev = newnode;\\n        temp-&gt;next = newnode;\\n    }\\n}</pre>(L8; tested with g++.)\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"viz\":{\"type\":\"lstack\",\"title\":\"Remove all the items (example values)\",\"init\":[30,20,10],\"ops\":[\"pop\",\"pop\",\"pop\"]},\"q\":\"f- You have a Linked Stack, write C++ program to remove all the items from the stack.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>void removeAll()            // inside class Stack\\n{\\n    if (top == NULL) { cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Stack is empty\\\" &lt;&lt; endl; return; }\\n    while (top != NULL)\\n    {\\n        Node *temp = top;\\n        cout &lt;&lt; \\\"Popped \\\" &lt;&lt; temp-&gt;data &lt;&lt; endl;\\n        top = top-&gt;next;\\n        delete temp;\\n    }\\n}</pre>Full class with push and main: see Summer 2023/24 (60 marks) Q3 (L6).\"}]}]}]}");
